{"parentUuid":"25823fa9-1466-44cc-ba4f-cf2e72ce0b94","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffT5kBT5cT5896fVGw66","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AP6U5NJRiqFCkxQCkU3r67","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 1,80p docs/QUEUE.md","description":"Read top of Flint queue"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1222,"cache_read_input_tokens":93909,"output_tokens":276,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1222,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":276,"cache_read_input_tokens":93909,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1222,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1222},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01AP6U5NJRiqFCkxQCkU3r67":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && sed -n 1,80p docs/QUEUE.md","description":"Read top of Flint queue"}},"wireIngestContext":{"toolu_01AP6U5NJRiqFCkxQCkU3r67":{"cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffT5jtaufWJVwqr9m1Dc","type":"assistant","uuid":"4c6e6ef8-19eb-4575-a719-dc29e205616d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:20:03.295Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"0949803b-b5a4-4eec-8bc8-87b3ccc4c359","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main","sessionKind":"bg"}
{"parentUuid":"4c6e6ef8-19eb-4575-a719-dc29e205616d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"f097ba1e-802d-4788-86d0-92157481e0f4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AP6U5NJRiqFCkxQCkU3r67","type":"tool_result","content":"# Flint ingest queue\n\nThe order in which sources go into the vault library. Each course or book is atomized\nby Sonnet into `staging/library/` and reviewed by Codex against its source, following\nthe ingest review gate. Only then is it copied into SecondBrain, indexed and committed.\n\n## Promoted to the vault\n\n- **Harries, The Fundamentals of On-Field Rehabilitation**: 33 entries, domain `rehab-and-return-to-play`. SecondBrain `c15460d` (2026-09-26), after two review rounds by an independent Opus reviewer (Codex was capped) and nine reciprocal links. Harries batch 2 is pending: 5 items the first pass missed (the case-review template, fitting rehab into the team week, the HSR format progression, the mid-rehab hamstring checkpoint, and speed prescription in late rehab).\n- **2026-09-27, SecondBrain `aacbccd`:** Harries batch 2 (+4, now 37); Old Bull course (37, new domain `performance-technology-and-leadership`); Issurin *Block Periodization* Ch1-3 (28); Bondarchuk *Transfer of Training* I Ch1-2 (20); *Supertraining* Ch1-3 (113); Landon Evans *Elite Sports Performance* lectures (34). Every source went through independent Opus falsification reviews, with a round 2 where fixes were heavy. Resume points: *Supertraining* Ch4, Issurin Ch4, Bondarchuk I Ch3.\n\n## In progress\n\n1. (promoted, see above) **Harries**.\n   - Domain: new `rehab-and-return-to-play`. Book slug: `on-field-rtp--harries`.\n   - Stance: Eric's 2026-09-25 review. Keep it as a dosage reference; the theory is Taberner's control-chaos continuum with a velocity layer.\n2. **Old Bull Performance course**.\n   - Domain: new `performance-technology-and-leadership`. Book slug: `old-bull-performance-course--old-bull`.\n\n## Staged, not yet atomized: 42 Analytics (2026-10-01)\n\n128 SSAC talk sources staged at `staging/sources/42analytics/` (local). The 23 `supported` talks are atomized (152 entries, `staging/library/sports-analytics-and-decision-making/ssac-talks--42analytics/`), sample-reviewed and fixed, **awaiting a second review round before promotion**. See `docs/intake-42analytics.md`.\n\n## Next: Landon Evans (queued 2026-09-26 by Eric)\n\n681 unique files. Most of it is a curated library Landon gave Eric (`From Landon Evans/Eric Files/`), not Landon's own writing. Landon's own teaching goes first, because it is his voice that gets compared with Eric's method.\n\n| Step | What | Files | Route |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L1 | His *Elite Sports Performance* course: Units 1 to 4 lecture slides, plus the fuller Evans Unit 3 set (lectures 1 to 8, with an exam study guide) | about 30 PDFs | Atomize as a course (`elite-sports-performance--evans`). The slides are text but sparse (22 to 45 words a page), so slide-only entries are capped at `untested` |\n| L2 | His talks: CVASPS \"Physical Preparation in the NCAA: A Complementary Approach\" (1 GB video, plus a CVASPS mp3), and the 2014 Boston \"Performance Analytics\" slides | 3 | Transcribe the CVASPS talk first in the transcription queue. Where the talk matches an L1 entry, raise that entry to `supported` |\n| L3 | Therapy folder: fills the rehab gap next to Harries | 46 papers, 17 videos, 3 audio | Papers go to Zotero; video and audio go to the transcription queue |\n| L4 | Training books (Verkhoshansky, Bondarchuk, Issurin, Zatsiorsky, Siff, Francis, and others) | 188 flagged as books | `ingest-book`, one at a time behind the gate. **First: *Supertraining*, started 2026-09-26 at Eric's call.** Source is the 6th Expanded single PDF from TB (591 pages, text, md5-verified), slug `supertraining--verkhoshansky`, domain strength-and-conditioning. Chapter map plus Chapter 1 are in progress; later chapters run one batch each. **Then Bondarchuk (queued by Eric 2026-09-26)**, in the order below |\n| L5 | Papers in Physical Preparation, Science and Training Science | about 350 | Bulk import into Zotero, deduplicated against the library |\n| L6 | Nutrition | 242 | Later. It is outside the current synthesis order |\n\n### Bondarchuk, after *Supertraining*\n\nAll paths are under `Athletic Performance & Sports Science/Shared Reference Library/`. Text was checked by sampling 13 pages of each file.\n\n| Order | Book | Source copy | Pages | Why this order |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| B1 | *Transfer of Training in Sports* (Vol I) | `From Landon Evans/Training Books/Transfer of Trainng in Sports - Bondarchuk.pdf` (text). Mike Golden's 238-page copy is **scanned**, so it is not used | 236 | Exercise classification (general, special preparatory, specific developmental, competition) and transfer research follow on from Supertraining's dynamic correspondence, which the HHPR course team teaches |\n| B2 | *Transfer of Training in Sports II* | `From Landon Evans/Eric Files/Physical Preparation/Books/...` (text, 176 pages). Golden's 152-page text copy is a different layout, kept as a cross-check | 176 | Continues B1 |\n| B3 | *Track and Field Training* | `From Landon Evans/Eric Files/Physical Preparation/Books/Track and Field Training - Anatoly Bondarchuk.pdf` (text). Also on Drive: a 4 MB version and Golden's \"Original Manuscript\" | 171 | Planning and periodization |\n| B4 | *Long Term Training for Throwers* | Landon copy (text) | 34 | Thrower-specific; short |\n| B5 | *Constructing a Training System, Part 1* plus 12 short CCCE articles (periodization, strength mesocycles, transition period) | Landon and Golden libraries | small | Bundle as one source |\n| n/a | *Simplifying Bondarchuk* | Landon library | n/a | Secondary summary. Cross-check only, not ingested |\n\n### Zatsiorsky, after Bondarchuk (queued by Eric 2026-09-26)\n\n| Order | Book | Source copy | Pages | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Z1 | *Science and Practice of Strength Training*, 1st ed. (1995, Zatsiorsky) | `AP & Sports Science/Strength and Conditioning Resources/From Coach Lowe/Science and Practice of Strength Training.pdf` (same file as Golden's \"1st Edition\") | 118 PDF pages = about 236 printed | Complete: two book pages per landscape PDF page, running through the glossary (pp. 228 to 229). **The text layer is noisy OCR** (for example \"Si7ience\"), so every number must be checked against the page image, and the Codex review targets OCR misreads |\n| Z1b | *Science and Practice of Strength Training*, 2nd ed. (2006, with Kraemer) | TB snapshot `SC Books/S&C Books/...Zatsiorsky.pdf` | 247 | **Image-only scan, no text.** Cross-check only, unless OCR tooling is added later. Where it revises the 1st edition, note it in the entry |\n| Z2 | *Biomechanics in Sport: Performance Enhancement and Injury Prevention* (IOC, ed. Zatsiorsky, 2000) | Landon library copy (clean text) | 682 | Edited volume: ingest selectively (strength and power, sprinting, injury mechanisms), not cover to cover |\n| Z3 | Short pieces: \"Russian and Eastern European Approach\", \"Main Principles of Periodization\" | TB and Golden | small | Bundle as one source |\n\n### Issurin, after Zatsiorsky (queued by Eric 2026-09-26)\n\n| Order | Book or source | Source copy | Pages | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| I1 | *Block Periodization: Breakthrough in Sport Training* (Ultimate Athlete Concepts, 2008) | Landon library, `Eric Files/Physical Preparation/Books/Block Periodization - Issurin.pdf` (clean text) | 221 | Targets the strength-and-conditioning miss-log gap from 2026-09-03 (\"peaking phase structure for collegiate football\"). Converges with Bompa's periodization entries and Bondarchuk's |\n| I2 | *Principles and Basics of Advanced Athletic Training* (Ultimate Athlete Concepts, 2008) | Landon library, same folder (clean text) | 242 | Training theory: adaptation, residual training effects, training loads |\n| I3 | Five peer-reviewed papers: \"Benefits and Limitations of Block Periodized Training Approaches\", \"New Horizons for the Methodology and Physiology of Training Periodization\", \"Training Transfer: Scientific Background and Insights\", \"Periodization Training from Ancient Precursors to Structured Block Models\", and the vibration strength study | TB snapshot, `SC Books/S&C Articles/Issurin, Vladimir/` | papers | Into **Zotero**, not `ingest-book`. The papers are where Issurin's claims meet peer review, so a book entry that a paper qualifies gets marked `contested` or linked to it |\n| I4 | Two Issurin presentations (Golden library) and the block periodization deck for rowers | Golden and Landon libraries | slides | Sparse slides (about 29 words a page). Cross-check only |\n\n### Charlie Francis, after Issurin (queued by Eric 2026-09-26)\n\n| Order | Source | Copy | Size | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| F1 | *Key Concepts, 2008 Elite Edition* (CharlieFrancis.com) | TB snapshot `Charlie Francis/Charlie Francis - Key Concepts Elite Edition.pdf` (text, about 400 words a page) | 270 pages | The best text source. Topics: speed, special endurance, tempo, high-low organization, CNS load |\n| F2 | Lecture videos, 13 files: *Team Sport Training Concepts*, *Peaking When It Counts: The 10-Day Taper*, *Vancouver Lecture 2004*, *Weights for Speed 1 to 3*, *Section 1: Adaptation and Training Element Selection*, *Practical Sessions 1 and 2*, *GPP Essentials*, *Passive and Active Recovery*, *Medicine Ball*, *Bike Workout* | `AP & Sports Science/Strength and Conditioning Resources/Charlie Francis/` | about 2.7 GB | Transcribe locally (`scripts/transcribe.py`), then atomize as a course. Team Sport Training Concepts and the 10-Day Taper go first: football, and the library's peaking gap |\n| F3 | *CFTS: The Charlie Francis Training System* | Landon copy and `FrancisTrainingSystem.pdf` (218 pages each) | 218 pages | **Both copies are image-only scans.** Blocked until OCR is available (proposal: `rapidocr` through `uv`, CPU, no admin). Until then F1 and F2 cover most of its content |\n| F4 | *Speed Trap* | Landon copy (text) | 321 pages | Memoir with training content. Ingest selectively (the training chapters) |\n| F5 | Bundle: *Forum Review 2002* (text, 131 pages) and 2009, Micro Reviews 1 and 2, *The Classics*, *GPP*, the Vancouver 2004 notes, the USA presentation, \"On Sprinting\", and the rest-interval discussion | TB snapshot and Landon | small to medium | One source. Forum Q&A carries his applied answers |\n| F6 | *Training for Power and Strength in Speed* | TB snapshot | 32 pages | **Scan.** Waits for OCR along with F3 |\n\n\n## After Landon","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"b4e4fe8c-6f29-4546-85d2-005a57224ee0","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:20:03.486Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Flint ingest queue\n\nThe order in which sources go into the vault library. Each course or book is atomized\nby Sonnet into `staging/library/` and reviewed by Codex against its source, following\nthe ingest review gate. Only then is it copied into SecondBrain, indexed and committed.\n\n## Promoted to the vault\n\n- **Harries, The Fundamentals of On-Field Rehabilitation**: 33 entries, domain `rehab-and-return-to-play`. SecondBrain `c15460d` (2026-09-26), after two review rounds by an independent Opus reviewer (Codex was capped) and nine reciprocal links. Harries batch 2 is pending: 5 items the first pass missed (the case-review template, fitting rehab into the team week, the HSR format progression, the mid-rehab hamstring checkpoint, and speed prescription in late rehab).\n- **2026-09-27, SecondBrain `aacbccd`:** Harries batch 2 (+4, now 37); Old Bull course (37, new domain `performance-technology-and-leadership`); Issurin *Block Periodization* Ch1-3 (28); Bondarchuk *Transfer of Training* I Ch1-2 (20); *Supertraining* Ch1-3 (113); Landon Evans *Elite Sports Performance* lectures (34). Every source went through independent Opus falsification reviews, with a round 2 where fixes were heavy. Resume points: *Supertraining* Ch4, Issurin Ch4