{"parentUuid":"077e7f8f-12b3-4093-baf8-5100519b55d2","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffT89M3TBWYpVrCVnt5R","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YBji9cX1wWWat8iU2quzzC","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md staging/review_book_prompt.md; sed -n 1,40p staging/fixes_supertraining_ch4-2b_applied.md","description":"Read atomize brief, review prompt, last fix log"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1560,"cache_read_input_tokens":106576,"output_tokens":168,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1560,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":168,"cache_read_input_tokens":106576,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1560,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1560},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01YBji9cX1wWWat8iU2quzzC":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && cat docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md staging/review_book_prompt.md; sed -n 1,40p staging/fixes_supertraining_ch4-2b_applied.md","description":"Read atomize brief, review prompt, last fix log"}},"wireIngestContext":{"toolu_01YBji9cX1wWWat8iU2quzzC":{"cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffT891CtBjiEiJMnMCke","type":"assistant","uuid":"9ad9d1d9-c9da-4af6-bce3-6fbbfe3ee476","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:20:35.925Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"0949803b-b5a4-4eec-8bc8-87b3ccc4c359","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main","sessionKind":"bg"}
{"parentUuid":"9ad9d1d9-c9da-4af6-bce3-6fbbfe3ee476","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"f097ba1e-802d-4788-86d0-92157481e0f4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YBji9cX1wWWat8iU2quzzC","type":"tool_result","content":"# Atomization brief (shared by every Flint ingest agent)\n\nRead this whole file before writing anything. It holds the rules the Harries review taught (2026-09-26: 25 of 35 files needed fixes). The review gate will check every rule here.\n\n## Schema\n\nFollow `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\skills\\ingest-book\\SKILL.md` exactly: `_domain.md`, `_book.md` (map and resume checkpoint, updated after EACH chapter or unit), and entries named `<kind>--<kebab-slug>.md` with kind one of principle, framework, case-study, heuristic, claim. Use the frontmatter keys exactly as the skill gives them. The body has **What it is** / **Why it works** / **When to apply** / **When NOT to apply** (mandatory, non-trivial) / **Example** / **Related**.\n\nStyle reference, read before writing: `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library\\strength-and-conditioning\\fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer\\case-study--isometric-sprinter-and-hurdler.md`, plus one principle file in the same folder.\n\n## Voices: label every one\n\n- **The source author's statements are plain text.**\n- **Eric's views** (from `docs/method-baseline.md` or his reviews) are prefixed \"Eric's baseline:\" or \"Eric's review:\".\n- **Your own reasoning, application or synthesis** is prefixed \"Inference:\".\n- **Nothing unlabeled that the author did not say.** The most common failures: a judgment from Eric's review written as if the author said it; a mechanism the author never gave presented as \"why it works\" (write \"The author gives no mechanism. Inference: ...\"); an invented example.\n\n## Lessons from the Supertraining Chapter 1 review (2026-09-26: 30 FIX, 1 NO-GO of 41)\n\n- **Keep the source's internal contradictions.** When a book says X on one page and not-X on another, record both with their pages and set `contested`. Never pick one side silently. The NO-GO entry reversed the book's SJ-CMJ strength-deficit rule because the book states it both ways.\n- **Name the right voice.** In co-authored books, write \"the authors\" unless a passage clearly belongs to one author. A passage that names Verkhoshansky in the third person is Siff writing.\n- **Keep `proven` rare and scoped.** Scope it to the replicated core (for example, \"Hill's force-velocity curve\"), never the whole entry, and mark any disputed sentence inside it. Four of eight `proven` ratings were lowered.\n- **No mechanism the source does not give.** About a dozen \"Why it works\" sections supplied physiology the book never states. Label it \"Inference:\" or leave it out.\n- **Do not add \"well established\" or \"modern research shows\" without a citation.** Name the paper, or delete the phrase.\n\n## Lessons from the 2026-09-27 review wave (Supertraining Ch2-3, Issurin Ch3, Bondarchuk, Evans, Old Bull)\n\n- **Invented Examples are the most common failure.** 34 of 36 Supertraining Ch2-3a Examples were unlabelled inventions. Use the source's own example where it gives one; otherwise start the Example with \"Inference:\".\n- **\"Verified against the page image\" means you opened the image.** Four Issurin Ch3 entries claimed it for figures they had misread (Fig 3.7 inverted, a Table 3.7 row swapped). Sparse slides are not \"bare diagrams\" until you have looked.\n- **Name the population and method the source gives.** Bondarchuk's data are 7,000+ native and foreign athletes, questionnaires, r >= 0.349, steroid-free; entries that said \"Soviet athletes\" were wrong everywhere.\n- **Check what a cited paper actually says.** Issurin 2016's critique targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's A-T-R; Herzog & Leonard 2000 shows force enhancement, against the book's sentence. When the book misreports a study, record both.\n- **No review history in entries.** \"Prior version\", \"corrected from a NO-GO finding\" and similar belong in `staging/fixes_*_applied.md`, never in the entry body.\n- **Fix logs must match the files.** One fixer log claimed fixes that were not made. Fixers run their own grep checks and paste the output.\n- **Section headers use the vault form** `**What it is** —` (with the em dash). Do not add suffixes such as \": mandatory\". Em dashes are banned only in titles and body text. Never wrap a `[[wikilink]]` across lines.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Every number, definition and quote carries a checkable locator: PDF page (and printed page if it differs), or file plus timestamp. Never write \"confirmed in PDF\" unless you opened that page.\n- Never invent a number. Keep ambiguity visible: mark unclear audio or OCR as unclear, and do not harmonize two conflicting numbers from the source; record both with their locators.\n- OCR sources (`staging/ocr/*.md`): pages listed as low_confidence in the header must be checked against the page image before any number from them is cited. Use the Read tool on the PDF page, or render it.\n- **OCR confidence scores miss the worst errors** (checked 2026-09-27). Zatsiorsky 2e: the OCR interleaves the two columns and the sidebars line by line, so its text is not readable prose; use it only to find content, and read and quote from the page image. *CFTS*: single-column prose that mostly reads in order, but upside-down lines come out as gibberish, some lines are dropped, and figures are scrambled, all at \"0 low-confidence pages\". For any OCR source, quote only after checking the page image, and read every figure and table from the image.\n\n## Confidence\n\n- Practitioner courses and lectures: ceiling `supported`.\n- Books: `proven` only for established, widely replicated physiology or mechanics. Soviet-era or single-lab empirical findings and training prescriptions: `supported` at most. `untested` for single cases and anecdotes. `contested` where another library source or modern evidence disagrees (say which).\n\n## Dedup and converge\n\nBefore each entry, grep `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library` and the staging library (`C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\staging\\library`) for the concept. If it is covered, add a new entry only for a genuinely new angle, and link the existing one in Related. List reciprocal-link suggestions in your final report. Never edit files in the vault or another agent's staging folder.\n\n## Baylor context\n\nRead `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\method-baseline.md` (approved). Use it in When to apply, labelled \"Eric's baseline:\". Department names: Athletic Performance, Athletic Medicine, Applied Performance. Plain style, no em dashes. Use commas, colons and parentheses.\n\n## Scope and output\n\n- Write ONLY into your assigned staging folder. No git, no vault edits.\n- Stop at a chapter or unit boundary. It is better to finish fewer chapters well; the `_book.md` checkpoint lets the next batch resume.\n- Final reply: entries by kind and by confidence; chapters or units done and pending with page ranges; reciprocal-link suggestions; unclear or contradictory spots in the source; anything you could not read.\nYou are an independent reviewer in Flint's ingest review gate. Another model wrote these entries; your job is to FALSIFY them, not confirm. Read-only on entries and sources. Write only your report file.\n\nStandards: `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md` (read it first). Every rule there is a check.\n\nFor EVERY entry in scope:\n1. Every number, definition, table value and quote appears at the cited PDF page (and printed page) and means the same thing. Open the pages yourself; do not trust the entry.\n2. Attribution: plain text is the book author's own statement. Anything else must be labelled \"Eric's baseline:\", \"Eric's review:\" or \"Inference:\". Flag any unlabelled claim the author did not make, any \"why it works\" mechanism the author did not give, and any invented example.\n3. Confidence: `proven` only for established, widely replicated physiology or mechanics, so challenge every `proven`. Soviet-era or single-lab findings and prescriptions: `supported` at most. `untested` for anecdotes. `contested` needs a stated reason.\n4. \"When NOT to apply\" is present and substantive.\n5. Duplicates: flag any entry that restates an existing vault entry (grep `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library`) without a new angle.\n6. Missing: up to 5 important ideas in the covered pages that no entry captures.\n\nOutput (Markdown): a table with one row per entry: file | verdict (GO / FIX / NO-GO) | problem, quoting the entry and the source with its page | exact correction. For GO rows, list what you checked (\"checked: 2.5x at PDF 23 / p.12\"). Then the missing list. Then an overall verdict: PROMOTE, GO AFTER FIXES (list them), or NO-GO.\n# Fixes applied: Supertraining 4.2.4-4.2.9 (Ch4-2b), 20 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-09-27. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch4-2b_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, text of PDF idx 32-35 and 233-253 extracted with PyMuPDF; page images rendered and opened for PDF 236 (Figs 4.11, 4.12), PDF 238 (Figs 4.13, 4.14), PDF 244 (the 4.2.6.2 heading position) and PDF 245 (Fig 4.16). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 20 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-2b.txt`. `_book.md` not touched (the review asks for no change there). No new entries written.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| claim--hettinger-muller-isometric-strength-gain-rate.md | Locator to PDF pp. 234-235 / printed 223-224. When NOT: \"corroborated only by a 1970 review\" replaced by the book's \"rather contradictory data\" (Verkhoshansky 1970 review, p.224). Added the duration-vs-intensity tension with both sides: H&M one-third threshold and the authors' 80-95% (PDF 33 / p.22), Korobkov et al \"approximately equivalent increases\" at 20-80% 1RM early in training (PDF 34 / p.23, partly agreeing with p.224), the book's 70-80% explosive protocol (PDF 237 / p.226), and Oranchuk 2019 as relayed by Natera in the Moyer entry. Added the 6 / 10 / 8 s hold-length cross-reference (PDF 237 / p.226). \"Why it works\" reworded so the no-mechanism statement is the authors' own, not an unlabelled writer claim. Quoted the \"fundamental conditioning factor\" sentence exactly. Linked [[claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus]] and [[framework--isometric-training-variables-and-adaptations]] in body and Related. |\n| claim--isometric-endurance-limits-and-recovery-phases.md | Locator to PDF pp. 238-239 / printed 227-228. Added Clarke et al (1958) as the source of the temperature optimum, with Petrofsky & Phillips (1986) placing it near 27-28 C. Fig 4.13 description tightened after re-opening the image (about 0 kPa at 0% to about 20 kPa at 100%). \"Why it works\" now uses the book's wording (\"unable to meet the physiological demands\") instead of a paraphrased pressure-exceeds-perfusion mechanism. When to apply: recovery timeline scoped, as Inference, to recovery of endurance after a fatiguing sustained contraction. Example: plank, wall-sit and mid-thigh pull removed; kept as Inference with \"whether a given hold is under 10-15% of maximum is not shown in the source\". Added a note that the text's \"(Fig 4.14)\" pointer for temperature is a source oddity (Fig 4.14 plots endurance vs %MVC). |\n| claim--isometric-regional-specificity-not-strictly-angular.md | No change needed. Review verdict GO; I re-checked the 15-degree band, Thepault-Mathieu numbers (54/25, 30/25, 25/7) and the \"should not be interpreted\" caution on PDF 237-238 / p.226-227 and found no error. Reciprocal-link suggestion below. |\n| framework--isometric-contraction-categorization-voluntary-reflexive.md | Fig 4.12 sentence rewritten from the image: curves are labelled \"Slow Isometrics\" and \"Explosive Isometrics\"; the caption says reflexive explosive action is steeper still, with the caption quoted. When NOT: \"detailed experimental corroboration\" rescoped to the absolute-strength vs explosive-strength split (p.225). Added oscillatory isometrics with Soechting & Roberts 1975, 5 Hz, and Basmajian 1978 (PDF 235 / p.224), plus \"negative evaluations of this method are premature\" (PDF 236 / p.225). Recorded a source mismatch the review did not raise: the only evidence is for voluntarily produced 5 Hz jerks, while Fig 4.11 files oscillatory under reflexive (Inference line in When NOT). When to apply: \"reflexive (explosive or oscillatory)\" changed to reflexive explosive, per the text; \"clean-and-jerk catch\" changed to the book's \"clean-and-jerk\". Locators now carry PDF and printed pages. |\n| heuristic--isometric-priming-and-external-internal-classification.md | \"Why it works\": reaction-time mechanism replaced with \"The authors give no mechanism for the 7% reaction-time figure\", plus a labelled Inference linking to the p.224 inhibition argument and noting it concerns force. When to apply: reframed as a progressive-overload step (p.229); \"before attempting a true max attempt\" removed. Example: invented 110-120% replaced with the book's own 120-150% shoulder support and quasi-isometric first degrees of a squat. Locator to PDF p. 240 / printed p. 229. Quoted the priming and \"advocated by their scientists\" wording. Rack/spotter safety sentence labelled Inference. |\n| heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks.md | Recorded the unreconciled 6 s / 10 s / 8 s hold limits on PDF 237 / p.226 with the exact wording. Methods reworded to \"methods of execution ... recommended for developing absolute strength\". Cardiovascular sentence split: the source's \"may have a harmful effect ... in some individuals\", then \"Inference:\" for the risk-factor and pairing advice. |\n| heuristic--loadless-training-broomstick-protocol.md | Kovalik attribution fixed: only the 5 sets are Kovalik's; the 3-10 s rest is \"research indicates\"; breathing, 10-15 s movement and 6-8 s hold are given without a source. When to apply points to \"the protocol given on PDF p. 241, printed p. 230\". Extensor-flexor reasoning labelled Inference. Added the book's stated corroboration (dynamic stretching against resistance, no citation) and the pushups/free-standing squats travel note. \"early-stage\" changed to the book's \"recuperating from musculoskeletal injuries\". Added one sentence on active visualisation (review Missing item 5, PDF 241 / p.230) with its proviso. |\n| claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff.md | Title changed to \"... Adaptation Removes Both Within 2-3 Weeks While Eccentric Work Capacity Rises\". Hortobagyi sentence quoted and cross-referenced to the p.230 \"often contradictory\" statement and the contested sibling. Downhill-running application labelled Inference and flagged as rat data. Locator to PDF p. 242 / printed p. 231. \"over time\" deleted. Also removed the unsourced label \"(the repeated-bout effect)\" and replaced the unsourced \"microdamage that manifests as DOMS\" causal claim with the book's \"accompanied by\" wording. |\n| claim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings.md | Added the p.231 Hortobagyi side (quoted, PDF 242). When NOT now opens with the contested reason (p.230 vs p.231) and Handford et al 2024 (vault note, p.9 non-superiority statement, checked). Deleted \"consistent with modern eccentric tendon-loading protocols\"; replaced with the book's own \"training and rehabilitation implication\" and a note that tendinopathy use is an extension. \"small, heterogeneous samples\" changed to \"sample sizes not given in the text\"; \"populations\" changed to the book's training velocity, measurement velocity, muscle groups and specificity principle. |\n| framework--isokinetic-method-claimed-advantages-and-mechanism.md | Locator to PDF pp. 242-243 / printed 231-232. \"most effective maintenance\" changed to the quoted \"highly effective maintenance of muscular strength\". Added the p.232 quadriceps-to-squat non-transfer and the untested-qualities / elite-competitor sentences. When NOT: recorded Thistle et al 1967 as \"established\" on p.232 vs \"neither proved nor ... universally accepted\" on p.235 (PDF 246). Also repointed the swimmer quote to the physics-limitation entry, where it actually sits (it was attributed to the testing-limitations entry). |\n| heuristic--isokinetic-strength-ratios-vary-by-angle-and-velocity.md | Fig 4.16 convention added (90 degrees = full extension), with values re-read from the image (about 3.0 and 2.1 at 80 degrees; 1.5 near 50 degrees; y-axis 0.5-3.5) and the caption's angle sentence quoted. Figure attribution fixed: uncited Cybex data; Vorobyev supplies the 80:20 / 50:50 ratios, Stephens & Reid the gravity-correction critique. Recorded the same-page tension (Vorobyev targets \"should be\" vs \"meaningless\"). Eric's baseline split into its own paragraph quoting method-baseline line 111, with the RTP judgment on a separate Inference line. Locator to PDF pp. 244-245 / printed 233-234. \"Why it works\" now says the authors give no mechanism, with the averaging point labelled Inference. Added Inference notes on the reversed angle convention and on Vorobyev's named measurement point. Confidence left `supported` (see Disagreements). Added [[claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive]] to Related. Frontmatter `chapter: \"4.2.6.1\"` changed to `\"4.2.6.2\"`, and \"4.2.6.2 Limitations of the Isokinetic Method\" added to the locator string: the rendered PDF 244 image shows the 4.2.6.2 heading at the top of the page, above \"Fundamental Biomechanics\" and \"Recommended Strength Ratios\" (the text extraction puts it at the foot of the page). |\n| claim--isokinetic-devices-cannot-be-truly-isokinetic-physics-limitation.md | Title changed to \"... Isokinetic Tests Miss the Acceleration Phases Where the Authors Say Most Injuries Occur, and the Elastic-Energy Contribution of Natural Movement\". Injury-transition claim (a) and elastic-energy claim (b) separated in What it is, Why it works and When NOT, both quoted. Injury claim marked as the authors' assertion with no citation. When NOT: added the book's own use of isokinetic data (Fig 4.16, PDF 245 / p.234; Komi & Buskirk, PDF 241 / p.230) and a pointer to the contested sibling. Locator to PDF p. 244 / printed p. 233. Example: the unsourced sprinting-hamstring injury-concentration clause removed. Added [[framework--isokinetic-method-claimed-advantages-and-mechanism]] to Related. |\n| heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md | Title changed to \"Three Grounds (Anatomy, Physiology, Specificity) on Which ...\"; \"six points\" changed to \"the itemised points\". Added the p.235 phase-restriction sentence (PDF 246) and the p.236 \"relatively useless\" sentence (PDF 247), both quoted; When to apply now says, per the authors, to confine isokinetic work to early general conditioning or rehabilitation. Contested reason now has pages: Fig 4.16 (PDF 245 / p.234), Komi & Buskirk (PDF 241 / p.230), Figs 2.8-2.9 (cited PDF 243 / p.232 and PDF 246 / p.235). \"Ostering\" changed to \"Osternig\". \"(see 4.3, not yet ingested in this pass)\" changed to \"(see 4.3)\". Locator changed from PDF pp. 245-247 / printed 234-236 to PDF pp. 244-247 / printed 233-236, because the Cybex/Orthotron introduction the entry summarises sits under the 4.2.6.2 heading on PDF 244. |\n| claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method.md | Variant 2 sentence quoted verbatim (\"an equivalent affect on the improvement of speed-strength and absolute strength\") with the note that the comparator is unclear. When to apply: \"useful mainly for variety\" now an Inference conditional on the second reading. |\n| heuristic--choice-of-muscle-training-regime-favor-combination.md | `confidence: supported` changed to `contested`. Added the p.231 \"premature ... any regime or combination of regimes\" statement with its three stated deficiencies (PDF 242), set against the p.237 \"reasonable certainty\" (PDF 248), in What it is and as the contested reason in When NOT. Noted that only Pletnev 1975 is a reported direct comparison. Title changed to \"... The Authors Judge Combining Regimes the Most Rational Approach\". \"(see 4.3, not yet ingested in this pass)\" changed to \"(see 4.3)\". \"nine corroborating citations\" changed to \"The corroborating citations\" (the list is nine author groups, ten works). \"Why it works\" reworded so it no longer asserts that combined regimes outperform. |\n| framework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines.md | Reversed sentence fixed: the book says the NFR machine-only facility gives the inferior multi-dimensional conditioning (quoted, PDF 249 / p.238). Ratov wording fixed to the book's \"supposed to imitate or train ... specific loads in the different regimes of muscular work (Ratov, 1976)\". \"to a single plane and pattern\" changed to the book's \"fixed bars and pivots\". Added the p.237 \"profoundly counterproductive for sport specific trainees\" sentence (PDF 248). |\n| heuristic--nfr-machine-limitations-safety-and-appropriate-use.md | False Eric's baseline replaced. It now quotes method-baseline lines 59-60 (\"the standard CMJ family\" on ForceDecks; \"the isometric seated calf raise is run on the ForceDecks 'Mid-Thigh Pull' setting: 90-degree ankle/knee position\") and notes they are tagged Inferred (protocol). A separate Inference line says the seated calf raise is a fixed-angle isometric test, not NFR machine training. Injury-equivalence comparison marked as uncited in What it is and When NOT (p.239). Locator printed pp. 238-240 changed to 238-239. Wilke 1999 not inserted (see Disagreements); instead When NOT notes the disc figures rest on one cited source (Chaffin & Andersson 1984) and that the library holds no modern source on the point. |\n| claim--nfr-machine-marketing-claims-examined.md | Title changed to \"The Authors Argue Variable-Resistance Machines' Core Marketing Premise Is a Physics Error, and That NFR Machines Need Many More Units to Match Free Weights\". Machine-count mechanism now reads \"The authors give no mechanism for the counts. Inference: ...\". Added an Inference caveat to When NOT: the Newtonian point does not rebut the torque-angle argument. When to apply points to that limit. The \"useful features\" sentence is now labelled Inference. |\n| claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive.md | \"Usefully\" dropped from the title. When NOT now opens with the Class-2 peak (asymmetry declined with higher mastery, PDF 251 / p.240). Mechanism replaced with the book's \"could exacerbate the existing problem or cause new injuries\" plus a labelled Inference. The 10% finding is marked as research the authors do not cite. Eric's baseline now quotes method-baseline (\"optional symmetry metrics (L/R differences)\", tagged \"Adopted practice, framework not Eric's own\"), with the monitoring-not-correction judgment on its own Inference line. \"Soviet sprint-training research programme\" (not in the source) replaced by the classification levels the text gives. Linked Bishop 2016 and Callaway 2026 in Related (both resolve in the vault). |\n| claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load.md | Title changed to \"'Muscle Isolation' Is Possible Only for Small Muscle Groups at Low Intensity; Heavy or Prolonged Resistance ...\". The EMG evidence is now quoted as \"minute muscle groups\". When to apply: rehab value separated from the EMG evidence, and limb-muscle isolation labelled Inference. Added \"or the greater the duration\" (quoted, with the restrained-joints clause), \"equal or exceed\", and the PNF overflow point. \"nearly as strongly\" changed to the quoted \"may be as strongly contracted as the triceps\". The irradiation linkage is now labelled Inference. |\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Ratio entry not raised to `contested`.** The review says \"consider\". I read the page: \"they have determined that this ratio ... should be nearer 80:20\" is Vorobyev's finding in reported speech (PDF 244 / p.233). The authors' own conclusion runs one way (\"meaningless to prescribe an optimal ratio for any joint\"). The brief's rule is X and not-X in the source's own voice, or another library source or modern evidence that disagrees. Neither applies: a grep of the vault for hamstring:quadriceps / H:Q ratio sources found none. I recorded the same-page tension in the body and left `supported`.\n2. **Regime-choice entry set to `contested`**, as the review recommends. Both statements are the authors' own voice on the same question: \"to speak of anything definitive ... any regime or combination of regimes is premature\" (PDF 242 / p.231) and \"Today it can be ascertained with reasonable certainty ...\" (PDF 248 / p.237). This agrees with the review.\n3. **Wilke et al 1999 not inserted** (NFR limitations). The reviewer marked it as to be verified. It is not in the vault (grep for \"Wilke\" found nothing), and a Zotero search (\"Wilke intradiscal pressure in vivo sitting standing\") returned no items. I left it out and added a neutral single-source caveat instead.\n4. **Alfredson et al 1998 not inserted** (eccentric vs concentric). It is not in the vault. Zotero holds it only as a reference inside other papers (Malliaras 2013, Kjaer 2004, Douglas 2017), not as a held paper, so I could not confirm the \"slow\" characterisation. I deleted the unsupported clause, as the review requires, and added no citation.\n5. **Oscillatory isometrics: an extra source mismatch recorded.** The review asks only for the Soechting & Roberts sentence. The page says \"the maximum tension that can be produced voluntarily\" during the 5 Hz jerks (PDF 235 / p.224), while Fig 4.11 (PDF 236 / p.225) files oscillatory under reflexive. I recorded both.\n6. **Hettinger duration-vs-intensity: attribution refined.** The review attributes \"about one-third max rising to 80-95%\" to Hettinger & Muller. On PDF 33 / p.22 only the one-third threshold is cited to H&M (1953); the 80-95% rise is the authors' continuation (Verkhoshansky 1977 follows). PDF 34 / p.23 (Korobkov et al) also partly agrees with the p.224 inference for beginners. Both sides are written that way.\n7. **Endurance entry: Fig 4.14 not cited for temperature.** The book's own \"(Fig 4.14)\" pointer for the temperature optimum (PDF 238 / p.227) points to a figure of endurance vs %MVC with no temperature data. I recorded this as a source oddity.\n8. **Regional-specificity entry (GO): I added no link.** The review's suggested Moyer link is a reciprocal link for the vault note; it is listed below, not added here.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d6d2cefe-9b0c-403c-adf6-5d67fb33286f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:20:36.122Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Atomization brief (shared by every Flint ingest agent)\n\nRead this whole file before writing anything. It holds the rules the Harries review taught (2026-09-26: 25 of 35 files needed fixes). The review gate will check every rule here.\n\n## Schema\n\nFollow `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\skills\\ingest-book\\SKILL.md` exactly: `_domain.md`, `_book.md` (map and resume checkpoint, updated after EACH chapter or unit), and entries named `<kind>--<kebab-slug>.md` with kind one of principle, framework, case-study, heuristic, claim. Use the frontmatter keys exactly as the skill gives them. The body has **What it is** / **Why it works** / **When to apply** / **When NOT to apply** (mandatory, non-trivial) / **Example** / **Related**.\n\nStyle reference, read before writing: `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library\\strength-and-conditioning\\fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer\\case-study--isometric-sprinter-and-hurdler.md`, plus one principle file in the same folder.\n\n## Voices: label every one\n\n- **The source author's statements are plain text.**\n- **Eric's views** (from `docs/method-baseline.md` or his reviews) are prefixed \"Eric's baseline:\" or \"Eric's review:\".\n- **Your own reasoning, application or synthesis** is prefixed \"Inference:\".\n- **Nothing unlabeled that the author did not say.** The most common failures: a judgment from Eric's review written as if the author said it; a mechanism the author never gave presented as \"why it works\" (write \"The author gives no mechanism. Inference: ...\"); an invented example.\n\n## Lessons from the Supertraining Chapter 1 review (2026-09-26: 30 FIX, 1 NO-GO of 41)\n\n- **Keep the source's internal contradictions.** When a book says X on one page and not-X on another, record both with their pages and set `contested`. Never pick one side silently. The NO-GO entry reversed the book's SJ-CMJ strength-deficit rule because the book states it both ways.\n- **Name the right voice.** In co-authored books, write \"the authors\" unless a passage clearly belongs to one author. A passage that names Verkhoshansky in the third person is Siff writing.\n- **Keep `proven` rare and scoped.** Scope it to the replicated core (for example, \"Hill's force-velocity curve\"), never the whole entry, and mark any disputed sentence inside it. Four of eight `proven` ratings were lowered.\n- **No mechanism the source does not give.** About a dozen \"Why it works\" sections supplied physiology the book never states. Label it \"Inference:\" or leave it out.\n- **Do not add \"well established\" or \"modern research shows\" without a citation.** Name the paper, or delete the phrase.\n\n## Lessons from the 2026-09-27 review wave (Supertraining Ch2-3, Issurin Ch3, Bondarchuk, Evans, Old Bull)\n\n- **Invented Examples are the most common failure.** 34 of 36 Supertraining Ch2-3a Examples were unlabelled inventions. Use the source's own example where it gives one; otherwise start the Example with \"Inference:\".\n- **\"Verified against the page image\" means you opened the image.** Four Issurin Ch3 entries claimed it for figures they had misread (Fig 3.7 inverted, a Table 3.7 row swapped). Sparse slides are not \"bare diagrams\" until you have looked.\n- **Name the population and method the source gives.** Bondarchuk's data are 7,000+ native and foreign athletes, questionnaires, r >= 0.349, steroid-free; entries that said \"Soviet athletes\" were wrong everywhere.\n- **Check what a cited paper actually says.** Issurin 2016's critique targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's A-T-R; Herzog & Leonard 2000 shows force enhancement, against the book's sentence. When the book misreports a study, record both.\n- **No review history in entries.** \"Prior version\", \"corrected from a NO-GO finding\" and similar belong in `staging/fixes_*_applied.md`, never in the entry body.\n- **Fix logs must match the files.** One fixer log claimed fixes that were not made. Fixers run their own grep checks and paste the output.\n- **Section headers use the vault form** `**What it is** —` (with the em dash). Do not add suffixes such as \": mandatory\". Em dashes are banned only in titles and body text. Never wrap a `[[wikilink]]` across lines.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Every number, definition and quote carries a checkable locator: PDF page (and printed page if it differs), or file plus timestamp. Never write \"confirmed in PDF\" unless you opened that page.\n- Never invent a number. Keep ambiguity visible: mark unclear audio or OCR as unclear, and do not harmonize two conflicting numbers from the source; record both with their locators.\n- OCR sources (`staging/ocr/*.md`): pages listed as low_confidence in the header must be checked against the page image before any number from them is cited. Use the Read tool on the PDF page, or render it.\n- **OCR confidence scores miss the worst errors** (checked 2026-09-27). Zatsiorsky 2e: the OCR interleaves the two columns and the sidebars line by line, so its text is not readable prose; use it only to find content, and read and quote from the page image. *CFTS*: single-column prose that mostly reads in order, but upside-down lines come out as gibberish, some lines are dropped, and figures are scrambled, all at \"0 low-confidence pages\". For any OCR source, quote only after checking the page image, and read every figure and table from the image.\n\n## Confidence\n\n- Practitioner courses and lectures: ceiling `supported`.\n- Books: `proven` only for established, widely replicated physiology or mechanics. Soviet-era or single-lab empirical findings and training prescriptions: `supported` at most. `untested` for single cases and anecdotes. `contested` where another library source or modern evidence disagrees (say which).\n\n## Dedup and converge\n\nBefore each entry, grep `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library` and the staging library (`C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\staging\\library`) for the concept. If it is covered, add a new entry only for a genuinely new angle, and link the existing one in Related. List reciprocal-link suggestions in your final report. Never edit files in the vault or another agent's staging folder.\n\n## Baylor context\n\nRead `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\method-baseline.md` (approved). Use it in When to apply, labelled \"Eric's baseline:\". Department names: Athletic Performance, Athletic Medicine, Applied Performance. Plain style, no em dashes. Use commas, colons and parentheses.\n\n## Scope and output\n\n- Write ONLY into your assigned staging folder. No git, no vault edits.\n- Stop at a chapter or unit boundary. It is better to finish fewer chapters well; the `_book.md` checkpoint lets the next batch resume.\n- Final reply: entries by kind and by confidence; chapters or units done and pending with page ranges; reciprocal-link suggestions; unclear or contradictory spots in the source; anything you could not read.\nYou are an independent reviewer in Flint's ingest review gate. Another model wrote these entries; your job is to FALSIFY them, not confirm. Read-only on entries and sources. Write only your report file.\n\nStandards: `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md` (read it first). Every rule there is a check.\n\nFor EVERY entry in scope:\n1. Every number, definition, table value and quote appears at the cited PDF page (and printed page) and means the same thing. Open the pages yourself; do not trust the entry.\n2. Attribution: plain text is the book author's own statement. Anything else must be labelled \"Eric's baseline:\", \"Eric's review:\" or \"Inference:\". Flag any unlabelled claim the author did not make, any \"why it works\" mechanism the author did not give, and any invented example.\n3. Confidence: `proven` only for established, widely replicated physiology or mechanics, so challenge every `proven`. Soviet-era or single-lab findings and prescriptions: `supported` at most. `untested` for anecdotes. `contested` needs a stated reason.\n4. \"When NOT to apply\" is present and substantive.\n5. Duplicates: flag any entry that restates an existing vault entry (grep `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library`) without a new angle.\n6. Missing: up to 5 important ideas in the covered pages that no entry captures.\n\nOutput (Markdown): a table with one row per entry: file | verdict (GO / FIX / NO-GO) | problem, quoting the entry and the source with its page | exact correction. For GO rows, list what you checked (\"checked: 2.5x at PDF 23 / p.12\"). Then the missing list. Then an overall verdict: PROMOTE, GO AFTER FIXES (list them), or NO-GO.\n# Fixes applied: Supertraining 4.2.4-4.2.9 (Ch4-2b), 20 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-09-27. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch4-2b_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, text of PDF idx 32-35 and 233-253 extracted with PyMuPDF; page images rendered and opened for PDF 236 (Figs 4.11, 4.12), PDF 238 (Figs 4.13, 4.14), PDF 244 (the 4.2.6.2 heading position) and PDF 245 (Fig 4.16). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 20 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-2b.txt`. `_book.md` not touched (the review asks for no change there). No new entries written.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| claim--hettinger-muller-isometric-strength-gain-rate.md | Locator to PDF pp. 234-235 / printed 223-224. When NOT: \"corroborated only by a 1970 review\" replaced by the book's \"rather contradictory data\" (Verkhoshansky 1970 review, p.224). Added the duration-vs-intensity tension with both sides: H&M one-third threshold and the authors' 80-95% (PDF 33 / p.22), Korobkov et al \"approximately equivalent increases\" at 20-80% 1RM early in training (PDF 34 / p.23, partly agreeing with p.224), the book's 70-80% explosive protocol (PDF 237 / p.226), and Oranchuk 2019 as relayed by Natera in the Moyer entry. Added the 6 / 10 / 8 s hold-length cross-reference (PDF 237 / p.226). \"Why it works\" reworded so the no-mechanism statement is the authors' own, not an unlabelled writer claim. Quoted the \"fundamental conditioning factor\" sentence exactly. Linked [[claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus]] and [[framework--isometric-training-variables-and-adaptations]] in body and Related. |\n| claim--isometric-endurance-limits-and-recovery-phases.md | Locator to PDF pp. 238-239 / printed 227-228. Added Clarke et al (1958) as the source of the temperature optimum, with Petrofsky & Phillips (1986) placing it near 27-28 C. Fig 4.13 description tightened after re-opening the image (about 0 kPa at 0% to about 20 kPa at 100%). \"Why it works\" now uses the book's wording (\"unable to meet the physiological demands\") instead of a paraphrased pressure-exceeds-perfusion mechanism. When to apply: recovery timeline scoped, as Inference, to recovery of endurance after a fatiguing sustained contraction. Example: plank, wall-sit and mid-thigh pull removed; kept as Inference with \"whether a given hold is under 10-15% of maximum is not shown in the source\". Added a note that the text's \"(Fig 4.14)\" pointer for temperature is a source oddity (Fig 4.14 plots endurance vs %MVC). |\n| claim--isometric-regional-specificity-not-strictly-angular.md | No change needed. Review verdict GO; I re-checked the 15-degree band, Thepault-Mathieu numbers (54/25, 30/25, 25/7) and the \"should not be interpreted\" caution on PDF 237-238 / p.226-227 and found no error. Reciprocal-link suggestion below. |\n| framework--isometric-contraction-categorization-voluntary-reflexive.md | Fig 4.12 sentence rewritten from the image: curves are labelled \"Slow Isometrics\" and \"Explosive Isometrics\"; the caption says reflexive explosive action is steeper still, with the caption quoted. When NOT: \"detailed experimental corroboration\" rescoped to the absolute-strength vs explosive-strength split (p.225). Added oscillatory isometrics with Soechting & Roberts 1975, 5 Hz, and Basmajian 1978 (PDF 235 / p.224), plus \"negative evaluations of this method are premature\" (PDF 236 / p.225). Recorded a source mismatch the review did not raise: the only evidence is for voluntarily produced 5 Hz jerks, while Fig 4.11 files oscillatory under reflexive (Inference line in When NOT). When to apply: \"reflexive (explosive or oscillatory)\" changed to reflexive explosive, per the text; \"clean-and-jerk catch\" changed to the book's \"clean-and-jerk\". Locators now carry PDF and printed pages. |\n| heuristic--isometric-priming-and-external-internal-classification.md | \"Why it works\": reaction-time mechanism replaced with \"The authors give no mechanism for the 7% reaction-time figure\", plus a labelled Inference linking to the p.224 inhibition argument and noting it concerns force. When to apply: reframed as a progressive-overload step (p.229); \"before attempting a true max attempt\" removed. Example: invented 110-120% replaced with the book's own 120-150% shoulder support and quasi-isometric first degrees of a squat. Locator to PDF p. 240 / printed p. 229. Quoted the priming and \"advocated by their scientists\" wording. Rack/spotter safety sentence labelled Inference. |\n| heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks.md | Recorded the unreconciled 6 s / 10 s / 8 s hold limits on PDF 237 / p.226 with the exact wording. Methods reworded to \"methods of execution ... recommended for developing absolute strength\". Cardiovascular sentence split: the source's \"may have a harmful effect ... in some individuals\", then \"Inference:\" for the risk-factor and pairing advice. |\n| heuristic--loadless-training-broomstick-protocol.md | Kovalik attribution fixed: only the 5 sets are Kovalik's; the 3-10 s rest is \"research indicates\"; breathing, 10-15 s movement and 6-8 s hold are given without a source. When to apply points to \"the protocol given on PDF p. 241, printed p. 230\". Extensor-flexor reasoning labelled Inference. Added the book's stated corroboration (dynamic stretching against resistance, no citation) and the pushups/free-standing squats travel note. \"early-stage\" changed to the book's \"recuperating from musculoskeletal injuries\". Added one sentence on active visualisation (review Missing item 5, PDF 241 / p.230) with its proviso. |\n| claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff.md | Title changed to \"... Adaptation Removes Both Within 2-3 Weeks While Eccentric Work Capacity Rises\". Hortobagyi sentence quoted and cross-referenced to the p.230 \"often contradictory\" statement and the contested sibling. Downhill-running application labelled Inference and flagged as rat data. Locator to PDF p. 242 / printed p. 231. \"over time\" deleted. Also removed the unsourced label \"(the repeated-bout effect)\" and replaced the unsourced \"microdamage that manifests as DOMS\" causal claim with the book's \"accompanied by\" wording. |\n| claim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings.md | Added the p.231 Hortobagyi side (quoted, PDF 242). When NOT now opens with the contested reason (p.230 vs p.231) and Handford et al 2024 (vault note, p.9 non-superiority statement, checked). Deleted \"consistent with modern eccentric tendon-loading protocols\"; replaced with the book's own \"training and rehabilitation implication\" and a note that tendinopathy use is an extension. \"small, heterogeneous samples\" changed to \"sample sizes not given in the text\"; \"populations\" changed to the book's training velocity, measurement velocity, muscle groups and specificity principle. |\n| framework--isokinetic-method-claimed-advantages-and-mechanism.md | Locator to PDF pp. 242-243 / printed 231-232. \"most effective maintenance\" changed to the quoted \"highly effective maintenance of muscular strength\". Added the p.232 quadriceps-to-squat non-transfer and the untested-qualities / elite-competitor sentences. When NOT: recorded Thistle et al 1967 as \"established\" on p.232 vs \"neither proved nor ... universally accepted\" on p.235 (PDF 246). Also repointed the swimmer quote to the physics-limitation entry, where it actually sits (it was attributed to the testing-limitations entry). |\n| heuristic--isokinetic-strength-ratios-vary-by-angle-and-velocity.md | Fig 4.16 convention added (90 degrees = full extension), with values re-read from the image (about 3.0 and 2.1 at 80 degrees; 1.5 near 50 degrees; y-axis 0.5-3.5) and the caption's angle sentence quoted. Figure attribution fixed: uncited Cybex data; Vorobyev supplies the 80:20 / 50:50 ratios, Stephens & Reid the gravity-correction critique. Recorded the same-page tension (Vorobyev targets \"should be\" vs \"meaningless\"). Eric's baseline split into its own paragraph quoting method-baseline line 111, with the RTP judgment on a separate Inference line. Locator to PDF pp. 244-245 / printed 233-234. \"Why it works\" now says the authors give no mechanism, with the averaging point labelled Inference. Added Inference notes on the reversed angle convention and on Vorobyev's named measurement point. Confidence left `supported` (see Disagreements). Added [[claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive]] to Related. Frontmatter `chapter: \"4.2.6.1\"` changed to `\"4.2.6.2\"`, and \"4.2.6.2 Limitations of the Isokinetic Method\" added to the locator string: the rendered PDF 244 image shows the 4.2.6.2 heading at the top of the page, above \"Fundamental Biomechanics\" and \"Recommended Strength Ratios\" (the text extraction puts it at the foot of the page). |\n| claim--isokinetic-devices-cannot-be-truly-isokinetic-physics-limitation.md | Title changed to \"... Isokinetic Tests Miss the Acceleration Phases Where the Authors Say Most Injuries Occur, and the Elastic-Energy Contribution of Natural Movement\". Injury-transition claim (a) and elastic-energy claim (b) separated in What it is, Why it works and When NOT, both quoted. Injury claim marked as the authors' assertion with no citation. When NOT: added the book's own use of isokinetic data (Fig 4.16, PDF 245 / p.234; Komi & Buskirk, PDF 241 / p.230) and a pointer to the contested sibling. Locator to PDF p. 244 / printed p. 233. Example: the unsourced sprinting-hamstring injury-concentration clause removed. Added [[framework--isokinetic-method-claimed-advantages-and-mechanism]] to Related. |\n| heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md | Title changed to \"Three Grounds (Anatomy, Physiology, Specificity) on Which ...\"; \"six points\" changed to \"the itemised points\". Added the p.235 phase-restriction sentence (PDF 246) and the p.236 \"relatively useless\" sentence (PDF 247), both quoted; When to apply now says, per the authors, to confine isokinetic work to early general conditioning or rehabilitation. Contested reason now has pages: Fig 4.16 (PDF 245 / p.234), Komi & Buskirk (PDF 241 / p.230), Figs 2.8-2.9 (cited PDF 243 / p.232 and PDF 246 / p.235). \"Ostering\" changed to \"Osternig\". \"(see 4.3, not yet ingested in this pass)\" changed to \"(see 4.3)\". Locator changed from PDF pp. 245-247 / printed 234-236 to PDF pp. 244-247 / printed 233-236, because the Cybex/Orthotron introduction the entry summarises sits under the 4.2.6.2 heading on PDF 244. |\n| claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method.md | Variant 2 sentence quoted verbatim (\"an equivalent affect on the improvement of speed-strength and absolute strength\") with the note that the comparator is unclear. When to apply: \"useful mainly for variety\" now an Inference conditional on the second reading. |\n| heuristic--choice-of-muscle-training-regime-favor-combination.md | `confidence: supported` changed to `contested`. Added the p.231 \"premature ... any regime or combination of regimes\" statement with its three stated deficiencies (PDF 242), set against the p.237 \"reasonable certainty\" (PDF 248), in What it is and as the contested reason in When NOT. Noted that only Pletnev 1975 is a reported direct comparison. Title changed to \"... The Authors Judge Combining Regimes the Most Rational Approach\". \"(see 4.3, not yet ingested in this pass)\" changed to \"(see 4.3)\". \"nine corroborating citations\" changed to \"The corroborating citations\" (the list is nine author groups, ten works). \"Why it works\" reworded so it no longer asserts that combined regimes outperform. |\n| framework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines.md | Reversed sentence fixed: the book says the NFR machine-only facility gives the inferior multi-dimensional conditioning (quoted, PDF 249 / p.238). Ratov wording fixed to the book's \"supposed to imitate or train ... specific loads in the different regimes of muscular work (Ratov, 1976)\". \"to a single plane and pattern\" changed to the book's \"fixed bars and pivots\". Added the p.237 \"profoundly counterproductive for sport specific trainees\" sentence (PDF 248). |\n| heuristic--nfr-machine-limitations-safety-and-appropriate-use.md | False Eric's baseline replaced. It now quotes method-baseline lines 59-60 (\"the standard CMJ family\" on ForceDecks; \"the isometric seated calf raise is run on the ForceDecks 'Mid-Thigh Pull' setting: 90-degree ankle/knee position\") and notes they are tagged Inferred (protocol). A separate Inference line says the seated calf raise is a fixed-angle isometric test, not NFR machine training. Injury-equivalence comparison marked as uncited in What it is and When NOT (p.239). Locator printed pp. 238-240 changed to 238-239. Wilke 1999 not inserted (see Disagreements); instead When NOT notes the disc figures rest on one cited source (Chaffin & Andersson 1984) and that the library holds no modern source on the point. |\n| claim--nfr-machine-marketing-claims-examined.md | Title changed to \"The Authors Argue Variable-Resistance Machines' Core Marketing Premise Is a Physics Error, and That NFR Machines Need Many More Units to Match Free Weights\". Machine-count mechanism now reads \"The authors give no mechanism for the counts. Inference: ...\". Added an Inference caveat to When NOT: the Newtonian point does not rebut the torque-angle argument. When to apply points to that limit. The \"useful features\" sentence is now labelled Inference. |\n| claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive.md | \"Usefully\" dropped from the title. When NOT now opens with the Class-2 peak (asymmetry declined with higher mastery, PDF 251 / p.240). Mechanism replaced with the book's \"could exacerbate the existing problem or cause new injuries\" plus a labelled Inference. The 10% finding is marked as research the authors do not cite. Eric's baseline now quotes method-baseline (\"optional symmetry metrics (L/R differences)\", tagged \"Adopted practice, framework not Eric's own\"), with the monitoring-not-correction judgment on its own Inference line. \"Soviet sprint-training research programme\" (not in the source) replaced by the classification levels the text gives. Linked Bishop 2016 and Callaway 2026 in Related (both resolve in the vault). |\n| claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load.md | Title changed to \"'Muscle Isolation' Is Possible Only for Small Muscle Groups at Low Intensity; Heavy or Prolonged Resistance ...\". The EMG evidence is now quoted as \"minute muscle groups\". When to apply: rehab value separated from the EMG evidence, and limb-muscle isolation labelled Inference. Added \"or the greater the duration\" (quoted, with the restrained-joints clause), \"equal or exceed\", and the PNF overflow point. \"nearly as strongly\" changed to the quoted \"may be as strongly contracted as the triceps\". The irradiation linkage is now labelled Inference. |\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Ratio entry not raised to `contested`.** The review says \"consider\". I read the page: \"they have determined that this ratio ... should be nearer 80:20\" is Vorobyev's finding in reported speech (PDF 244 / p.233). The authors' own conclusion runs one way (\"meaningless to prescribe an optimal ratio for any joint\"). The brief's rule is X and not-X in the source's own voice, or another library source or modern evidence that disagrees. Neither applies: a grep of the vault for hamstring:quadriceps / H:Q ratio sources found none. I recorded the same-page tension in the body and left `supported`.\n2. **Regime-choice entry set to `contested`**, as the review recommends. Both statements are the authors' own voice on the same question: \"to speak of anything definitive ... any regime or combination of regimes is premature\" (PDF 242 / p.231) and \"Today it can be ascertained with reasonable certainty ...\" (PDF 248 / p.237). This agrees with the review.\n3. **Wilke et al 1999 not inserted** (NFR limitations). The reviewer marked it as to be verified. It is not in the vault (grep for \"Wilke\" found nothing), and a Zotero search (\"Wilke intradiscal pressure in vivo sitting standing\") returned no items. I left it out and added a neutral single-source caveat instead.\n4. **Alfredson et al 1998 not inserted** (eccentric vs concentric). It is not in the vault. Zotero holds it only as a reference inside other papers (Malliaras 2013, Kjaer 2004, Douglas 2017), not as a held paper, so I could not confirm the \"slow\" characterisation. I deleted the unsupported clause, as the review requires, and added no citation.\n5. **Oscillatory isometrics: an extra source mismatch recorded.** The review asks only for the Soechting & Roberts sentence. The page says \"the maximum tension that can be produced voluntarily\" during the 5 Hz jerks (PDF 235 / p.224), while Fig 4.11 (PDF 236 / p.225) files oscillatory under reflexive. I recorded both.\n6. **Hettinger duration-vs-intensity: attribution refined.** The review attributes \"about one-third max rising to 80-95%\" to Hettinger & Muller. On PDF 33 / p.22 only the one-third threshold is cited to H&M (1953); the 80-95% rise is the authors' continuation (Verkhoshansky 1977 follows). PDF 34 / p.23 (Korobkov et al) also partly agrees with the p.224 inference for beginners. Both sides are written that way.\n7. **Endurance entry: Fig 4.14 not cited for temperature.** The book's own \"(Fig 4.14)\" pointer for the temperature optimum (PDF 238 / p.227) points to a figure of endurance vs %MVC with no temperature data. I recorded this as a source oddity.\n8. **Regional-specificity entry (GO): I added no link.** The review's suggested Moyer link is a reciprocal link for the vault note; it is listed below, not added here.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9ad9d1d9-c9da-4af6-bce3-6fbbfe3ee476","session_id":"0949803b-b5a4-4eec-8bc8-87b3ccc4c359","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main","sessionKind":"bg"}
