~/.claude/skills/ingest-book/SKILL.md | head -150; cd ~/SecondBrain && git pull --no-rebase --no-edit 2>&1 | tail -2; git log --oneline -3","description":"Read gate memory, ingest-book schema, pull vault"}},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011CffT9DMfkBJz6bp6u6ysk","type":"assistant","uuid":"22f6e9aa-446f-43ad-bfde-d30fd78f4ae2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:21:56.955Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"0949803b-b5a4-4eec-8bc8-87b3ccc4c359","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main","sessionKind":"bg"}
{"parentUuid":"22f6e9aa-446f-43ad-bfde-d30fd78f4ae2","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"f097ba1e-802d-4788-86d0-92157481e0f4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019R4Xs9TiAC8Hp683vQiUBS","type":"tool_result","content":"---\nname: ingest-review-gate\ndescription: \"Before any expensive, hard-to-inspect ingest (ingest-book atomization): a verification step plus an independent model review must pass first\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: feedback\n  originSessionId: 4597514e-1de9-4f80-87b7-4c8742ddec83\n  modified: 2026-09-05T14:57:33.666Z\n---\n\n**Stated 2026-09-05**, finishing the Myszka book scan, when Eric was asked how much of the\npipeline could run on a cheaper model.\n\n**Do not run `/ingest-book` (or any comparable atomization) straight off a freshly built\nartifact. Gate it on two things: an automated verification step, and a review by an\nindependent model — Codex, or a fresh adversarial agent if Codex is unavailable.**\n\n**Why:** the ingest is the expensive step *and* the one whose output is hardest to eyeball.\nA page-order error costs almost nothing to make, is baked into the PDF, and is then\ninherited silently by every note built from it. Eric's own framing: \"cheap step, very\nexpensive mistake.\" He explicitly declined to cheapen the ingest itself, because that is\nwhere judgement about what matters in the text happens.\n\n**How to apply:**\n\n- Build the artifact, then **verify by a route that shares no code path with how it was\n  built.** For the book scan: the order was set by a *visual* read of cropped folio bands,\n  so verification read the folio out of the *full-page OCR text* of the finished PDF, and a\n  third pass rendered the PDF and read the folios off the rendered pages.\n- **A cheap arithmetic identity is worth having, but do not oversell it.** `files −\n  duplicates == last − first + 1` costs nothing; make it a hard gate that refuses to\n  proceed, and test it with a deliberately corrupted input. **But it is a map-consistency\n  check, not a proof of order.** On the default path — where folios are assigned as a\n  running counter — the two sides of the identity are equal *by construction*, so the gate\n  compares a number with itself. A transposition, a wrong start page, a missed duplicate,\n  and two cancelling errors all pass it.\n- **Audit the verification itself, not just the artifact.** The first version of\n  `verify_pdf.py` \"checked\" that folios increase by appending the *expected* value and\n  testing that those increased — true by construction, so it printed YES unconditionally.\n  A check that cannot fail is worse than no check. **Prove a check can fail**: feed it a\n  deliberately corrupted copy and confirm it says NO.\n- **State coverage and blind spots in the output.** Six of 240 pages printed no folio and\n  contributed no evidence; a uniform offset error is invisible to a folio check. Print\n  that, do not imply 100%.\n- Then hand it to the independent reviewer with an instruction to **falsify**, not confirm,\n  and with the specific claims spelled out.\n\n**The related cheap-model decision (same conversation):** a small model may take the first\npass at reading page numbers off contact sheets, because the sheet prints the *expected*\nnumber beside each crop — so the job is spotting disagreements, not reading every number.\nAnything it flags, and anything ambiguous, gets a careful second look. Eric chose this\nmainly for speed; he was explicit that the savings are small and **the gate is the part\nworth keeping.**\n\n**What the gate actually caught, 2026-09-05.** The independent review returned **GO** on the\nbook PDF and **NO-GO** on the reference file shipped alongside it — which is the whole\nargument for the gate in one result. It found: a verification check that was a tautology and\ncould never fail; an overstated claim about what the arithmetic gate proves; 7 chimeric\nbibliography records welding one entry's authors to another's title; and 67 of 113 records\ncarrying a phantom author literally named \"K\". All were fixed before ingest. **The page order\nwas sound, and the thing shipped beside it was not** — so gate every deliverable, not just\nthe expensive one.\n\nRelated: [[book-scan-page-ordering]] · [[keystoneos-verification-habits]]\n\n---\nname: ingest-book\ndescription: >-\n  Ingest a book (PDF/EPUB full-text, Kindle highlights, or framework-only from a\n  physical book) into the SecondBrain library as atomized, citable advisor entries. Use\n  when the user wants to \"ingest a book\", \"add a book to my library/advisors\",\n  \"/ingest-book\", or points at a book file for the advisor system. Do not use for\n  articles, transcripts, or general resources (use ingest-resource).\nargument-hint: \"[book file or title] [domain]\"\n---\n\n# Ingest Book\n\nTurn a book into atomized, citable entries under the library subtree, grouped by domain and book, ready for the `advisor` skill.\n\n**Library root:** `vault_library` in the selected platform config\n**Paths:** canonical vault locations live in `~/.claude/skills/_shared/config.<platform>.json` (`vault_library`, `vault_root`) — load it if unsure; if a literal path here ever disagrees, the config wins.\n**Extractors:** `~/.claude/skills/ingest-book/lib/extract_epub.py`, `extract_pdf.py`\nRun extractors inside the context-mode sandbox (`ctx_execute`, language python) so raw book text never enters the conversation — only the atomized entries you write do.\n\n## Model split (cost vs quality)\n\nThis skill mixes three kinds of work with very different model needs:\n- **Extraction & structure mapping** (probe, chapter map, text cleanup) — deterministic\n  Python in the sandbox. **Zero model tokens; model choice irrelevant.**\n- **Orchestration** (searches, `_book.md` updates, dedup checks) — mechanical.\n  **Haiku-tier is fine.**\n- **Atomization** (Phase 2/3: distilling prose into accurate entries with correct\n  mechanism-vs-claim, honest confidence, and a real \"when NOT to apply\") — this is where\n  the advisor's entire value is created. **Run on Sonnet or stronger. Do NOT use Haiku\n  for atomization** — a weak distillation becomes a permanent low-quality entry, and the\n  generated output is only ~3K tokens/chapter, so quality-per-token matters far more than\n  price-per-token. Cost levers that DON'T hurt quality: atomize only chapters you'll\n  consult, and checkpoint + `/clear` between chapter batches (see Rules) so context\n  doesn't compound across the book.\n\n## Schema for every entry note\n\nFilename: `<kind>--<kebab-slug>.md`, `kind ∈ {principle, framework, case-study, heuristic, claim}`.\n\n```yaml\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: <domain-slug>\nbook: <book-slug>\nchapter: \"N\"            # or section label; \"\" if highlights with no location\ntopic: <2-5 word topic>\ncategory: [tag, tag]\nconfidence: proven      # proven | supported | untested | contested\ningest-tier: full-text  # full-text | highlights | framework-only\ncreated: <YYYY-MM-DD>\n---\n\n# <Human title of the idea>\n\n**What it is** — one tight paragraph.\n**Why it works** — mechanism, not restatement.\n**When to apply** — concrete triggers.\n**When NOT to apply** — scope limits. (MANDATORY — never omit.)\n**Example** — one concrete instance.\n```\n\n**Confidence is bounded by tier:** full-text ≤ proven; highlights ≤ supported;\nframework-only ≤ untested. Set `contested` by hand when sources conflict.\n\n## Phase 1 — Intake\n\n1. Identify: book title, author, source type (PDF/EPUB | Kindle highlights | physical), target domain.\n2. Compute `<book-slug>` = `kebab(title)--kebab(lastname)` (e.g. `supertraining--verkhoshansky`).\n3. If the domain folder/`_domain.md` doesn't exist, create them (mirror the S&C `_domain.md` format).\n4. Build `_book.md` chapter map:\n   - EPUB/PDF: run the extractor with `--list` / `--probe` to get the chapter or page structure.\n   - Physical: ask the user for the chapter list, or assemble from a verified public listing.\n   Write `_book.md` (template below) with every chapter `status: pending`.\n\n`_book.md` template:\n```markdown\n---\ntype: book-index\nbook: <book-slug>\ntitle: <full title>\nauthor: <author>\ndomain: <domain-slug>\ningest-tier: full-text | highlights | framework-only\nsource: \"<file path or 'physical' or 'kindle-highlights'>\"\ncreated: <YYYY-MM-DD>\n---\n\n# <title> — ingest map\n\n| # | Chapter | Status | Entries |\n|---|---------|--------|---------|\n| 0 | <title> | pending | 0 |\n```\n\n## Phase 2 — Extract (by tier)\n\n**Full-text (PDF/EPUB):**\n- First run `extract_pdf.py --probe`. If `scanned: true`, STOP the full-text path:\n  tell the user the PDF is image-only, and fall back to framework-only tier (set\n  `ingest-tier: framework-only`, cap confidence at untested). Never emit full-text-\n  confidence entries from a scanned file.\n- Work **one chapter at a time** (EPUB `--chapter N`; PDF `--pages A-B`, where A and B are\n  0-based PDF indexes and the range is inclusive: `--pages 12-20` returns 9 pages). Each page\n  comes back with `page` (0-based index) and `viewer_page` (what a PDF viewer shows, index + 1).\n  Cite pages one way throughout a book and state the convention in `_book.md`. For each:\n  extract candidate ideas → atomize per the schema → write entry notes.\n- **After each chapter**, update that row in `_book.md` to `status: done` with the\n  entry count. This is the resume checkpoint — never batch this update.\n\n**Kindle highlights:**\n- Parse `My Clippings.txt` or the highlights export in the sandbox. Cluster highlights\n  by theme; write one entry per theme. `ingest-tier: highlights`, confidence ≤ supported.\n- Attribute `chapter` from the highlight's location metadata when present, else \"\".\n\n**Framework-only (physical / scanned):**\n- Briefly interview the user for their takeaways. Supplement with *verified* public\n  chapter summaries. Write 5–15 framework-level entries. `ingest-tier: framework-only`,\n  confidence ≤ untested.\n\n## Phase 3 — Quality gate (run before writing each batch)\n\n- One idea per note. If an extracted point is compound, split it.\n- \"When NOT to apply\" present and non-trivial.\n- **Dedup + converge:** before writing, `memsearch search` the library for the same\n  concept. If an existing entry (any book) covers it, DON'T duplicate — instead add a\n  `[[wikilink]]` + one line to both notes recording the agreement, and only add a new\n  note if it contributes a genuinely new angle.\n- Never overwrite an existing file; on slug collision append `-2`.\n\n## Phase 4 — Close-out\n\n1. Set all done rows in `_book.md`; if partial, leave pending rows for next session.\n2. Add the book to `_domain.md`'s `books:` list and the \"Books in this domain\" section.\n3. Add the book-slug to the advisor persona's `books:` list (`advisors/<domain>.md`)\n   if an advisor exists.\n4. Run `memsearch index \"<vault_library>\"` using the selected platform config\n   so entries are searchable immediately (don't wait for the SessionEnd hook).\n5. Commit the vault using `vault_root` from the selected platform config: `git -C \"<vault_root>\" add Knowledge/library && git commit -m \"ingest(<book-slug>): <N> entries, ch X-Y\"`.\n6. Report: entries by kind and confidence, links created, chapters remaining.\n\n## Rules\n- Raw book text stays in the sandbox; only atomized entries enter context/the vault.\n- Resumable by design: `_book.md` is the source of truth for \"where am I\".\n- A 300-page book is a multi-session job — stop at a chapter boundary, never mid-chapter.\n create mode 100644 Knowledge/library/sports-analytics-and-decision-making/ssac-talks--42analytics/principle--training-schedule-confounds-acwr-and-injury.md\n create mode 100644 Knowledge/library/sports-analytics-and-decision-making/ssac-talks--42analytics/principle--value-an-uncertain-asset-by-its-outcome-distribution-not-its-mean.md\naa41109 merge(42analytics): two Jenkins causal-credit principles into one entry\nad006ad fix(42analytics): Codex falsify review, 99 entries fixed\n1931871 meetings: fireflies sync (6 new)\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/kristinarash/DEV","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d5c11afc-ea33-4f89-a187-8440466d5747","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:21:58.460Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\nname: ingest-review-gate\ndescription: \"Before any expensive, hard-to-inspect ingest (ingest-book atomization): a verification step plus an independent model review must pass first\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: feedback\n  originSessionId: 4597514e-1de9-4f80-87b7-4c8742ddec83\n  modified: 2026-09-05T14:57:33.666Z\n---\n\n**Stated 2026-09-05**, finishing the Myszka book scan, when Eric was asked how much of the\npipeline could run on a cheaper model.\n\n**Do not run `/ingest-book` (or any comparable atomization) straight off a freshly built\nartifact. Gate it on two things: an automated verification step, and a review by an\nindependent model — Codex, or a fresh adversarial agent if Codex is unavailable.**\n\n**Why:** the ingest is the expensive step *and* the one whose output is hardest to eyeball.\nA page-order error costs almost nothing to make, is baked into the PDF, and is then\ninherited silently by every note built from it. Eric's own framing: \"cheap step, very\nexpensive mistake.\" He explicitly declined to cheapen the ingest itself, because that is\nwhere judgement about what matters in the text happens.\n\n**How to apply:**\n\n- Build the artifact, then **verify by a route that shares no code path with how it was\n  built.** For the book scan: the order was set by a *visual* read of cropped folio bands,\n  so verification read the folio out of the *full-page OCR text* of the finished PDF, and a\n  third pass rendered the PDF and read the folios off the rendered pages.\n- **A cheap arithmetic identity is worth having, but do not oversell it.** `files −\n  duplicates == last − first + 1` costs nothing; make it a hard gate that refuses to\n  proceed, and test it with a deliberately corrupted input. **But it is a map-consistency\n  check, not a proof of order.** On the default path — where folios are assigned as a\n  running counter — the two sides of the identity are equal *by construction*, so the gate\n  compares a number with itself. A transposition, a wrong start page, a missed duplicate,\n  and two cancelling errors all pass it.\n- **Audit the verification itself, not just the artifact.** The first version of\n  `verify_pdf.py` \"checked\" that folios increase by appending the *expected* value and\n  testing that those increased — true by construction, so it printed YES unconditionally.\n  A check that cannot fail is worse than no check. **Prove a check can fail**: feed it a\n  deliberately corrupted copy and confirm it says NO.\n- **State coverage and blind spots in the output.** Six of 240 pages printed no folio and\n  contributed no evidence; a uniform offset error is invisible to a folio check. Print\n  that, do not imply 100%.\n- Then hand it to the independent reviewer with an instruction to **falsify**, not confirm,\n  and with the specific claims spelled out.\n\n**The related cheap-model decision (same conversation):** a small model may take the first\npass at reading page numbers off contact sheets, because the sheet prints the *expected*\nnumber beside each crop — so the job is spotting disagreements, not reading every number.\nAnything it flags, and anything ambiguous, gets a careful second look. Eric chose this\nmainly for speed; he was explicit that the savings are small and **the gate is the part\nworth keeping.**\n\n**What the gate actually caught, 2026-09-05.** The independent review returned **GO** on the\nbook PDF and **NO-GO** on the reference file shipped alongside it — which is the whole\nargument for the gate in one result. It found: a verification check that was a tautology and\ncould never fail; an overstated claim about what the arithmetic gate proves; 7 chimeric\nbibliography records welding one entry's authors to another's title; and 67 of 113 records\ncarrying a phantom author literally named \"K\". All were fixed before ingest. **The page order\nwas sound, and the thing shipped beside it was not** — so gate every deliverable, not just\nthe expensive one.\n\nRelated: [[book-scan-page-ordering]] · [[keystoneos-verification-habits]]\n\n---\nname: ingest-book\ndescription: >-\n  Ingest a book (PDF/EPUB full-text, Kindle highlights, or framework-only from a\n  physical book) into the SecondBrain library as atomized, citable advisor entries. Use\n  when the user wants to \"ingest a book\", \"add a book to my library/advisors\",\n  \"/ingest-book\", or points at a book file for the advisor system. Do not use for\n  articles, transcripts, or general resources (use ingest-resource).\nargument-hint: \"[book file or title] [domain]\"\n---\n\n# Ingest Book\n\nTurn a book into atomized, citable entries under the library subtree, grouped by domain and book, ready for the `advisor` skill.\n\n**Library root:** `vault_library` in the selected platform config\n**Paths:** canonical vault locations live in `~/.claude/skills/_shared/config.<platform>.json` (`vault_library`, `vault_root`) — load it if unsure; if a literal path here ever disagrees, the config wins.\n**Extractors:** `~/.claude/skills/ingest-book/lib/extract_epub.py`, `extract_pdf.py`\nRun extractors inside the context-mode sandbox (`ctx_execute`, language python) so raw book text never enters the conversation — only the atomized entries you write do.\n\n## Model split (cost vs quality)\n\nThis skill mixes three kinds of work with very different model needs:\n- **Extraction & structure mapping** (probe, chapter map, text cleanup) — deterministic\n  Python in the sandbox. **Zero model tokens; model choice irrelevant.**\n- **Orchestration** (searches, `_book.md` updates, dedup checks) — mechanical.\n  **Haiku-tier is fine.**\n- **Atomization** (Phase 2/3: distilling prose into accurate entries with correct\n  mechanism-vs-claim, honest confidence, and a real \"when NOT to apply\") — this is where\n  the advisor's entire value is created. **Run on Sonnet or stronger. Do NOT use Haiku\n  for atomization** — a weak distillation becomes a permanent low-quality entry, and the\n  generated output is only ~3K tokens/chapter, so quality-per-token matters far more than\n  price-per-token. Cost levers that DON'T hurt quality: atomize only chapters you'll\n  consult, and checkpoint + `/clear` between chapter batches (see Rules) so context\n  doesn't compound across the book.\n\n## Schema for every entry note\n\nFilename: `<kind>--<kebab-slug>.md`, `kind ∈ {principle, framework, case-study, heuristic, claim}`.\n\n```yaml\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: <domain-slug>\nbook: <book-slug>\nchapter: \"N\"            # or section label; \"\" if highlights with no location\ntopic: <2-5 word topic>\ncategory: [tag, tag]\nconfidence: proven      # proven | supported | untested | contested\ningest-tier: full-text  # full-text | highlights | framework-only\ncreated: <YYYY-MM-DD>\n---\n\n# <Human title of the idea>\n\n**What it is** — one tight paragraph.\n**Why it works** — mechanism, not restatement.\n**When to apply** — concrete triggers.\n**When NOT to apply** — scope limits. (MANDATORY — never omit.)\n**Example** — one concrete instance.\n```\n\n**Confidence is bounded by tier:** full-text ≤ proven; highlights ≤ supported;\nframework-only ≤ untested. Set `contested` by hand when sources conflict.\n\n## Phase 1 — Intake\n\n1. Identify: book title, author, source type (PDF/EPUB | Kindle highlights | physical), target domain.\n2. Compute `<book-slug>` = `kebab(title)--kebab(lastname)` (e.g. `supertraining--verkhoshansky`).\n3. If the domain folder/`_domain.md` doesn't exist, create them (mirror the S&C `_domain.md` format).\n4. Build `_book.md` chapter map:\n   - EPUB/PDF: run the extractor with `--list` / `--probe` to get the chapter or page structure.\n   - Physical: ask the user for the chapter list, or assemble from a verified public listing.\n   Write `_book.md` (template below) with every chapter `status: pending`.\n\n`_book.md` template:\n```markdown\n---\ntype: book-index\nbook: <book-slug>\ntitle: <full title>\nauthor: <author>\ndomain: <domain-slug>\ningest-tier: full-text | highlights | framework-only\nsource: \"<file path or 'physical' or 'kindle-highlights'>\"\ncreated: <YYYY-MM-DD>\n---\n\n# <title> — ingest map\n\n| # | Chapter | Status | Entries |\n|---|---------|--------|---------|\n| 0 | <title> | pending | 0 |\n```\n\n## Phase 2 — Extract (by tier)\n\n**Full-text (PDF/EPUB):**\n- First run `extract_pdf.py --probe`. If `scanned: true`, STOP the full-text path:\n  tell the user the PDF is image-only, and fall back to framework-only tier (set\n  `ingest-tier: framework-only`, cap confidence at untested). Never emit full-text-\n  confidence entries from a scanned file.\n- Work **one chapter at a time** (EPUB `--chapter N`; PDF `--pages A-B`, where A and B are\n  0-based PDF indexes and the range is inclusive: `--pages 12-20` returns 9 pages). Each page\n  comes back with `page` (0-based index) and `viewer_page` (what a PDF viewer shows, index + 1).\n  Cite pages one way throughout a book and state the convention in `_book.md`. For each:\n  extract candidate ideas → atomize per the schema → write entry notes.\n- **After each chapter**, update that row in `_book.md` to `status: done` with the\n  entry count. This is the resume checkpoint — never batch this update.\n\n**Kindle highlights:**\n- Parse `My Clippings.txt` or the highlights export in the sandbox. Cluster highlights\n  by theme; write one entry per theme. `ingest-tier: highlights`, confidence ≤ supported.\n- Attribute `chapter` from the highlight's location metadata when present, else \"\".\n\n**Framework-only (physical / scanned):**\n- Briefly interview the user for their takeaways. Supplement with *verified* public\n  chapter summaries. Write 5–15 framework-level entries. `ingest-tier: framework-only`,\n  confidence ≤ untested.\n\n## Phase 3 — Quality gate (run before writing each batch)\n\n- One idea per note. If an extracted point is compound, split it.\n- \"When NOT to apply\" present and non-trivial.\n- **Dedup + converge:** before writing, `memsearch search` the library for the same\n  concept. If an existing entry (any book) covers it, DON'T duplicate — instead add a\n  `[[wikilink]]` + one line to both notes recording the agreement, and only add a new\n  note if it contributes a genuinely new angle.\n- Never overwrite an existing file; on slug collision append `-2`.\n\n## Phase 4 — Close-out\n\n1. Set all done rows in `_book.md`; if partial, leave pending rows for next session.\n2. Add the book to `_domain.md`'s `books:` list and the \"Books in this domain\" section.\n3. Add the book-slug to the advisor persona's `books:` list (`advisors/<domain>.md`)\n   if an advisor exists.\n4. Run `memsearch index \"<vault_library>\"` using the selected platform config\n   so entries are searchable immediately (don't wait for the SessionEnd hook).\n5. Commit the vault using `vault_root` from the selected platform config: `git -C \"<vault_root>\" add Knowledge/library && git commit -m \"ingest(<book-slug>): <N> entries, ch X-Y\"`.\n6. Report: entries by kind and confidence, links created, chapters remaining.\n\n## Rules\n- Raw book text stays in the sandbox; only atomized entries enter context/the vault.\n- Resumable by design: `_book.md` is the source of truth for \"where am I\".\n- A 300-page book is a multi-session job — stop at a chapter boundary, never mid-chapter.\n create mode 100644 Knowledge/library/sports-analytics-and-decision-making/ssac-talks--42analytics/principle--training-schedule-confounds-acwr-and-injury.md\n create mode 100644 Knowledge/library/sports-analytics-and-decision-making/ssac-talks--42analytics/principle--value-an-uncertain-asset-by-its-outcome-distribution-not-its-mean.md\naa41109 merge(42analytics): two Jenkins causal-credit principles into one entry\nad006ad fix(42analytics): Codex falsify review, 99 entries fixed\n1931871 meetings: fireflies sync (6 new)","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/kristinarash/DEV","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"22f6e9aa-446f-43ad-bfde-d30fd78f4ae2","session_id":"0949803b-b5a4-4eec-8bc8-87b3ccc4c359","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main","sessionKind":"bg"}
