{"parentUuid":"c706e1eb-79a2-4f8b-ac0e-c4d4fad24524","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:fork","toolUseID":"24758806-fd99-478a-af5d-cb7467747970","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\\n  </when_not_to_use>\\n\\n  <file_writing_policy>\\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\\n  </file_writing_policy>\\n\\n  <output_constraints>\\n    <artifact_policy>\\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\\n    </artifact_policy>\\n  </output_constraints>\\n  <session_continuity>\\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\\n  </session_continuity>\\n\\n  <ctx_commands>\\n    \\\"ctx stats\\\" | \\\"ctx-stats\\\" | \\\"/ctx-stats\\\" | context savings question\\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx doctor\\\" | \\\"ctx-doctor\\\" | \\\"/ctx-doctor\\\" | diagnose context-mode\\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx upgrade\\\" | \\\"ctx-upgrade\\\" | \\\"/ctx-upgrade\\\" | update context-mode\\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx purge\\\" | \\\"ctx-purge\\\" | \\\"/ctx-purge\\\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\\n\\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \\\"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\\\"\\n  </ctx_commands>\\n\\n</context_window_protection>\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/sessionstart.mjs\"","durationMs":428},"type":"attachment","uuid":"4fc1fa18-459e-4edb-afc6-c371ef7f82ec","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:26:21.124Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"4fc1fa18-459e-4edb-afc6-c371ef7f82ec","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash, Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds football GPS and force-plate pipelines. Wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo on 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-first player-coach near Iowa, $90K+ floor, mid-2027. Read `~/DEV/ContextVault` before career or personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python/R. Two machines: Windows by day, Mac by evening (split 9/24). Tool paths: machine.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB. No cloud servers or schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Google Drive only; keep DuckDB local to avoid sync locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n- Staff-facing text: Athletic Performance (not S&C), Athletic Medicine (not sports medicine), Applied Performance (not sport science).\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Challenge vague or rehearsed reasoning: \"sharpened, not soothed.\"\n- Decide standard technical details, state the choice, and move on.\n- Build in small steps. Do not break mid-season work.\n- Authors direction when leadership does not; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo group.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads (max 5; park one to add one)\n- **Status report**: biweekly to 12/18. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline\n- **SW/Tech Review (10/1)**: Erin asked; NEXT Teams 10/2, ET by 11/30. →`SoftwareTech_Review/docs/`\n- **Ship queue (9/25)**: q2wk to a named user: RI5 #002 wk 9/28, intern M1 10/9, AP Digest ch1 10/23. →`RunningTheRoom/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **PP Recruiting (9/30)**: 017 sent-ready; 018 SHIP. NEXT send 018, walkthrough. →`PP_Recruiting/docs/`\n- **RTP CLA (9/26)**: L1–2 built. NEXT Tim/Jacob export; Joe → L3. →`RTP_Constraints_Course/docs/`\n\n## Parked (NEXT lives in each STATUS.md; `where-was-i.py` lists them)\n- Prioritization slate LIVE 5/5 →Tier A prioritization-slate\n- Personal site: scroll-craft LOCAL, Artifact live →Tier A personal-site-build-routes\n- FB matchups: EPA = PFF via `scripts/pff_epa_profile.py` →`MatchupAnalysis_FB2026`\n- Flint: NEXT Eric: OneDrive keep/drop, 2 verify →`Flint`\n- 42Analytics: NEXT captions (429s) on Mac →`42Analytics`\n- VB GRID: fix merged 10/2 (#2); await Mike's reply →`Volleyball_GRID`\n- Sleep sched: NEXT night/early-kickoff →`sleep-scheduler`\n- TypeSafe: MAINTENANCE; PR #31 merged as record →`TypeSafe_Sandbox`\n\n## Environment Notes\n- Machine split (9/24): Win=day, Mac=eve; Mac sole writer GPS/Perch/VALD.\n- Hooks: patch both copies for shared events. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- daily-backup: manifest entries alone copy nothing; sweep nested ~/.claude repos →handoff 09-19.\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` = private I-983 repo; folder has a space, repo an underscore.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Win: Drive sync, junction and renv traps →machine.md. PFF: swap Parquet on Drive, never the DB; `PFF_DB`.\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture Mac only. Dashboard →command-centre.\n- Post-merge: delete merged branches unasked (verify) →Tier A post-merge-cleanup.\n- Sessions: start `where-was-i.py`, prune with `session-sweep.py` →Tier A session-hygiene.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- None open. (OKR gate + OKRs_2026-27 CLOSED 10/2: Eric not developing further.)\n\n---\n\n## HOOK FAILURES — surface these to the user on the first response\n\n- **memsearch-sync** ×1 (2026-10-02 22:00 → 2026-10-02 22:00 UTC): index exit 1: grpc._channel._InactiveRpcError: <_InactiveRpcError of RPC that terminated with: (full output: C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\global-memory\\transcripts\\.memsearch-index.log)\n\nFull log: `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log`. Surface this once; clear the file after the user has seen it and the cause is handled.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4310 chars, updated 2026-10-02 11:26)"],"hookName":"SessionStart","toolUseID":"SessionStart","hookEvent":"SessionStart"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"4fa4f85e-2aef-4b85-8856-767fd3e24776","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:26:21.191Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nSessionStart hook additional context: ## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash, Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds football GPS and force-plate pipelines. Wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo on 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-first player-coach near Iowa, $90K+ floor, mid-2027. Read `~/DEV/ContextVault` before career or personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python/R. Two machines: Windows by day, Mac by evening (split 9/24). Tool paths: machine.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB. No cloud servers or schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Google Drive only; keep DuckDB local to avoid sync locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n- Staff-facing text: Athletic Performance (not S&C), Athletic Medicine (not sports medicine), Applied Performance (not sport science).\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Challenge vague or rehearsed reasoning: \"sharpened, not soothed.\"\n- Decide standard technical details, state the choice, and move on.\n- Build in small steps. Do not break mid-season work.\n- Authors direction when leadership does not; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo group.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads (max 5; park one to add one)\n- **Status report**: biweekly to 12/18. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline\n- **SW/Tech Review (10/1)**: Erin asked; NEXT Teams 10/2, ET by 11/30. →`SoftwareTech_Review/docs/`\n- **Ship queue (9/25)**: q2wk to a named user: RI5 #002 wk 9/28, intern M1 10/9, AP Digest ch1 10/23. →`RunningTheRoom/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **PP Recruiting (9/30)**: 017 sent-ready; 018 SHIP. NEXT send 018, walkthrough. →`PP_Recruiting/docs/`\n- **RTP CLA (9/26)**: L1–2 built. NEXT Tim/Jacob export; Joe → L3. →`RTP_Constraints_Course/docs/`\n\n## Parked (NEXT lives in each STATUS.md; `where-was-i.py` lists them)\n- Prioritization slate LIVE 5/5 →Tier A prioritization-slate\n- Personal site: scroll-craft LOCAL, Artifact live →Tier A personal-site-build-routes\n- FB matchups: EPA = PFF via `scripts/pff_epa_profile.py` →`MatchupAnalysis_FB2026`\n- Flint: NEXT Eric: OneDrive keep/drop, 2 verify →`Flint`\n- 42Analytics: NEXT captions (429s) on Mac →`42Analytics`\n- VB GRID: fix merged 10/2 (#2); await Mike's reply →`Volleyball_GRID`\n- Sleep sched: NEXT night/early-kickoff →`sleep-scheduler`\n- TypeSafe: MAINTENANCE; PR #31 merged as record →`TypeSafe_Sandbox`\n\n## Environment Notes\n- Machine split (9/24): Win=day, Mac=eve; Mac sole writer GPS/Perch/VALD.\n- Hooks: patch both copies for shared events. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- daily-backup: manifest entries alone copy nothing; sweep nested ~/.claude repos →handoff 09-19.\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` = private I-983 repo; folder has a space, repo an underscore.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Win: Drive sync, junction and renv traps →machine.md. PFF: swap Parquet on Drive, never the DB; `PFF_DB`.\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture Mac only. Dashboard →command-centre.\n- Post-merge: delete merged branches unasked (verify) →Tier A post-merge-cleanup.\n- Sessions: start `where-was-i.py`, prune with `session-sweep.py` →Tier A session-hygiene.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- None open. (OKR gate + OKRs_2026-27 CLOSED 10/2: Eric not developing further.)\n\n---\n\n## HOOK FAILURES — surface these to the user on the first response\n\n- **memsearch-sync** ×1 (2026-10-02 22:00 → 2026-10-02 22:00 UTC): index exit 1: grpc._channel._InactiveRpcError: <_InactiveRpcError of RPC that terminated with: (full output: C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\global-memory\\transcripts\\.memsearch-index.log)\n\nFull log: `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log`. Surface this once; clear the file after the user has seen it and the cause is handled.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4310 chars, updated 2026-10-02 11:26)\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"4fa4f85e-2aef-4b85-8856-767fd3e24776","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"f097ba1e-802d-4788-86d0-92157481e0f4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>aee5390828e4af715</task-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/aee5390828e4af715.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Atomize Supertraining 4.3-4.4\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I've atomized Chapter 4, sections 4.3 and 4.4, into 23 new entries, and I stopped at the end of 4.4 without starting Chapter 5. Lint is clean for these files once the vault path is corrected for the Mac (see lint output below).\n\nA diff of the staging folder against the vault folder shows only the 23 new files plus `_book.md` as different. No existing entry was touched. In `_book.md` I set rows 4.3 (`done | 13`) and 4.4 (`done | 10`) after each section and appended a batch note that says to resume at Chapter 5, printed 255 / PDF idx 266. The scope file is `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-3-4.txt`. I did not edit the Chapter 4 parent row, which still reads `in progress | 6`; whoever owns `_book.md` should update it.\n\n**Entries by kind:** 9 claim, 7 heuristic, 3 principle, 2 framework, 2 case-study.\n\n**Entries by confidence:**\n- **supported:** 18\n- **contested:** 2\n  - The endurance-muscle-qualities entry: the authors say both raise VO2 max (p.249) and that training's value is not in raising it (p.250). They also say both \"decrease the proportion of glycolysis\" (p.249) and that high glycolytic productivity is needed (p.253).\n  - The haemoglobin/myoglobin entry: one citation doesn't fit (see the source problems below).\n- **untested:** 2\n  - The all-round-athletes regime claim, which the authors say \"needs rigorous experimental substantiation\".\n  - The regime-switching claim, which has no citation.\n- **proven:** 1, limited at the top of the entry to the maths that P = F.V holds only when force is constant.\n\n**Sections done:**\n\n| Section | Printed pages | PDF idx | Entries |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 4.3 | 241-248 | 252-259 | 13 |\n| 4.4 | 248-253 | 259-264 | 10 |\n\nSection 4.3.6 runs onto printed 248, where 4.4 starts mid-page. Printed 254 (PDF 265) is blank. Chapter 4 is now complete.\n\n**Page images:** I opened PDF 253-257 and 260-264 as images, covering Figs 4.17-4.24, Tables 4.1-4.2 and the equations. PDF 252, 258, 259 and 265 were read from the text layer only, and no entry claims image verification for them.\n\n**Duplicate check:** Supertraining had no entry for the five dynamic-correspondence criteria themselves. `framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence` is now the anchor, and it links the Ch1.4 ten-dimensions entry, both Evans entries, Bondarchuk's three-viewpoints entry and Moyer's Soviet-lineage entry.\n\n**Suggested reciprocal links (not made):**\n- `principle--specificity-of-training-ten-dimensions`: replace \"(this book's Ch4.3, not yet ingested)\" with `[[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]]`.\n- `heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity` and `heuristic--choice-of-muscle-training-regime-favor-combination`: change \"(see 4.3)\" to \"(see 4.3, [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]])\".\n- Evans `case-study--dynamic-correspondence-applied-to-wrestling-takedown`: its own pending suggestion can now point to the five-criteria entry and to `heuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter`.\n- `claim--isometric-regional-specificity-not-strictly-angular` and `claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer`: add `[[heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle]]`.\n- `claim--joint-angle-and-limb-position-changes-force-output` (the 210° sartorius data): add `[[heuristic--match-amplitude-direction-and-line-of-resistance-to-the-sport]]`.\n- `framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test` and `principle--rate-of-force-development-as-independent-limiter`: add `[[heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements]]`.\n- `claim--lactate-and-oxygen-debt-myths`: add `[[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]]`.\n- `claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order`: add the phasic-analysis entry and the high-jumper case study.\n- `claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases`: add the four-level functional-specialisation framework.\n- Issurin `case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay` and Ch1 `claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals`: add `[[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]]` (an effect in the opposite direction).\n- Moyer `claim--wearable-resistance-kinetic-energy-sprint` and the Petrakos 2016 sled paper: add both parachute entries.\n\n**Unclear or contradictory spots in the source:**\n- **Fig 4.21:** the text calls it \"hip flexion torque in the air\", but the caption says \"take-off for the triple jump\".\n- **\"6% ... only 7%\":** p.250 (PDF 261) says blood lactate \"can increase by some 6% ... while the VO2 max level increases by only 7%\". The page image confirms both numbers, so the sentence doesn't make sense as printed. I marked it unclear and do not cite it.\n- **Equation (4.1):** it is introduced with \"if F is constant ... we obtain\", but it is the general form. The impulse F.Δt is defined \"where s is the displacement\", though s does not appear in it (PDF 257).\n- **4.3.3 effort rule:** training effort \"should not be less than ... should even exceed\" the sport's, yet light, fast sports get \"a moderate amount of effort\".\n- **Aerobic/anaerobic antagonism:** offered as an explanation on p.249, then \"must be questioned\" on p.250. I recorded both but didn't mark it contested on that ground alone.\n- **The two contested contradictions** described above: raising VO2 max, and how much glycolytic work to do.\n- **Pattengale &amp; Holloszy 1967:** the book cites it as showing strength training raises myoglobin more than endurance training. Its published title is \"Augmentation of skeletal muscle myoglobin by a program of treadmill running\" (Am J Physiol 213:783-785). I checked the title and listing only, not the paper itself.\n- **Tables 4.1 and 4.2:** the columns sum to about 79-89%, and the book doesn't explain the remainder.\n- **Other small items:** \"vesicular\" probably means \"vascular\"; the 210° hip angle has no source; the Fig 4.23 lines are labelled ambiguously, so I read only stride length off it.\n\n**Could not read:** nothing; every page in range was readable.\n\n**Lint output.** The official script hard-codes the Windows vault path, so on the Mac every cross-vault link shows as unresolved. The total of 55 includes older files; this is the official run filtered to my files plus the total:\n```\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence.md: unresolved [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/claim--wearable-resistance-kinetic-energy-sprint]]; unresolved [[research-papers/petrakos-2016-resisted-sled-sprint-training-to-improve-sprint-performance-a-systematic-review]]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence.md: unresolved [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/framework--soviet-lineage-of-sprint-training-ideas]]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--match-amplitude-direction-and-line-of-resistance-to-the-sport.md: unresolved [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/claim--wearable-resistance-kinetic-energy-sprint]]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release.md: unresolved [[research-papers/petrakos-2016-resisted-sled-sprint-training-to-improve-sprint-performance-a-systematic-review]]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--phasic-biomechanical-analysis-before-selecting-special-strength-means.md: unresolved [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/heuristic--emg-timing-shortlists-exercises-it-does-not-prove-transfer]]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 55\n```\nThe same script with the vault path set to `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`:\n```\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\nThat one remaining issue is the known `_book.md` false positive. My own checks over the 23 files found no em dashes outside section headers or in titles, no lines that break a link, no review-history wording, six section headers in every file, `created: 2026-10-03` everywhere, and every link resolving in staging or the vault.\n\nTo fix the hard-coded vault path in `scripts/lint_library.py`, it should read the platform config.\n\nOutput folder: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>283010</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>43</tool_uses><duration_ms>981092</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"},"uuid":"ecf1d7d1-071a-45ef-825d-11ab3f2790b5","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:38:38.767Z","permissionMode":"bypassPermissions","origin":{"kind":"task-notification","producer":"session-task"},"promptSource":"system","turnOrigin":"task_notification","turnPosition":{"promptIndex":2,"turnIndex":3},"queueSkipAttachments":true,"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
