{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:47:45.724Z","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ad35f6feb999bff94</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01TBbp8MX45cnZ3Q6r1Bo98D</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/ad35f6feb999bff94.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review Supertraining 4.3-4.4 round 1\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: GO AFTER FIXES.** Of the 23 entries, 15 are GO, 8 need fixes and none are NO-GO. `_book.md` needs one fix as well.\n\nReport: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch4-3-4_opus.md`\n\n**Required fixes:**\n1. **Power entry** (`claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant`): the physics is wrong and it is rated `proven`. Moment-to-moment power is always force times velocity, even when force varies. The real caution is that an average or peak force times velocity is not power. It needs a new title, a narrower `proven` scope and a rewritten \"When NOT to apply\" line.\n2. **Five-criteria framework**: the inference says the criteria conflict and cites Fig 4.22. The source says the opposite: awkward positions arise \"only when an exercise ... fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\" (PDF 256 / p.245).\n3. **Accentuated joint angle entry**: it quotes the \"incongruous and artificial\" positions but drops that same \"However, this occurs only when...\" sentence. It needs adding back.\n4. **Skater case study**: in Table 4.2, Class 1 factor IV is 2.7, which is smaller than factor V (8.9) and breaks the factor order. It may be a misprint for 12.7. The entry should mark 2.7 as unclear and stop quoting the \"2.7 to 36.5\" jump.\n5. **High jumper case study**: \"less to plantarflexor power\" must be labelled \"Inference:\". The book reports a smaller factor share, not a cut in training.\n6. **Parachute tradeoffs**: \"heavy athletes, athletes returning from lower-limb injury\" must be labelled \"Inference:\". The book names no population.\n7. **Boxer vs shot putter**: \"full recovery\" is not in the source. Label it or drop it.\n8. **Parachute sizing**: \"standard fluid mechanics\" is the writer's uncited claim. Use the author's own \"(actually the square of the velocity)\" plus a labelled inference.\n9. **`_book.md`**: the Chapter 4 parent row still says \"in progress | 6\", while the batch note says Chapter 4 is complete.\n\n**Known source oddities:**\n- Fig 4.21, the \"6% ... only 7%\" sentence, the 4.3.3 effort rule and the aerobic/anaerobic antagonism are all handled honestly.\n- The Equation 4.1 and impulse wording quirks are recorded correctly, but the power entry has the separate physics error in fix 1.\n- I confirmed the Pattengale &amp; Holloszy 1967 citation myself (PubMed 6036801, a treadmill-running study), so the entry's `contested` rating stands.\n- The writer's Table 4.1 and 4.2 column sums are correct: about 79/81% and 88/89%.\n\n**Other checks:**\n- I opened every figure, table and equation page as an image.\n- Every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote matches `method-baseline.md` at its cited line.\n- All wikilinks resolve, and every file has the six section headers with no other em dash.\n- There are no duplicates in the vault. The only possible missing idea is OBLA as the practical endurance target (PDF 260 / p.249), which is optional.\n\nAs the coordinator asked, page renders went to `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_renders/` (gitignored) instead of the scratchpad.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>198562</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>35</tool_uses><duration_ms>534039</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
