, do not apply it; record why under \\\"Disagreements with the review\\\". In particular, for the power-equals-force-times-velocity entry, get the physics right: instantaneous power P(t) = F(t)·v(t) always holds; the error is multiplying an average or peak force by an average or peak velocity. Keep what the authors actually wrote, quoted with locators, and label your own physics as \\\"Inference:\\\". Rescope `proven` narrowly or lower it.\\n- Edit only the files the review flags, plus `_book.md` (fix only the Chapter 4 parent row so it agrees with 4.1-4.4 all done; set its entry count to the sum of the Chapter 4 sub-rows). Do not touch any other entry, and no file outside this folder except your log.\\n- The OBLA idea is optional: add it as a new entry only if no existing entry covers it; if you add one, append its filename to the scope file and say so in the log.\\n- Brief rules hold: label every voice, locators on every number and quote, no em dashes in titles or body (section headers keep `**What it is** —`), no review history in entries, never wrap a [[wikilink]] across lines.\\n- Write the log to `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch4-3-4_applied.md` (Fixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03).\\n- Then prove the fixes landed: for each fixed file paste a grep showing the new wording, and run the lint with the Mac vault path, e.g. `cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && grep -n \\\"SecondBrain\\\" scripts/lint_library.py` to find the hard-coded path, and run a temp copy of the script with that path replaced by `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library` (do not edit the original script). Paste both outputs in your final reply and at the end of the log.\\n- No git. No vault edits.\\n\\nFinal reply: per-fix outcome (applied / disagreed), any new entry, grep proof, lint output.\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <deferred_tool_bootstrap>\\n    The context-mode tools below may be DEFERRED in your harness — their schemas are not loaded yet, so calling them directly fails (e.g. \\\"tool not found\\\" / InputValidationError). Load them ONCE before your first ctx_* call:\\n    ToolSearch(query: \\\"select:mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\\\")\\n    After that they are callable. If any ctx_* call fails as not-found, ToolSearch it and retry — do NOT fall back to Bash/Read just because the schema was not loaded yet.\\n  </deferred_tool_bootstrap>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\\n  </when_not_to_use>\\n\\n  <file_writing_policy>\\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\\n  </file_writing_policy>\\n\\n  <output_constraints>\\n    <artifact_policy>\\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\\n    </artifact_policy>\\n  </output_constraints>\\n  <session_continuity>\\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\\n  </session_continuity>\\n\\n</context_window_protection>\",\"subagent_type\":\"general-purpose\",\"model\":\"opus\"}}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/pretooluse.mjs\"","durationMs":134},"type":"attachment","uuid":"13a780b9-ba92-4c50-905c-338943d665f6","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:48:01.302Z","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"7713a909-3354-41f2-854a-2654f1794fb3","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"2a81cff2-a7a8-4e62-bcc6-0c023068f2b2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JpwDgPREm63sYovDLQnJm7","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Async agent launched successfully. (This tool result is internal metadata — never quote or paste any part of it, including the agentId below, into a user-facing reply.)\nagentId: a3aafe568bae89c86 (internal ID - do not mention to user. Use SendMessage with to: 'a3aafe568bae89c86', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent.)\nThe agent is working in the background. You will be notified automatically when it completes. You know nothing about its results until that notification arrives — do not report, assume, or predict them; continue other work or respond to the user in the meantime.\nDo not duplicate this agent's work — avoid working with the same files or topics it is using.\noutput_file: /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/a3aafe568bae89c86.output\nDo NOT Read or tail this file via the shell tool — it is the full subagent JSONL transcript and reading it will overflow your context. If the user asks for progress, say the agent is still running; you'll get a completion notification."}]}]},"uuid":"94233a15-dd5e-4a6e-ac11-6112e119388b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:48:01.441Z","toolUseResult":{"isAsync":true,"status":"async_launched","agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","description":"Fix Supertraining 4.3-4.4 entries","resolvedModel":"claude-opus-5-5[1m]","prompt":"You are the fixer in Flint's ingest review gate. Apply the fixes from a round-1 review to staged library entries, check each against the source yourself, and write a fix log.\n\nRead first:\n- Review: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch4-3-4_opus.md` (verdict GO AFTER FIXES: 8 FIX rows plus one `_book.md` fix; also an optional missing idea, OBLA as the practical endurance target, PDF 260 / p.249).\n- Standards: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md` (Windows paths in it; vault on this Mac is `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`, baseline is `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/method-baseline.md`).\n- Format to copy for your log: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch4-2b_applied.md` (a table of file | what changed, then \"Disagreements with the review\").\n\nFiles: the 23 entries in `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-3-4.txt`, in `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`, plus that folder's `_book.md`.\n\nSource: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. Page key: PDF idx (0-based) = printed + 11. Text: `~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \"import pymupdf; d=pymupdf.open('<pdf>'); print(d[N].get_text())\"`. Render pages to `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_renders/` and open them with Read for any figure, table or number you touch (Table 4.2 for the skater fix).\n\nRules:\n- Verify every review claim against the page before applying it. If the review is wrong, do not apply it; record why under \"Disagreements with the review\". In particular, for the power-equals-force-times-velocity entry, get the physics right: instantaneous power P(t) = F(t)·v(t) always holds; the error is multiplying an average or peak force by an average or peak velocity. Keep what the authors actually wrote, quoted with locators, and label your own physics as \"Inference:\". Rescope `proven` narrowly or lower it.\n- Edit only the files the review flags, plus `_book.md` (fix only the Chapter 4 parent row so it agrees with 4.1-4.4 all done; set its entry count to the sum of the Chapter 4 sub-rows). Do not touch any other entry, and no file outside this folder except your log.\n- The OBLA idea is optional: add it as a new entry only if no existing entry covers it; if you add one, append its filename to the scope file and say so in the log.\n- Brief rules hold: label every voice, locators on every number and quote, no em dashes in titles or body (section headers keep `**What it is** —`), no review history in entries, never wrap a [[wikilink]] across lines.\n- Write the log to `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch4-3-4_applied.md` (Fixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03).\n- Then prove the fixes landed: for each fixed file paste a grep showing the new wording, and run the lint with the Mac vault path, e.g. `cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && grep -n \"SecondBrain\" scripts/lint_library.py` to find the hard-coded path, and run a temp copy of the script with that path replaced by `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library` (do not edit the original script). Paste both outputs in your final reply and at the end of the log.\n- No git. No vault edits.\n\nFinal reply: per-fix outcome (applied / disagreed), any new entry, grep proof, lint output.\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n    The context-mode tools below may be DEFERRED in your harness — their schemas are not loaded yet, so calling them directly fails (e.g. \"tool not found\" / InputValidationError). Load them ONCE before your first ctx_* call:\n    ToolSearch(query: \"select:mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\")\n    After that they are callable. If any ctx_* call fails as not-found, ToolSearch it and retry — do NOT fall back to Bash/Read just because the schema was not loaded yet.\n  </deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n</context_window_protection>","outputFile":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/a3aafe568bae89c86.output","canReadOutputFile":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7713a909-3354-41f2-854a-2654f1794fb3","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg"