NOT edit `_book.md` (the coordinator owns it). Dedup against vault and staging; much of this ground (maximum strength methods, speed-strength, strength deficit, autoregulation/DAPRE, velocity-based training) may be covered by Supertraining Ch1-4, Zatsiorsky, Issurin, Bompa, Moyer or the VBT guide. Add an entry only for a genuinely new angle and link the existing one in Related. List reciprocal-link suggestions in your report instead of editing other files.\n- Use the source's own examples, otherwise start the Example with \"Inference:\". No mechanism the authors do not give. Record internal contradictions with both pages and set `contested`. Locators \"PDF p. X / printed p. Y\" for every number, definition and quote. \"The authors\" unless a passage clearly belongs to one author (a passage naming Verkhoshansky in the third person is Siff writing).\n- Frontmatter `chapter:` uses the section number (e.g. \"5.2.2\"). `created: 2026-10-03`. No em dashes in titles or body (section headers keep `**What it is** —`). No review history. Never wrap a [[wikilink]] across lines.\n- Write a scope file `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt` listing your new filenames, one per line.\n- Lint: copy `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py` already exists with Mac paths: run `cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky` and fix anything flagged in YOUR files. Paste the output lines for your files.\n- No git. No vault edits.\n\n## Final reply\nEntries by kind and confidence; entry count per section (5.1, 5.2, 5.2.1, 5.2.2, 5.2.3) with page ranges, for the coordinator's `_book.md`; reciprocal-link suggestions; unclear or contradictory spots in the source; anything you could not read; lint output.\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs rea