{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:02:24.413Z","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a9704cda0111951fd</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01CCBM77YmxEqh6YE3YG3UQe</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/a9704cda0111951fd.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Atomize Supertraining 5a\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I wrote 19 new entries for Chapter 5, sections 5.1 through 5.2.3 (PDF 266-278), and lint finds no problems in any of them. I added files only: no existing entry or `_book.md` was changed, and I made no git commits or vault edits. I opened the page image for all 13 pages and read every number, table, figure and equation from the image, not from the noisy text layer.\n\n**By kind:** 6 claims, 6 heuristics, 4 frameworks, 2 case studies, 1 principle.\n\n**By confidence:**\n- **proven, 1:** the Newton II / impulse entry. The rating covers one sentence only: the net force's impulse equals the change in momentum, for constant mass. The book's loose physics is marked outside that scope, under \"When NOT to apply\":\n  - The v·dm/dt term in Eq 5.1 is not a valid law for systems that lose mass.\n  - I = FΔT holds only when F is the average force.\n  - \"Area under the curve (i.e. the momentum)\" is wrong as written. The area is impulse, and in Fig 5.1's hold test nothing is gaining momentum.\n  - \"Change in direction is associated with a change in force\" mixes up needing a net force with the force itself changing.\n- **supported, 13.**\n- **untested, 3:** the APRE skill-learning, 6RM pyramid and muscle-testing protocol; the arm-throw-after-press case; the bounding-with-thigh-cuffs case.\n- **contested, 2:** the three maximum-strength methods framework, and the APRE framework. Both rest on contradictions inside the book.\n\n**Entry counts for `_book.md`:**\n\n| Section | Printed pp | PDF idx | Entries |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 5.1 | 255-257 | 266-268 | 4 |\n| 5.2 | 257-258 | 268-269 | 1 |\n| 5.2.1 | 258-261 | 269-272 | 5 |\n| 5.2.2 | 261-264 | 272-275 | 3 |\n| 5.2.3 | 264-267 | 275-278 | 6 |\n\nPage notes for the map:\n- 5.1 starts partway down PDF 266. The chapter lead-in above it produced no entry.\n- 5.2.3 runs onto PDF 278 and stops mid-page where 5.2.4 begins.\n- PDF 267 has a stray printed \"254\" above its page number \"256\".\n- I did not atomize the \"sports modelling\" definition on PDF 276, because it has no claim attached.\n- The scope file lists all 19 files: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt`.\n\n**Reciprocal links to add (I didn't edit these files)**, as existing file → new 5a entry:\n- `principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target` → Newton II entry. Fig 5.1 repeats this entry's athlete A/B comparison, so I linked it rather than re-atomizing it.\n- `claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines` → three-methods framework (the point that strength built on hypertrophy is retained longer).\n- `claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus` → tempo claim (only the 45 lifts per minute tempo detail is new).\n- `framework--two-force-regimes-overcoming-weight-vs-inertia` → speed-strength loading heuristic.\n- `framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing` and `claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature` → arm-throw case.\n- `heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity` (already names APRE) → APRE framework and the APRE testing heuristic.\n- `framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence` and Evans `case-study--dynamic-correspondence-applied-to-wrestling-takedown` → not-copying heuristic.\n- `claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases` → independent-qualities principle and analytical-synthetic heuristic.\n- Bompa `framework--ltad-sequential-model` and Issurin `principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout` → analytical-synthetic heuristic.\n- `framework--eight-types-of-muscular-tension` and Zatsiorsky `claim--strength-speed-correlation-depends-on-external-resistance` → two-groups framework.\n- `claim--velocity-and-contraction-specificity-asymmetric-transfer` and `claim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings` → Ivanov claim.\n- Bondarchuk `heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning` (it agrees that implements close to competition weight transfer best) and `claim--throws-implement-weight-transfer-near-universal` → implement-mass claim.\n- Bondarchuk `claim--sprint-jump-exercises-most-transferable-assistance` and Moyer `claim--wearable-resistance-kinetic-energy-sprint` → bounding case.\n- Moyer `framework--isometric-set-positions-ankle-knee-hip` → isometric APRE.\n- The Texas A&amp;M VBT guide → APRE framework.\n\n**Contradictions and unclear spots in the source (all recorded in the entries with both pages):**\n- **Sets:** \"more than three\" sets (printed 260) against \"reducing the number of sets\" (261).\n- **Method effects:** brief maximal tension gives \"a larger training effect\" (260) against \"similar significant increases\" from all three methods (261). Isometrics are \"not more\" effective than dynamic work (260-261) against \"similar increases\" with isometrics (261).\n- **Load to reps:** the 5RM is 89% of 1RM (258), yet 90-95% is paired with 3-6 reps (261) and 85-95% with 3-5RM (260). These cannot all be right.\n- **Krusen (1949):** sets at \"125%\" and \"150% of the 5RM\" for 5 reps, which no one can lift. Marked unclear.\n- **Berger (1965):** conclusion 1 describes a programme that is not among the five listed, and the page shows no data for conclusions 3, 5 and 6.\n- **APRE adjustment:** the text says more than 6 reps means increase the weight and fewer means decrease it, but Table 5.2 leaves the weight unchanged at 5-7 reps. The 10RM column also has no row for 0-3 reps, and the 3RM column none for 0.\n- **Table 5.3 arithmetic:** printed +38.8% and -6.8% recompute to +38.6% and -6.0%. The power row is roughly one tenth of force times speed, but not exactly, and its derivation and units are never stated. I did not compute power from it.\n- **Fig 5.4 against Table 5.3:** the figure is credited to 1999 and the table to 1977. The figure shows second-effort heights of about 67-69 cm, where the table gives 61.0.\n- **Isometric figures:** the captions say \"Ivenov\", the text says \"Ivanov\". The text says \"100% of one's 1RM\", the captions say \"percentages of maximal contraction\". Fig 5.3's rep labels can't be matched to its curves with confidence.\n- **Other:** \"146% of the initial level\" could mean \"to\" or \"by\". Explosive-ballistic tension is filed under the large-resistance group. The shot-put conclusion is stronger than its \"no significant difference\" data. The 5:1 light-to-heavy ratio has no citation.\n\n**Could not read:** nothing. I fixed text-layer errors from the images: Table 5.2's interleaved columns, \"iJO.2\" (30.2), \"Rabat\" (Kabat) and \"skilMeaming\" (skills/learning).\n\n**Lint output:**\n```\n(no lint lines for the 19 batch-5a files)\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\nThe one remaining issue is in `_book.md`, which you own, not in my files.\n\nAll 19 entries are in `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`. Page renders are in `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_renders/` (5a_p266.png to 5a_p278.png).</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>281630</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>72</tool_uses><duration_ms>1077906</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
