{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:14:17.745Z","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a4980461b059c604c</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01StygRvyFAB9ttgQW6L57hD</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/a4980461b059c604c.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review Supertraining 5b round 1\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>The review is written: **GO AFTER FIXES**, with 17 GO, 13 FIX and 0 NO-GO across the 30 entries.\n\nI checked every page from PDF 278 to 298 against the rendered page image, not the text layer.\n\n- **Prescription numbers:** all correct as printed, including the drop heights, contact times, sets, reps and rest (\"1-15\" min is printed that way), the weekly frequency, the \"475 jumps\" and \"1472 push-offs\" counts and the \"28 repetitions per minute\".\n- **Physics:** both readings are right and labelled Inference. 21.693 = 9.8 × √4.9, so the Lewis formula is body weight times the mean free-fall speed through the jump height. Contact momentum m·√(2gh) rises by √2 (about 1.41 times) when the drop height doubles.\n- **Confidence ratings:** no entry is rated `proven`. The 4 `untested` ratings are right, not too high.\n- **The 8 `contested` entries:** 5 are real contradictions. Two are not and should be `supported`: surface-footwear (the book places sand next to its soft-surface warning on purpose) and high-volume (its conflict is about a neighbouring sentence). One overstates its conflict: in the strength-endurance ceiling entry, the rowers' 50-80% is not a clash; only the skaters' 80-85% is.\n- **Duplicates:** none. One link to batch 5c is missing (see fix 4).\n\n**Required fixes, one line:**\n1. high-volume and surface-footwear: change `contested` to `supported`.\n2. strength-endurance ceiling: drop rowers from the conflict, and fix the glycolytic inference. Baseline line 117 says staff do program glycolytic work off-season.\n3. brief-maximal-tension: add the line-76 DSI tag verbatim (the 2026-09-29 evidence-check wording: contested, Pleša 2024 supported only the strength arm, no football data). Fix its \"jumping from depth\" Example, which is not in the source.\n4. 475-jumps and brief-maximal-tension: note that the novice experiments in batch 5c (PDF 299) found exercise order made no difference, and link that 5c entry.\n5. depth-jump-height: mRSI is Eric's daily readiness marker (line 74), not a reactive-ability read. Add the Ch4 p.222 locator for \"considerably lower\".\n6. volume-and-rest: Fig 5.11 shows ground jumps with no box, so the \"sets of 10, 1-15 min\" dose is not a depth-jump dose. Label Siff's text and Verkhoshansky's 1977 list separately.\n7. five-phases: the EM-delay \"conflict\" is only a wording difference.\n8. classification and momentum-intensity: the \"powermetrics\" passages are Siff's voice, not \"the authors'\".\n9. Lewis: the title states an Inference as fact. Mark baseline line 77 as Inferred.\n10. injury-risk: the right margin of PDF 281 is cut, so quote \"adequ[ate]\" and \"running a[nd] jumping\"; \"or\" is not on the page.\n11. resisted-water: quote the printed double negative \"No resistance methods should not use\" as it stands.\n\nOne thing to check by hand: in the prestretch entry, the Fig 5.6 caption says Wilson et al. 1991 while the text says 1990. I recall the bench-press delay study as Wilson, Elliott &amp; Wood, *Med Sci Sports Exerc* 1991, which would make the caption right. I did not verify that.\n\nThe report also lists 5 missing ideas and per-entry corrections.\n\nFile: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5b_opus.md`</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>303257</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>42</tool_uses><duration_ms>563492</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
