{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:22:13.241Z","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ad4688aeb65d86edb</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_016Eyasnu1hAX3AsJENA8rYL</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/ad4688aeb65d86edb.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review Supertraining 5c round 1\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Overall verdict: GO AFTER FIXES.** Of the 26 entries, 15 are GO, 11 need fixes, and none is a NO-GO. The report is at `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5c_opus.md`, with one row per entry. I opened every page I checked as an image myself, including 303, 314 and 318. All 74 links resolve and every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote matches `method-baseline.md`.\n\n**Required fixes:**\n1. **Deniskin (Fig 5.14):** the entry says Ss and Se rise through stages 1-3. In the image both fall during stage 3, and Se also ends lower at the stage-4 test.\n2. **Junior sprinters (Fig 5.12):** several bars are misread. The 30 m low-start combined bar is about 0.25 s, not 0.29. At 60 m low start the bars are about 0.27 / 0.20 / 0.29, and the 100 m long-jump bar is about 0.48. With these values the \"contested\" call still holds and gets stronger: the combined group exceeds the sum of the two single groups only at 100 m.\n3. **Novice sequencing:** drop the \"mixed sessions\" contradiction. Page 299 and page 300 compare mixed sessions against different groups, so the two statements are compatible. Add the real conflict it missed: page 288 says resistance work should come before plyometrics in the same session.\n4. **Excess heavy strength:** the Inference \"about volume, not intensity\" is wrong. Page 302 also blames \"excessively heavy\" and \"excessive maximum strength\" work.\n5. **Traditional circuit claim:** \"10-15 reps at over 80% is not possible for most lifters\" is the writer's judgment and needs an \"Inference:\" label.\n6. **Aerobic vs anaerobic circuits:** narrow \"contested\" to the one real conflict, 3-8 reps at over 70% versus about 50 reps at 50% within the same anaerobic category. The page 317 scheme is for a different goal, so it is not a contradiction.\n7. **Layout variants:** label the superset-planning sentence as \"Inference:\".\n8. **Allow restoration:** the error about the 4-12 weeks belongs to Bompa &amp; Haff's book, not just the vault entry (see below). Also add a link to the existing 4.1 entry on the delay before strength gains appear.\n9. **Sequence jumps, barbell, depth jumps:** drop the same mixed-sessions strand. Add the real conflict: rule 1 against page 299's \"no real significance for novices\" and against page 288.\n10. **Accelerating specific adaptation:** change `contested` to `supported`. Two definitions of one term that the entry itself says \"can coexist\" is ambiguity, not a disagreement.\n11. **Sequentially introduce means:** the \"experimental assessment\" locator should be PDF 308 / p.297, not PDF 299.\n\n**The other contested entries hold:**\n- **Conjugate sequence and concentrated-loading conditions:** both state Issurin 2016 accurately. The vault brief (pp. 6-7, 11) aims the critique at Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's own A-T-R model.\n- **Circuits cannot build sport-specific strength:** both sides are on the cited pages, and I confirmed the Ch1 quote at PDF 91.\n- **Voice:** the two passages naming Verkhoshansky in the third person (PDF 302 and 310) are correctly attributed to Siff.\n- **Circuit load numbers (PDF 313, 315, 317, 322-323):** every value matches the page images.\n\n**Bompa finding:** the attribution is wrong as a claim about when gains appear, but the error starts in Bompa &amp; Haff's book, not in the vault entry.\n- **What Bompa &amp; Haff write:** the 5th edition (PDF idx 61 in the Drive copy) says \"Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase.\" The vault entry repeats this faithfully, then turns it into a 4-12 week delay before performance lifts.\n- **What Supertraining says:**\n  - The wait before gains appear is 1-3 weeks (PDF 305).\n  - 4-12 weeks is how long the delayed training effect lasts (PDF 375). It starts when the block ends: Fig 6.31 shows gains rising right after the block.\n  - 4-12 weeks is also the length of the training stages themselves (PDF 305, 381, 536).\n- **Caveat:** I could not find Bompa &amp; Haff's reference 83 in the Drive copy.\n- **Suggested wording for the vault entry, for you to decide:** \"Bompa &amp; Haff cite Siff and Verkhoshansky for gains 4 to 12 weeks after the block; Supertraining itself gives a 1-3 week latency and 4-12 weeks as the duration of the delayed effect (PDF 305, 375).\"</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>311513</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>66</tool_uses><duration_ms>807652</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
