{"parentUuid":"6aed8cd0-8ab2-46a9-953e-1b2bb5084685","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0b81aa46-3ac1-41a7-bee9-ff3aba05ec92","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ld4pVWtDC7y7yj9qdnXbeZ","type":"tool_result","content":"22:| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md | FIX | Title, When to apply and the Bondarchuk framing are fixed and correct (Bondarchuk sentence confirmed verbatim in the linked vault entry `heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning`, line 23). Fixer disagreements 1 and 2 are justified by the page: Egstrom transfer is of \"precision\", and shot transfer \"apparently is possible only by alternating\" (PDF 276-277 images), so \"Can Run One Way\" is the right strength; water polo 2 kg beat 4 kg and 4 kg \"is detrimental to technique\" (PDF 276 image), and both balls are far above a match ball. **New error introduced by the round-1 wording and copied by the fixer:** When NOT says \"only the shot and baseball data fit it\" (a stay-close-to-competition-mass rule). The page gives the baseball masses: \"light (57 grams) and a heavy (184 gm) baseball ... a regulation ball is about 148 gm\" (PDF 276 / p.265, image). The heavy ball is +24% of regulation, the light ball is -61%, which is as far from competition mass as the counterexamples the same sentence lists; and Egstrom measured precision transfer, not the effect of closeness. These masses are on the page, so this is not outside-book knowledge. | In When NOT, replace \"only the shot and baseball data fit it, and the water polo result\" with \"only the shot data fit it (250 g vs 500 g differences); in the baseball study the heavy ball (184 g) is close to the regulation 148 g but the light ball (57 g) is far below it, and that study measured precision transfer, not closeness; the water polo result\". Keep the rest of the sentence. |\n23-\n24-## Part 2: spot checks of round-1 GO entries (6 of 11)\n25-\n26-| file | verdict | checked |\n27-|---|---|---|\n28-| principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md | GO | \"totally independent qualities\", \"does not necessarily enhance any of these three qualities\", \"(Verkhoshansky, 1963)\", \"inversely proportional to one another (see Ch 4)\", \"one of these qualities achieves greater development\" at PDF 268 / p.257 (image); baseline line 101 quote; Example labelled Inference. |\n--\n42:- Old slugs `claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves` and `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass`: no match in `staging/library`, the vault library, or any staging scope file. The only staging hits are the round-1 review and the fix log, which is expected. Both new files exist; the scope file lists them.\n43-\n44-## Part 4: format, all 19 files\n45-\n46-Scripted check: each of the six vault-form headers (`**What it is** —` through `**Related** —`) appears exactly once in every file; no em dash outside those headers (titles included); no `[[` without its `]]` on the same line; no review-history wording (searched for \"prior version\", \"previous version\", \"round\", \"corrected from\", \"renamed\", \"NO-GO finding\", \"fixed per\", \"earlier draft\"). All 19 pass.\n47-\n48-## Missing\n49-\n50-Nothing new. Both round-1 gaps are now filled (1-3 reps sentence in Berger; two-lever summary in the loading entry).\n51-\n52-## Overall verdict\n--\n54-**GO AFTER FIXES.** One required fix:\n--\n56:1. `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md`, When NOT: replace \"only the shot and baseball data fit it\" as given above (the 57 g light baseball is -61% of the 148 g regulation ball on PDF 276; Egstrom measured precision).\n57-\n58-The other 18 entries can be promoted as they stand. The single `proven` entry keeps its identity-only scope; both `contested` entries keep both sides on the cited pages. Optional nits (non-blocking): the Newton closing list should include (a); the Berger traceability sentence could carry \"Inference:\"; the loading entry's \"closes on two levers\" could read \"summary names two levers\".\n20:**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). Inference: for throwing and striking positions (quarterbacks, baseball and softball), these findings argue for overweight and underweight implements chosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete. Inference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight; his correlation data: \"The greatest amounts of correlational interrelationships were most frequently found in cases where the deviations between the lighter and heavier implement weights was insignificant\" ([[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning]]).\n22:**When NOT to apply** — The shot finding is muddy as printed: \"A statistically significant difference was not found in the mean results,\" and the 250 g advantage is described only as distances \"close to the mean,\" yet the authors conclude transfer is \"possible only\" by alternating masses. Treat that conclusion as stronger than the data shown. The baseball result concerns precision, not velocity. No group sizes or training durations are given for any study. The 2 kg table-tennis bat is an anecdote from coaches, with the authors' own warning against copying it. Inference: several of the book's own examples are far from competition mass (a 3 kg javelin training weight; a 0.6-0.8 kg puck; 2 kg bats), so the page does not support a \"stay close to competition mass\" rule; only the shot and baseball data fit it, and the water polo result fits only in rank order (both medicine balls are far heavier than a match ball, and the lighter one did better).","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"46019d12-e9a8-4cd3-b0cd-8a7f78f7186c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:25:47.646Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"22:| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md | FIX | Title, When to apply and the Bondarchuk framing are fixed and correct (Bondarchuk sentence confirmed verbatim in the linked vault entry `heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning`, line 23). Fixer disagreements 1 and 2 are justified by the page: Egstrom transfer is of \"precision\", and shot transfer \"apparently is possible only by alternating\" (PDF 276-277 images), so \"Can Run One Way\" is the right strength; water polo 2 kg beat 4 kg and 4 kg \"is detrimental to technique\" (PDF 276 image), and both balls are far above a match ball. **New error introduced by the round-1 wording and copied by the fixer:** When NOT says \"only the shot and baseball data fit it\" (a stay-close-to-competition-mass rule). The page gives the baseball masses: \"light (57 grams) and a heavy (184 gm) baseball ... a regulation ball is about 148 gm\" (PDF 276 / p.265, image). The heavy ball is +24% of regulation, the light ball is -61%, which is as far from competition mass as the counterexamples the same sentence lists; and Egstrom measured precision transfer, not the effect of closeness. These masses are on the page, so this is not outside-book knowledge. | In When NOT, replace \"only the shot and baseball data fit it, and the water polo result\" with \"only the shot data fit it (250 g vs 500 g differences); in the baseball study the heavy ball (184 g) is close to the regulation 148 g but the light ball (57 g) is far below it, and that study measured precision transfer, not closeness; the water polo result\". Keep the rest of the sentence. |\n23-\n24-## Part 2: spot checks of round-1 GO entries (6 of 11)\n25-\n26-| file | verdict | checked |\n27-|---|---|---|\n28-| principle--speed-strength-strength-endu