{"parentUuid":"501d7c59-02cc-423e-bf50-24532f63c2fa","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffcgXUzsuf5oPPV6LK6b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The page gives the 250 g shot difference but has no 500 g figure. So I am leaving out the reviewer's \"500 g\" and keeping only what the page says."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":697,"cache_read_input_tokens":243174,"output_tokens":982,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":137},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":697,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":982,"cache_read_input_tokens":243174,"cache_creation_input_tokens":697,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":697},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffcgW6tZ6KhWArQPJdrX","type":"assistant","uuid":"d0d6a9ea-05b0-4168-beba-08faeb07c14c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:25:57.546Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d0d6a9ea-05b0-4168-beba-08faeb07c14c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffcgXUzsuf5oPPV6LK6b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PvPSm71gxJEsHLHToEoTyT","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md","old_string":"only the shot and baseball data fit it, and the water polo result","new_string":"only the shot data fit it (shots \"differing in mass by 250 grams\", PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265); in the baseball study the heavy ball (184 g) is close to the regulation 148 g but the light ball (57 g) is far below it, and that study measured precision transfer, not closeness; the water polo result"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":697,"cache_read_input_tokens":243174,"output_tokens":982,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":137},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":697,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":982,"cache_read_input_tokens":243174,"cache_creation_input_tokens":697,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":697},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01PvPSm71gxJEsHLHToEoTyT":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md","old_string":"only the shot and baseball data fit it, and the water polo result","new_string":"only the shot data fit it (shots \"differing in mass by 250 grams\", PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265); in the baseball study the heavy ball (184 g) is close to the regulation 148 g but the light ball (57 g) is far below it, and that study measured precision transfer, not closeness; the water polo result"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffcgW6tZ6KhWArQPJdrX","type":"assistant","uuid":"ecb81651-4e49-4487-bbb1-d95088e674b0","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:25:59.372Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"ecb81651-4e49-4487-bbb1-d95088e674b0","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0b81aa46-3ac1-41a7-bee9-ff3aba05ec92","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PvPSm71gxJEsHLHToEoTyT","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"e831121a-1ff0-4402-aa77-d79d2b031d06","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:25:59.495Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md","oldString":"only the shot and baseball data fit it, and the water polo result","newString":"only the shot data fit it (shots \"differing in mass by 250 grams\", PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265); in the baseball study the heavy ball (184 g) is close to the regulation 148 g but the light ball (57 g) is far below it, and that study measured precision transfer, not closeness; the water polo result","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.3\"\ntopic: lighter and heavier implements\ncategory: [speed-strength, throwing, transfer]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Choose a Mass That Keeps Technique, Size the Heavy One per Athlete, and Transfer Can Run One Way\n\n**What it is** — For acyclic speed-strength the authors say \"plyometric or throwing exercises should be selected on the basis of their relevance to the sports activity,\" and give a run of implement findings (PDF p. 276-277 / printed p. 265-266, verified against the page images). Water polo throw: a 2 kg medicine ball beat a 4 kg ball, throwing distance up \"13.6% and 8.9%, respectively,\" and \"throwing a 4 kg ball is detrimental to technique (Rogener, 1961).\" Baseball: a light \"57 grams\" and heavy \"184 gm\" ball both improved throwing (regulation \"about 148 gm\"), but \"throwing the light ball improves precision for throwing the heavier ball, but the reverse effect does not occur (Egstrom et al, 1960).\" Javelin: \"The optimal weight for javelin training which does not disrupt technique is 3 kg (Matveyev, 1967).\" Shot: alternating shots \"differing in mass by 250 grams\" gave distances \"close to the mean,\" but \"this closeness to the average was not achieved with a difference of 500 grams (Ivanova, 1964; Vasiliev, 1975),\" so \"the transference of throwing speeds from a lighter to a heavier mass ... apparently is possible only by alternating the throwing of shots of different mass.\" Ice hockey: hitting a normal then a heavier puck confirmed the variable method; the heavier puck's optimal mass \"was found to be between 0.6-0.8 kg,\" selected \"for each player individually, based on the athlete's ability to hit the heavier puck efficiently (Savin, 1974).\" Table tennis: some Eastern European and Chinese players train with bats up to \"2kg,\" against typical bats of \"about 100 grams,\" only \"carefully balanced,\" by \"elite athletes about once a week before the competition phase\"; the authors warn this \"should not be emulated by less experienced coaches,\" because untutored use \"can lead to overload injuries and altered neural control patterns.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that too heavy an implement disrupts technique; they give no further mechanism. Inference: the Egstrom result (light-to-heavy transfer of precision, not the reverse) is consistent with the book's Ch 1 finding that high-velocity training transfers to low velocity better than the reverse, but the authors do not draw that link here.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). Inference: for throwing and striking positions (quarterbacks, baseball and softball), these findings argue for overweight and underweight implements chosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete. Inference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight; his correlation data: \"The greatest amounts of correlational interrelationships were most frequently found in cases where the deviations between the lighter and heavier implement weights was insignificant\" ([[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning]]).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The shot finding is muddy as printed: \"A statistically significant difference was not found in the mean results,\" and the 250 g advantage is described only as distances \"close to the mean,\" yet the authors conclude transfer is \"possible only\" by alternating masses. Treat that conclusion as stronger than the data shown. The baseball result concerns precision, not velocity. No group sizes or training durations are given for any study. The 2 kg table-tennis bat is an anecdote from coaches, with the authors' own warning against copying it. Inference: several of the book's own examples are far from competition mass (a 3 kg javelin training weight; a 0.6-0.8 kg puck; 2 kg bats), so the page does not support a \"stay close to competition mass\" rule; only the shot and baseball data fit it, and the water polo result fits only in rank order (both medicine balls are far heavier than a match ball, and the lighter one did better).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example: water polo players gained more throwing distance with a 2 kg medicine ball (13.6%) than with a 4 kg ball (8.9%), and the 4 kg ball harmed technique (Rogener 1961; PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265).\n\n**Related** — [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning]] (Bondarchuk's position: close to competition weight transfers best; this page does not test that); [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--throws-implement-weight-transfer-near-universal]]; [[claim--velocity-and-contraction-specificity-asymmetric-transfer]]; [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]]; [[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Eric's baseline: Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). Inference: for throwing and striking positions (quarterbacks, baseball and softball), these findings argue for overweight and underweight implements chosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete. Inference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight; his correlation data: \"The greatest amounts of correlational interrelationships were most frequently found in cases where the deviations between the lighter and heavier implement weights was insignificant\" ([[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning]])."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — The shot finding is muddy as printed: \"A statistically significant difference was not found in the mean results,\" and the 250 g advantage is described only as distances \"close to the mean,\" yet the authors conclude transfer is \"possible only\" by alternating masses. Treat that conclusion as stronger than the data shown. The baseball result concerns precision, not velocity. No group sizes or training durations are given for any study. The 2 kg table-tennis bat is an anecdote from coaches, with the authors' own warning against copying it. Inference: several of the book's own examples are far from competition mass (a 3 kg javelin training weight; a 0.6-0.8 kg puck; 2 kg bats), so the page does not support a \"stay close to competition mass\" rule; only the shot and baseball data fit it, and the water polo result fits only in rank order (both medicine balls are far heavier than a match ball, and the lighter one did better).","+**When NOT to apply** — The shot finding is muddy as printed: \"A statistically significant difference was not found in the mean results,\" and the 250 g advantage is described only as distances \"close to the mean,\" yet the authors conclude transfer is \"possible only\" by alternating masses. Treat that conclusion as stronger than the data shown. The baseball result concerns precision, not velocity. No group sizes or training durations are given for any study. The 2 kg table-tennis bat is an anecdote from coaches, with the authors' own warning against copying it. Inference: several of the book's own examples are far from competition mass (a 3 kg javelin training weight; a 0.6-0.8 kg puck; 2 kg bats), so the page does not support a \"stay close to competition mass\" rule; only the shot data fit it (shots \"differing in mass by 250 grams\", PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265); in the baseball study the heavy ball (184 g) is close to the regulation 148 g but the light ball (57 g) is far below it, and that study measured precision transfer, not closeness; the water polo result fits only in rank order (both medicine balls are far heavier than a match ball, and the lighter one did better)."," "," **Example** — The authors' own example: water polo players gained more throwing distance with a 2 kg medicine ball (13.6%) than with a 4 kg ball (8.9%), and the 4 kg ball harmed technique (Rogener 1961; PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ecb81651-4e49-4487-bbb1-d95088e674b0","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
