{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:26:02.960Z","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a49548ff867d262cc</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01N7tf3UoF7fPauyWUs2fhXF</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/a49548ff867d262cc.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Fix Supertraining 5b entries\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I applied all 12 numbered fixes and checked each one against the page image first. In four places I narrowed the review's wording rather than copying it. No new entries, no renames, nothing outside the 5b files. Lint is clean apart from the known `_book.md` false positive.\n\nI edited the 13 flagged entries plus the prestretch entry (for the Wilson note). I made no edits to `_book.md`, 5a or 5c files, or the vault, and ran no git. The scope file is unchanged at 30 entries. The log is at `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch5b_applied.md`.\n\n**Per-fix outcome**\n1. **High-volume plyometrics:** applied. Rating changed from contested to supported; the conflict now points to the brief-maximal-tension entry; baseline line 122 tagged Explicit.\n2. **Surface and footwear:** applied. Contested to supported; the \"tension\" is now a labelled Inference that the book gives different surfaces for different aims.\n3. **Strength-endurance ceiling:** applied. Rowers taken out of the conflict, skaters kept. The glycolytic point now uses baseline line 116 (in-season) and line 117 (Q19, staff do program it off-season).\n4. **Brief maximal tension:** applied, with the novice caveat narrowed.\n   - Line 76 now carries its DSI tag verbatim.\n   - The Example now quotes the p.278 wording instead of \"jumping from depth\".\n   - The novice caveat names the within-workout experiment on PDF 299 (no significant difference). It also notes the across-stage experiment on the same page, where weights first did work better, and quotes the authors' \"careful experimentation\" hedge.\n   - The 5c novice entry is now linked.\n5. **475 jumps:** applied the same way. Title now says \"93 Tons\"; caveat and link added.\n6. **Depth-jump height:** applied. The mRSI sentence is corrected to the readiness marker (line 74); \"considerably lower\" is now quoted in full with its locator (PDF 233 / printed p.222).\n7. **Volume and rest:** applied, with two changes from the review.\n   - Item 1 is moved out of Model 2. That it covers only the Fig 5.11 ground jumps is labelled Inference, because \"each exercise\" can be read either way.\n   - Voices are labelled per item, not per model: Siff for the powermetrics passages, \"the authors\" for the PDF 285 prescription items.\n   - Line 105 now shows its Inferred tag, and line 186 is added.\n8. **Five phases:** applied. The EM-delay \"conflict\" is now a wording difference, labelled Inference.\n9. **Classification and momentum entries:** applied. The powermetrics passages are now in Siff's voice; prescription items 1 and 4 stay \"the authors'\".\n10. **Lewis formula:** applied. The physics reading is labelled Inference in the title, and line 77 carries its full tag, \"Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)\".\n11. **Injury risk:** applied. \"adequ[ate]\" and \"running a[nd] jumping\" are quoted as printed; the margin cut is confirmed on the image.\n12. **Resisted and water:** applied. The double negative is quoted as printed with \"sic\", and the intended \"should not\" is labelled Inference.\n\nI also tagged every baseline line cited in the edited files, since the brief requires it.\n\n**Missing ideas** (all placed in flagged entries, no new entry):\n1. Low vs high inertia went into the classification entry. The Ch1 equal-force entry doesn't hold this split, so I linked it.\n2. Strength-endurance specificity went into the ceiling entry, linked to the Ch3.2.2 entry that has the Ch3 version.\n3. The Fig 5.9 catalogue went into the momentum-intensity entry, described from the image and marked approximate.\n4. Upper-body transfer was not added. The preparatory-progression entry already quotes that sentence, so I recorded it as a disagreement.\n5. \"Avoid the term drills\" went into the injury-risk entry, in Siff's voice.\n\n**Wilson:** confirmed. Zotero failed (\"MCP tenant gate is not configured\"), so I used the journal's own abstract page. The paper is Wilson, Elliott &amp; Wood, *Med Sci Sports Exerc* 1991;23(3):364-370: 12 lifters, bench press at 95%, half-life 0.85 s. It's now a source note in the prestretch entry. The match to Fig 5.6, which suggests the caption's 1991 is right and the text's 1990 wrong, is labelled Inference. The abstract does not give the 4 s figure, and I left the 0.37 s Wilson 1991 result unchecked. The entry stays contested.\n\n**Renames:** none.\n\n**For you to pass on:**\n- The 5c novice entry should link back to the 475-jumps and brief-maximal-tension entries. I didn't edit 5c.\n- Confidence counts are now 6 contested, 20 supported, 4 untested; contested was 8.\n\n**Grep proof**\n```\nclaim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md:9:confidence: supported\nclaim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md:20:that conflict concerns explosive strength, not this entry's Nicol et al (1997) claim\nheuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md:9:confidence: supported\nheuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md:22:Inference: these are different aims, not a contradiction\nheuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md:24:sits at or under the ceiling, so it is not part of the conflict\nheuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md:28:to-fatigue strength-endurance sets fit the off-season, not in-season weeks\nheuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md:19:expressed to a lesser degree than the specificity of speed\nheuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md:27:one controlled trial (Pleša 2024, basketball, split at 0.90) supported only the strength arm; no football data]\nheuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md:31:The passage names no drill for the second case\nheuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md:29:The resistance-first rule for combined sessions is not supported for novices\nclaim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md:14:93 Tons\nclaim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md:24:The resistance-first rule is untested for novices\nheuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact.md:26:the baseline has no drop-jump test\nheuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact.md:28:(Ch4.2.3, PDF p. 233 / printed p. 222\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md:29:Depth-jump item 1, kept out of Model 2\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md:18:are Siff's text\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md:35:line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\nframework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action.md:25:Inference: both describe the electrical-to-mechanical interval\nframework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md:16:The text (Siff's classification; it uses his term \"powermetrics\"\nframework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md:24:Explosive, low inertia training targets involuntary\nheuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md:16:The text (Siff, using his term \"powermetrics\"\nheuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md:20:The prescription list is introduced by Fig 5.9\nclaim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height.md:14:(Inference: It Is Weight Times Mean Free-Fall Speed, Not Take-Off Power)\nclaim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height.md:20:Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)\nclaim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md:21:adequ[ate] restoration regimes\nclaim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md:21:running a[nd] jumping\nclaim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md:22:militarism and authoritarianism\nheuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics.md:18:\"No resistance methods should not use heavy weights\nclaim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md:27:Source note: Wilson, G.J., Elliott, B.C. &amp; Wood, G.A. (1991)\n```\nNone of the replaced phrases remain in any 5b file. Across all 30 files: no em dashes in titles or body, no review-history words, no wrapped wikilinks, no CR line endings, and all six section headers present in each.\n\n**Lint**\n```\n$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\nThe one issue is the same `_book.md` false positive as in the Ch4-3-4 run, outside these edits.\n\nWilson source: [MSSE abstract (LWW/Ovid)](https://journals.lww.com/acsm-msse/Abstract/1991/03000/The_effect_on_performance_of_imposing_a_delay.16.aspx)</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>216098</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>56</tool_uses><duration_ms>689073</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
