{"parentUuid":"a959031b-f39e-42c7-a413-8a080ba62048","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"76867d42-750c-42f2-91fa-52b829c9c6cc","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HuviWKXm5yshhShfMiTuni","type":"tool_result","content":"24:**When NOT to apply** — Unclear points in the source: \"all of them did 475 jumps\" does not say whether 475 is per athlete or the group total; the experiment has no citation, group sizes, statistics or outcome test named beyond \"reactive ability\"; the comparison group's \"1472 general push-offs\" is also unclear per athlete or total. Supported only as a reported Soviet-era comparison. The result is for track-and-field jumpers in a preparation phase. The resistance-first rule is untested for novices: in a within-workout experiment on novices (strength then speed-strength work versus the reverse), \"There was no statistically significant difference in speed-strength of the subjects in the first two groups\" (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288; see [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]). The authors expect the strength-first effect to hold for well-trained athletes, but say \"the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation\" (same page).\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md:35:**When to apply** — Inference: one way to use both is to treat Model 1 as the rule for truly maximal efforts (maximal height, rebound to a marker, near-maximal tension) and Model 2 as an upper volume cap for depth-jump sessions, keeping per-session counts at or under 2-3 × 5-8 for less-conditioned athletes and 4 × 10 for well-conditioned ones. The book does not make this split. For any athlete, the authors' quality rule holds: \"It is the quality of the plyometric exercises, not the quantity, which is important\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 11). Eric's baseline: in-season \"the stated rule is 'maintain intensity, reduce volume' rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Eric's baseline: \"lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game\" (line 186, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)). Inference: Model 1's low-rep, long-rest structure fits the line-105 rule more directly than Model 2's sets of 10, and any maximal depth-jump session in season falls before that 72-hour cut-off.\nheuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement.md:26:**When to apply** — Placing depth jumps in a football week or year. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\"\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105). Inference: the authors' in-season rule (one exposure every 10-14 days, none in the 10 days before a game) is far sparser than a weekly game schedule allows; read literally, a team playing every 7 days would have no legal in-season depth-jump day. That is a reason to treat the 10-day rule as written for track-and-field competition calendars, which the book does not say.\n- Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work. In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule. **Inferred (transcript + shared slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.20-21; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.21] Not claimed in the interview. The in-season dose itself is unknown: Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q18): \"I don't have the specific sets and reps.\"","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"18172bd2-eda6-4176-bdea-c6cba42ccb85","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:35:24.744Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"24:**When NOT to apply** — Unclear points in the source: \"all of them did 475 jumps\" does not say whether 475 is per athlete or the group total; the experiment has no citation, group sizes, statistics or outcome test named beyond \"reactive ability\"; the comparison group's \"1472 general push-offs\" is also unclear per athlete or total. Supported only as a reported Soviet-era comparison. The result is for track-and-field jumpers in a preparation phase. The resistance-first rule is untested for novices: in a within-workout experiment on novices (strength then speed-strength work versus the reverse), \"There was no statistically significant difference in speed-strength of the subjects in the first two groups\" (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288; see [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]). The authors expect the strength-first effect to hold for well-trained athletes, but say \"the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation\" (same page).\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md:35:**When to apply** — Inference: one way to use both is to treat Model 1 as the rule for truly maximal efforts (maximal height, rebound to a marker, near-maximal tension) and Model 2 as an upper volume cap for depth-jump sessions, keeping per-session counts at or under 2-3 × 5-8 for less-conditioned athletes and 4 × 10 for well-conditioned ones. The book does not make this split. For any athlete, the authors' quality rule holds: \"It is the quality of the plyometric exercises, not the quantity, which is important\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 11). Eric's baseline: in-season \"the stated rule is 'maintain intensity, reduce volume' rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Eric's baseline: \"lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game\" (line 186, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)). Inference: Model 1's low-rep, long-rest structure fits the line-105 rule more directly than Model 2's sets of 10, and any maximal depth-jump session in season falls before that 72-hour cut-off.\nheuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement.md:26:**When to apply** — Placing depth jumps in a football week or year. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\"\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105). Inference: the authors' in-season rule (one exposure every 10-14 days, none in the 10 days before a game) is far sparser than a weekly game schedule allows; read literally, a team playing every 7 days would have no legal in-season depth-jump day. That is a reason to treat the 10-day rule as written for track-and-field competition calendars, which the book does not say.\n- Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work. In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule. **Inferred (transcript + shared slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.20-21; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.21] Not claimed in the interview. The in-season dose itself is unknown: Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q18): \"I don't have the specific sets and reps.\"","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a959031b-f39e-42c7-a413-8a080ba62048","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
