{"parentUuid":"2cc541b0-f1f7-4805-a539-1c6bafff1f7d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"76867d42-750c-42f2-91fa-52b829c9c6cc","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HiishF4Vv3As9DX6sk7397","type":"tool_result","content":"ok claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md\nok heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement.md\n1\n1","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"8136eb9c-3516-45fd-b396-c59594c90aa9","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:35:31.394Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"ok claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md\nok heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement.md\n1\n1","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch5b_applied.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":139,"oldLines":3,"newStart":139,"newLines":9,"lines":[" ```"," "," The one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes), outside my edits and outside scope.","+","+## Coordinator fixes after round 2","+","+Round 2 (`review_supertraining_ch5b_opus_round2.md`): GO AFTER FIXES, 28 GO / 2 FIX. Both applied:","+1. `claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md`: \"The resistance-first rule is untested for novices\" changed to \"not supported for novices\" (the PDF 299 experiment tested it on novices and found no significant difference).","+2. `heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement.md`: the line-105 baseline quote now carries its tag, \"Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\", plus \"Not claimed in the interview\" (checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` line 105)."]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":21,"oldLines":7,"newStart":21,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Making the case to coaches that a small, well-placed depth-jump dose can do more for reactive ability than a large barbell volume. Inference: the companion sentence (resistance work before plyometrics in the same session) is a direct sequencing rule for combined sessions (for novices, see When NOT to apply)."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Unclear points in the source: \"all of them did 475 jumps\" does not say whether 475 is per athlete or the group total; the experiment has no citation, group sizes, statistics or outcome test named beyond \"reactive ability\"; the comparison group's \"1472 general push-offs\" is also unclear per athlete or total. Supported only as a reported Soviet-era comparison. The result is for track-and-field jumpers in a preparation phase. The resistance-first rule is untested for novices: in a within-workout experiment on novices (strength then speed-strength work versus the reverse), \"There was no statistically significant difference in speed-strength of the subjects in the first two groups\" (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288; see [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]). The authors expect the strength-first effect to hold for well-trained athletes, but say \"the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation\" (same page).","+**When NOT to apply** — Unclear points in the source: \"all of them did 475 jumps\" does not say whether 475 is per athlete or the group total; the experiment has no citation, group sizes, statistics or outcome test named beyond \"reactive ability\"; the comparison group's \"1472 general push-offs\" is also unclear per athlete or total. Supported only as a reported Soviet-era comparison. The result is for track-and-field jumpers in a preparation phase. The resistance-first rule is not supported for novices: in a within-workout experiment on novices (strength then speed-strength work versus the reverse), \"There was no statistically significant difference in speed-strength of the subjects in the first two groups\" (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288; see [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]). The authors expect the strength-first effect to hold for well-trained athletes, but say \"the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation\" (same page)."," "," **Example** — The authors' own experiment as quoted above (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277)."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":23,"oldLines":7,"newStart":23,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors' stated reasons: strong excitation of the nervous system (item 4) and a 5-6 day delay of the training effect of prior strength work (item 15). Neither cites a study. Inference: the in-season 10-day cut-off before competition is consistent with item 15's 5-6 day delayed effect plus a margin; the book does not make that link."," ","-**When to apply** — Placing depth jumps in a football week or year. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\"\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105). Inference: the authors' in-season rule (one exposure every 10-14 days, none in the 10 days before a game) is far sparser than a weekly game schedule allows; read literally, a team playing every 7 days would have no legal in-season depth-jump day. That is a reason to treat the 10-day rule as written for track-and-field competition calendars, which the book does not say.","+**When to apply** — Placing depth jumps in a football week or year. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\"\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: the authors' in-season rule (one exposure every 10-14 days, none in the 10 days before a game) is far sparser than a weekly game schedule allows; read literally, a team playing every 7 days would have no legal in-season depth-jump day. That is a reason to treat the 10-day rule as written for track-and-field competition calendars, which the book does not say."," "," **When NOT to apply** — These are experience-based rules with no population, drop height or study given. They are written for an annual cycle with a long preparatory period and spaced competitions (the authors' examples elsewhere in 5.2.4 are track-and-field jumpers). Do not quote the 3-4 day spacing without the item 3 exception. The 5-6 day delay applies to depth jumps used \"as a means of stimulation\"; the book does not say how it scales with volume."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch5b_applied.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2cc541b0-f1f7-4805-a539-1c6bafff1f7d","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
