{"parentUuid":"66add6a2-e9d8-4743-8e39-5759cc34c616","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffda2wU4cWQeA8f5V9Z5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01H22Fb1Y5npa9yx7vGWodre","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging && grep -n \"allow-restoration\" review_supertraining_ch5c_opus_round2.md | head -3; grep -n \"Bompa\" library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md","description":"Find correction text and current Bompa lines"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1982,"cache_read_input_tokens":267987,"output_tokens":273,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":24},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1982,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":273,"cache_read_input_tokens":267987,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1982,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1982},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01H22Fb1Y5npa9yx7vGWodre":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging && grep -n \"allow-restoration\" review_supertraining_ch5c_opus_round2.md | head -3; grep -n \"Bompa\" library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md","description":"Find correction text and current Bompa lines"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffda2SCqCFvzVmpg4QcJ","type":"assistant","uuid":"e0246076-0b42-4ec1-974a-529d7f5dee69","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:37:36.557Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"e0246076-0b42-4ec1-974a-529d7f5dee69","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"33e25c8a-8e1f-4b6c-9ddd-e59e1af1c598","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01H22Fb1Y5npa9yx7vGWodre","type":"tool_result","content":"25:| heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md | FIX | (a) **Locator wrong.** Entry: \"(Bompa & Haff 5e, PDF idx 61 of the Drive copy)\". The quote is at 0-based PDF idx 60 / printed p. 49 (viewer page 61); idx 61 / printed p. 50 has only a second, generic Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) citation. The quote wording itself is verbatim. (b) **Unlabelled judgment and over-strong claim.** \"That matches neither Supertraining statement\" is the writer's reasoning and is unlabelled. It is also only true on one reading: Bompa & Haff's \"performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation\" can mean a 4-12 week delay (how the vault entry reads it) or gains appearing within the 4-12 weeks after the block, which is close to Supertraining's Chapter 6 duration figure (T2 starts where T1 ends, lasts about as long as T1, observed 4-12 weeks; PDF 375). The same Bompa & Haff sentence also gives the scaling rule \"the greater the magnitude and duration of the concentrated loading phase, the more time needed for fatigue to dissipate and performance to improve (83, 91, 103, 104)\", which the entry does not mention. Verified, no change needed: 1-3 week latency after 4-8 week blocks (PDF 305 image); \"observed to last from 4-12 weeks\", T2 about equal to T1, \"individualised\", \"relatively easy and painless ... intensive ... negatively to extensive\" (PDF 375 image); \"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" (PDF 381); \"stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks\" and t2 equal to the stage (PDF 536 image; the book says \"the steady appearance of LDTE (t2) is equal to the duration of the stage (t1)\", the entry's paraphrase is fair); 18-25% after a 10-day rest, Nyeniskin 1974 (PDF 213). Fig 6.31 reading acceptable (curves bottom out mid-T1 and are already turning up before the dashed line; \"climb during T2\" is true). | Replace \"(Bompa & Haff 5e, PDF idx 61 of the Drive copy)\" with \"(Bompa & Haff 5e, PDF idx 60 / printed p. 49)\". Replace \"That matches neither Supertraining statement: 5.3.3 gives ... (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364).\" with: \"Bompa & Haff also say that 'the greater the magnitude and duration of the concentrated loading phase, the more time needed for fatigue to dissipate and performance to improve' (same page, refs 83, 91, 103, 104). Inference: read as a 4-12 week wait before gains, the Bompa & Haff sentence matches neither Supertraining statement: 5.3.3 gives a 1-3 week latency to gains (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294), and Chapter 6 gives 4-12 weeks as how long the delayed effect lasts (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364). Read as the window in which gains show, it is close to the Chapter 6 figure.\" Optional: note that the Addendum's version of the volume-vs-intensity warning (PDF 536 / printed 525: \"volume concentrated loads are handled by the body relatively easily ... but intense loads negatively react to the work volume\") reads as the reverse of PDF 375, although its own triple-jumper example (a small volume increase caused a fast decline) agrees with PDF 375. |\n74:1. `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md`: Bompa & Haff locator to PDF idx 60 / printed p. 49; label the \"matches neither\" judgment as Inference, note the alternative reading, and add Bompa & Haff's magnitude-and-duration sentence (exact text in the table).\n22:**When NOT to apply** — The 1-3 week figure is \"most commonly,\" with no study named. It is a latency to the onset of gains. Chapter 6 gives a different number for a different quantity: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation,\" its duration \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage,\" and its appearance \"individualised\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image). **Contested across the library:** Bompa & Haff (5th ed.), not Supertraining, write that \"Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase\" (Bompa & Haff 5e, PDF idx 61 of the Drive copy), and the Bompa entry [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]] repeats this as a 4-12 week delay. That matches neither Supertraining statement: 5.3.3 gives a 1-3 week latency to gains (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294); Chapter 6 gives 4-12 weeks as how long the delayed effect lasts (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364). Fig 6.31 on that page (checked against the page image) shows the timing: the trained qualities (F1-F3) are depressed during the concentrated loading stage T1 and climb during T2, which begins where T1 ends (dashed line). The other 4-12 week figures in the book are also durations: Chapter 6 calls unidirectional training \"for prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" very effective (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, text layer), and the Addendum says \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks,\" with the delayed effect's duration \"equal to the duration of the stage\" (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525, text layer). Use the onset figure for when to expect a result and the duration figure for how long it may hold. Chapter 6 also qualifies the warning: during the emergence of the delayed effect \"it is relatively easy and painless for the body to change to intensive loading, but it will react negatively to extensive work\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364), so the restorative period is about volume, not a full stop.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"437347a2-64ad-4c95-8c9e-cfca9e76e1c1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:37:36.787Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"25:| heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md | FIX | (a) **Locator wrong.** Entry: \"(Bompa & Haff 5e, PDF idx 61 of the Drive copy)\". The quote is at 0-based PDF idx 60 / printed p. 49 (viewer page 61); idx 61 / printed p. 50 has only a second, generic Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) citation. The quote wording itself is verbatim. (b) **Unlabelled judgment and over-strong claim.** \"That matches neither Supertraining statement\" is the writer's reasoning and is unlabelled. It is also only true on one reading: Bompa & Haff's \"performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation\" can mean a 4-12 week delay (how the vault entry reads it) or gains appearing within the 4-12 weeks after the block, which is close to Supertraining's Chapter 6 duration figure (T2 starts where T1 ends, lasts about as long as T1, observed 4-12 weeks; PDF 375). The same Bompa & Haff sentence also gives the scaling rule \"the greater the magnitude and duration of the concentrated loading phase, the more time needed for fatigue to dissipate and performance to improve (83, 91, 103, 104)\", which the entry does not mention. Verified, no change needed: 1-3 week latency after 4-8 week blocks (PDF 305 image); \"observed to last from 4-12 weeks\", T2 about equal to T1, \"individualised\", \"relatively easy and painless ... intensive ... negatively to extensive\" (PDF 375 image); \"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" (PDF 381); \"stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks\" and t2 equal to the stage (PDF 536 image; the book says \"the steady appearance of LDTE (t2) is equal to the duration of the stage (t1)\", the entry's paraphrase is fair); 18-25% after a 10-day rest, Nyeniskin 1974 (PDF 213). Fig 6.31 reading acceptable (curves bottom out mid-T1 and are already turning up before the dashed line; \"climb during T2\" is true). | Replace \"(Bompa & Haff 5e, PDF idx 61 of the Drive copy)\" with \"(Bompa & Haff 5e, PDF idx 60 / printed p. 49)\". Replace \"That matches neither Supertraining statement: 5.3.3 gives ... (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364).\" with: \"Bompa & Haff also say that 'the greater the magnitude and duration of the concentrated loading phase, the more time needed for fatigue to dissipate and performance to improve' (same page, refs 83, 91, 103, 104). Inference: read as a 4-12 week wait before gains, the Bompa & Haff sentence matches neither Supertraining statement: 5.3.3 gives a 1-3 week latency to gains (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294), and Chapter 6 gives 4-12 weeks as how long the delayed effect lasts (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364). Read as the window in which gains show, it is close to the Chapter 6 figure.\" Optional: note that the Addendum's version of the volume-vs-intensity warning (PDF 536 / printed 525: \"volume concentrated loads are handled by the body relatively easily ... but intense loads negatively react to the work volume\") reads as the reverse of PDF 375, although its own triple-jumper example (a small volume increase caused a fast decline) agrees with PDF 375. |\n74:1. `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md`: Bompa & Haff locator to PDF idx 60 / printed p. 49; label the \"matches neither\" judgment as Inference, note the alternative reading, and add Bompa & Haff's magnitude-and-duration sentence (exact text in the table).\n22:**When NOT to apply** — The 1-3 week figure is \"most commonly,\" with no study named. It is a latency to the onset of gains. Chapter 6 gives a different number for a different quantity: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation,\" its duration \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage,\" and its appearance \"individualised\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image). **Contested across the library:** Bompa & Haff (5th ed.), not Supertraining, write that \"Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase\" (Bompa & Haff 5e, PDF idx 61 of the Drive copy), and the Bompa entry [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]] repeats this as a 4-12 week delay. That matches neither Supertraining statement: 5.3.3 gives a 1-3 week latency to gains (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294); Chapter 6 gives 4-12 weeks as how long the delayed effect lasts (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364). Fig 6.31 on that page (checked against the page image) shows the timing: the trained qualities (F1-F3) are depressed during the concentrated loading stage T1 and climb during T2, which begins where T1 ends (dashed line). The other 4-12 week figures in the book are also durations: Chapter 6 calls unidirectional training \"for prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" very effective (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, text layer), and the Addendum says \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks,\" with the delayed effect's duration \"equal to the duration of the stage\" (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525, text layer). Use the onset figure for when to expect a result and the duration figure for how long it may hold. Chapter 6 also qualifies the warning: during the emergence of the delayed effect \"it is relatively easy and painless for the body to change to intensive loading, but it will react negatively to extensive work\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364), so the restorative period is about volume, not a full stop.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e0246076-0b42-4ec1-974a-529d7f5dee69","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
