{"parentUuid":"bb76e009-3df3-4be8-90ad-6c966ff8f1ba","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffdaWGWq5bvLq3CnjFwA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018wnx2ts3Jxa7qNQn7j9PDK","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky && python3 - <<'EOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path(\"heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md\");s=p.read_text()\no1=\"(Bompa & Haff 5e, PDF idx 61 of the Drive copy)\"; n1=\"(Bompa & Haff 5e, PDF idx 60 / printed p. 49)\"\no2=\"That matches neither Supertraining statement: 5.3.3 gives a 1-3 week latency to gains (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294); Chapter 6 gives 4-12 weeks as how long the delayed effect lasts (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364).\"\nn2=\"Bompa & Haff also say that \\\"the greater the magnitude and duration of the concentrated loading phase, the more time needed for fatigue to dissipate and performance to improve\\\" (same page, refs 83, 91, 103, 104). Inference: read as a 4-12 week wait before gains, the Bompa & Haff sentence matches neither Supertraining statement: 5.3.3 gives a 1-3 week latency to gains (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294), and Chapter 6 gives 4-12 weeks as how long the delayed effect lasts (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364). Read as the window in which gains show, it is close to the Chapter 6 figure.\"\nfor o,n in((o1,n1),(o2,n2)):\n    assert s.count(o)==1,o[:40]; s=s.replace(o,n)\np.write_text(s);print(\"ok\")\nEOF\ngrep -c \"printed p. 49\" heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md; grep -n \"neither book\\|idx 61\" /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch5c_applied.md | head","description":"Apply 5c round-2 fix; find log lines to correct"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"c