{"parentUuid":"4a90987e-c3b3-436d-8225-578797c27fe4","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"33e25c8a-8e1f-4b6c-9ddd-e59e1af1c598","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018wnx2ts3Jxa7qNQn7j9PDK","type":"tool_result","content":"ok\n1\n26:| 8 | heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md | **When NOT:** the Bompa sentence is replaced. It now says Bompa & Haff (5th ed.), not Supertraining, write \"Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase\" (PDF idx 61 of the Drive copy), and that the Bompa entry repeats this as a 4-12 week delay. Against that: Supertraining gives a 1-3 week latency (PDF 305 / printed 294) and 4-12 weeks as how long the delayed effect lasts (PDF 375 / printed 364). Added the Fig 6.31 timing, read from the PDF 375 image: F1-F3 depressed during the loading stage T1, climbing during T2, which begins where T1 ends (dashed line). The rest of the paragraph (PDF 381 and 536 duration figures, intensive vs extensive) is unchanged.<br>**Related:** added `[[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]]` with \"(4.1.1, speed-strength up 18-25% after a 10-day rest, PDF p. 213; an in-library case for a short latency)\". The 18-25% and 10-day figures are confirmed in the PDF 213 text (Nyeniskin, 1974). The entry's frontmatter says chapter \"4.1.1\", so I wrote 4.1.1, not the review's \"4.1\". Confidence stays `contested`. |\n39:5. **Fix 8, chapter label.** The review calls the delayed-gains entry \"4.1\"; its frontmatter says chapter \"4.1.1\", so the Related note says 4.1.1. **Bompa & Haff quote:** I did not open Bompa & Haff 5e myself. A `find` over the Drive primary storage folder for a PDF with \"bompa\" in its name finished with no match, and the book is not staged in `staging/books/`. The quote and its PDF idx 61 locator come from the review, which says it checked the Drive copy. I did confirm on disk that the vault Bompa entry says \"4 to 12 weeks\" and \"delayed-rebound timing (4–12 wk)\" (lines 16 and 22).\n50:The entry reports Bompa & Haff 5e faithfully. Per the review, the book (Drive copy, PDF idx 61) says \"Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase.\" But Supertraining 6e does not say that:","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"43d369a0-b916-41a2-9c20-730df5c371af","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:37:46.895Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"ok\n1\n26:| 8 | heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md | **When NOT:** the Bompa sentence is replaced. It now says Bompa & Haff (5th ed.), not Supertraining, write \"Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase\" (PDF idx 61 of the Drive copy), and that the Bompa entry repeats this as a 4-12 week delay. Against that: Supertraining gives a 1-3 week latency (PDF 305 / printed 294) and 4-12 weeks as how long the delayed effect lasts (PDF 375 / printed 364). Added the Fig 6.31 timing, read from the PDF 375 image: F1-F3 depressed during the loading stage T1, climbing during T2, which begins where T1 ends (dashed line). The rest of the paragraph (PDF 381 and 536 duration figures, intensive vs extensive) is unchanged.<br>**Related:** added `[[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]]` with \"(4.1.1, speed-strength up 18-25% after a 10-day rest, PDF p. 213; an in-library case for a short latency)\". The 18-25% and 10-day figures are confirmed in the PDF 213 text (Nyeniskin, 1974). The entry's frontmatter says chapter \"4.1.1\", so I wrote 4.1.1, not the review's \"4.1\". Confidence stays `contested`. |\n39:5. **Fix 8, chapter label.** The review calls the delayed-gains entry \"4.1\"; its frontmatter says chapter \"4.1.1\", so the Related note says 4.1.1. **Bompa & Haff quote:** I did not open Bompa & Haff 5e myself. A `find` over the Drive primary storage folder for a PDF with \"bompa\" in its name finished with no match, and the book is not staged in `staging/books/`. The quote and its PDF idx 61 locator come from the review, which says it checked the Drive copy. I did confirm on disk that the vault Bompa entry says \"4 to 12 weeks\" and \"delayed-rebound timing (4–12 wk)\" (lines 16 and 22).\n50:The entry reports Bompa & Haff 5e faithfully. Per the review, the book (Drive copy, PDF idx 61) says \"Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase.\" But Supertraining 6e does not say that:","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4a90987e-c3b3-436d-8225-578797c27fe4","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
