{"parentUuid":"73ff379b-ff8c-4c10-9ed9-bd24417e15d8","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfffLgaxydboKZFnXxr9p","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018LkXHgXCdBUZiMS8HDXsSf","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd ~/SecondBrain && git pull -q --no-rebase --no-edit && git status --porcelain | head -3; cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/promote_mac.py strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky --apply 2>&1 | tail -3; sed -n '14,24p' ~/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching.md","description":"Apply promotion; show Bompa entry lines"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":131,"cache_read_input_tokens":282750,"output_tokens":273,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":131,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":273,"cache_read_input_tokens":282750,"cache_creation_input_tokens":131,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":131},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_018LkXHgXCdBUZiMS8HDXsSf":{"command":"cd ~/SecondBrain && git pull -q --no-rebase --no-edit && git status --porcelain | head -3; cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/promote_mac.py strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky --apply 2>&1 | tail -3; sed -n '14,24p' ~/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching.md","description":"Apply promotion; show Bompa entry lines"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfffLfUzmLRrtQpZHNwr5","type":"assistant","uuid":"994240f2-c8fc-4ad7-b3a9-27a747079d8b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T17:00:49.162Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"994240f2-c8fc-4ad7-b3a9-27a747079d8b","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"bb7640f3-4c73-49eb-8121-41eedd8509ec","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018LkXHgXCdBUZiMS8HDXsSf","type":"tool_result","content":"   changed: _book.md\n\nunresolved links: 0; APPLIED\n# Concentrated Loading and Planned Overreaching\n\n**What it is** — A short period of deliberately high, \"saturated\" training load on one quality (planned overreaching), followed by restitution. Performance is *suppressed* during the loading block and rebounds — supercompensates — afterward. Siff and Verkhoshansky note gains may appear **4 to 12 weeks** after the concentrated block ends; the longer/larger the loading, the longer the delay before performance lifts. The **conjugated sequence** extends this: concentrate on one emphasis (e.g. strength) while holding maintenance loads elsewhere, then shift emphasis in the unloading block so a different quality rises.\n\n**Why it works** — Saturating a stimulus drives a deeper adaptive signal than steady loading. Monitoring via the **testosterone:cortisol (T:C) ratio** tracks the anabolic–catabolic balance: ≥3 weeks of concentrated load lowers the basal T:C ratio (a catabolic shift, reduced preparedness); returning load to normal lets the T:C ratio and performance supercompensate. T:C indexes *preparedness*, not overtraining per se.\n\n**When to apply** — Advanced athletes who can tolerate high loads; building a potent stimulus before a taper/peak; reducing long-term work volume versus concurrent training; timing a delayed performance rebound to a competition window.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not for novices or athletes without the training capacity to absorb saturating loads — fatigue is substantial during accumulation and mismanagement tips into overtraining. The delayed-rebound timing (4–12 wk) must be planned backward from competition, or the peak lands in the wrong week. Builds on [[principle--supercompensation-cycle]] and [[framework--step-loading-paradigm]]; bounded by [[principle--individualization]].\n\n**Example** — A 3-week strength-concentrated block (strength up, speed maintained) is timed so the performance rebound surfaces ~4–6 weeks later at the first key competition; T:C ratio is monitored to confirm the post-block recovery.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d546e8d0-439b-4d63-ab59-1b1905682c3c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T17:00:50.547Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"   changed: _book.md\n\nunresolved links: 0; APPLIED\n# Concentrated Loading and Planned Overreaching\n\n**What it is** — A short period of deliberately high, \"saturated\" training load on one quality (planned overreaching), followed by restitution. Performance is *suppressed* during the loading block and rebounds — supercompensates — afterward. Siff and Verkhoshansky note gains may appear **4 to 12 weeks** after the concentrated block ends; the longer/larger the loading, the longer the delay before performance lifts. The **conjugated sequence** extends this: concentrate on one emphasis (e.g. strength) while holding maintenance loads elsewhere, then shift emphasis in the unloading block so a different quality rises.\n\n**Why it works** — Saturating a stimulus drives a deeper adaptive signal than steady loading. Monitoring via the **testosterone:cortisol (T:C) ratio** tracks the anabolic–catabolic balance: ≥3 weeks of concentrated load lowers the basal T:C ratio (a catabolic shift, reduced preparedness); returning load to normal lets the T:C ratio and performance supercompensate. T:C indexes *preparedness*, not overtraining per se.\n\n**When to apply** — Advanced athletes who can tolerate high loads; building a potent stimulus before a taper/peak; reducing long-term work volume versus concurrent training; timing a delayed performance rebound to a competition window.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not for novices or athletes without the training capacity to absorb saturating loads — fatigue is substantial during accumulation and mismanagement tips into overtraining. The delayed-rebound timing (4–12 wk) must be planned backward from competition, or the peak lands in the wrong week. Builds on [[principle--supercompensation-cycle]] and [[framework--step-loading-paradigm]]; bounded by [[principle--individualization]].\n\n**Example** — A 3-week strength-concentrated block (strength up, speed maintained) is timed so the performance rebound surfaces ~4–6 weeks later at the first key competition; T:C ratio is monitored to confirm the post-block recovery.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"994240f2-c8fc-4ad7-b3a9-27a747079d8b","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
