er wrap a [[wikilink]] across lines.\n- If 23 pages are too many to do well, stop at a clean section boundary and say exactly where. Quality over coverage.\n- Write a scope file `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6a.txt` listing your new filenames, one per line.\n- Lint: `cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky`; fix anything flagged in YOUR files and paste the lines for your files.\n- No git. No vault edits.\n\n## Final reply\nEntries by kind and confidence; entry count per section with printed and PDF page ranges (for `_book.md`); where you stopped if not at PDF 346; reciprocal-link suggestions; unclear or contradictory spots; anything you could not read; lint output."}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffkJdeQARNsQYKmQaQQo","type":"assistant","uuid":"f0d27859-49ea-40d1-a131-c08b891b8dab","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:06:12.931Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f0d27859-49ea-40d1-a131-c08b891b8dab","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PreToolUse:Agent","toolUseID":"toolu_01RHUKnUWFjvT2HYcWopvBdb","hookEvent":"PreToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"PreToolUse\",\"updatedInput\":{\"description\":\"Atomize Supertraining 6a\",\"prompt\":\"You are a Flint ingest atomizer. Task: atomize *Supertraining* (Siff and Verkhoshansky, 6th Expanded Edition) Chapter 6, batch 6a: sections 6.1 \\\"The Development of Training Organisation\\\", 6.2 \\\"Periodisation as a Form of Organisation\\\" (6.2.1 Types, 6.2.2 Calculation of the Parameters, 6.2.3 Intensity and Volume) and 6.3 \\\"The Periodisation Controversy\\\", PDF idx 324-346 (printed 313-335). Stop where 6.4 begins (PDF 347). Three other atomizers are working at the same time on 6.4-6.8 (PDF 347-362), 6.9-6.11 (PDF 363-378) and 6.12-6.17 (PDF 379-403); do not atomize their pages.\\n\\n## Read first, in full\\n1. `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md` (every rule is checked by a reviewer; Windows paths in it, use the Mac translations below).\\n2. `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills/ingest-book/SKILL.md` (schema, frontmatter keys, file kinds).\\n3. `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/method-baseline.md` (Eric's approved baseline; quote lines verbatim with line number AND tag, e.g. \\\"line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\\\").\\n4. `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md` (map; page key PDF idx = printed + 11, 0-based).\\n5. The Chapter 5 reviews, to avoid their errors: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md`, `..._ch5b_opus.md`, `..._ch5c_opus.md` and their `_round2` versions. Common misses: unlabelled writer judgments, \\\"contested\\\" set for things that are not real contradictions (different populations or aims), misread figure values, baseline quotes without tags, invented examples.\\n6. Two existing entries in the output folder as style references.\\n\\n## Mac paths\\n- Vault library (read-only, dedup greps): `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`\\n- Staging library: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library`\\n- Output folder: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`\\n- Source PDF (noisy text layer): `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`\\n- Text: `~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \\\"import pymupdf; d=pymupdf.open('<pdf>'); print(d[N].get_text())\\\"`. Render every page with a figure, table or number to `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_renders/6a_pN.png` (`d[N].get_pixmap(dpi=200).save(...)`) and open it with Read. Write \\\"about\\\" for values read off a graph. Only claim \\\"verified against the page image\\\" if you opened it.\\n\\n## Hard rules\\n- Add NEW entry files only. Do NOT edit any existing entry and do NOT edit `_book.md` (the coordinator owns it). Dedup against vault and staging: periodisation is heavily covered by Bompa (`periodization--bompa`), Issurin (`block-periodization--issurin`, including Issurin 2016's critique of traditional and concentrated models), Bondarchuk, Evans and Supertraining Ch1 and Ch5 (5.3.3 conjugate sequence, 5.4). Add an entry only for a genuinely new angle and link the existing one in Related; where another library source disagrees, record it and say which. List reciprocal-link suggestions in your report instead of editing other files.\\n- Use the source's own examples, otherwise start the Example with \\\"Inference:\\\". No mechanism the authors do not give. `contested` only for a real contradiction (same question, same population) or a named library source that disagrees; give both pages. Locators \\\"PDF p. X / printed p. Y\\\" on every number, definition and quote. \\\"The authors\\\" unless a passage clearly belongs to one author (a passage naming Verkhoshansky in the third person is Siff writing; 6.3 \\\"Periodisation Controversy\\\" is likely Siff's voice: check).\\n- Frontmatter `chapter:` uses the section number (e.g. \\\"6.2.1\\\"). `created: 2026-10-03`. No em dashes in titles or body (section headers keep `**What it is** —`). No review history. Never wrap a [[wikilink]] across lines.\\n- If 23 pages are too many to do well, stop at a clean section boundary and say exactly where. Quality over coverage.\\n- Write a scope file `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6a.txt` listing your new filenames, one per line.\\n- Lint: `cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky`; fix anything flagged in YOUR files and paste the lines for your files.\\n- No git. No vault edits.\\n\\n## Final reply\\nEntries by kind and confidence; entry count per section with printed and PDF page ranges (for `_book.md`); where you stopped if not at PDF 346; reciprocal-link suggestions; unclear or contradictory spots; anything you could not read; lint output.\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <deferred_tool_bootstrap>\\n    The context-mode tools below may be DEFERRED in your harness — their schemas are not loaded yet, so calling them directly fails (e.g. \\\"tool not found\\\" / InputValidationError). Load them ONCE before your first ctx_* call:\\n    ToolSearch(query: \\\"select:mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\\\")\\n    After that they are callable. If any ctx_* call fails as not-found, ToolSearch it and retry — do NOT fall back to Bash/Read just because the schema was not loaded yet.\\n  </deferred_tool_bootstrap>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\\n  </when_not_to_use>\\n\\n  <file_writing_policy>\\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\\n  </file_writing_policy>\\n\\n  <output_constraints>\\n    <artifact_policy>\\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\\n    </artifact_policy>\\n  </output_constraints>\\n  <session_continuity>\\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\\n  </session_continuity>\\n\\n</context_window_protection>\",\"subagent_type\":\"general-purpose\",\"model\":\"opus\"}}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/pretooluse.mjs\"","durationMs":92},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6ed673dd-4deb-4dc4-b220-127503911087","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:06:13.028Z","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f0d27859-49ea-40d1-a131-c08b891b8dab","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RHUKnUWFjvT2HYcWopvBdb","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Async agent launched successfully. (This tool result is internal metadata — never quote or paste any part of it, including the agentId below, into a user-facing reply.)\nagentId: a4b39cbfd8b94a37b (internal ID - do not mention to user. Use SendMessage with to: 'a4b39cbfd8b94a37b', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent.)\nThe agent is working in the background. You will be notified automatically when it completes. You know nothing about its results until that notification arrives — do not report, assume, or predict them; continue other work or respond to the user in the meantime.\nDo not duplicate this agent's work — avoid working with the same files or topics it is using.\noutput_file: /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/a4b39cbfd8b94a37b.output\nDo NOT Read or tail this file via the shell tool — it is the full subagent JSONL transcript and reading it will overflow your context. If the user asks for progress, say the agent is still running; you'll get a completion notification."}]}]},"uuid":"fe795e12-edd8-4910-a1dd-bc86bca6492e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:06:13.149Z","toolUseResult":{"isAsync":true,"status":"async_launched","agentId":"a4b39cbfd8b94a37b","description":"Atomize Supertraining 6a","resolvedModel":"claude-opus-5-5[1m]","prompt":"You are a Flint ingest atomizer. Task: atomize *Supertraining* (Siff and Verkhoshansky, 6th Expanded Edition) Chapter 6, batch 6a: sections 6.1 \"The Development of Training Organisation\", 6.2 \"Periodisation as a Form of Organisation\" (6.2.1 Types, 6.2.2 Calculation of the Parameters, 6.2.3 Intensity and Volume) and 6.3 \"The Periodisation Controversy\", PDF idx 324-346 (printed 313-335). Stop where 6.4 begins (PDF 347). Three other atomizers are working at the same time on 6.4-6.8 (PDF 347-362), 6.9-6.11 (PDF 363-378) and 6.12-6.17 (PDF 379-403); do not atomize their pages.\n\n## Read first, in full\n1. `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md` (every rule is checked by a reviewer; Windows paths in it, use the Mac translations below).\n2. `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills/ingest-book/SKILL.md` (schema, frontmatter keys, file kinds).\n3. `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/method-baseline.md` (Eric's approved baseline; quote lines verbatim with line number AND tag, e.g. \"line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\").\n4. `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md` (map; page key PDF idx = printed + 11, 0-based).\n5. The Chapter 5 reviews, to avoid their errors: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md`, `..._ch5b_opus.md`, `..._ch5c_opus.md` and their `_round2` versions. Common misses: unlabelled writer judgments, \"contested\" set for things that are not real contradictions (different populations or aims), misread figure values, baseline quotes without tags, invented examples.\n6. Two existing entries in the output folder as style references.\n\n## Mac paths\n- Vault library (read-only, dedup greps): `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`\n- Staging library: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library`\n- Output folder: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`\n- Source PDF (noisy text layer): `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`\n- Text: `~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \"import pymupdf; d=pymupdf.open('<pdf>'); print(d[N].get_text())\"`. Render every page with a figure, table or number to `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_renders/6a_pN.png` (`d[N].get_pixmap(dpi=200).save(...)`) and open it with Read. Write \"about\" for values read off a graph. Only claim \"verified against the page image\" if you opened it.\n\n## Hard rules\n- Add NEW entry files only. Do NOT edit any existing entry and do NOT edit `_book.md` (the coordinator owns it). Dedup against vault and staging: periodisation is heavily covered by Bompa (`periodization--bompa`), Issurin (`block-periodization--issurin`, including Issurin 2016's critique of traditional and concentrated models), Bondarchuk, Evans and Supertraining Ch1 and Ch5 (5.3.3 conjugate sequence, 5.4). Add an entry only for a genuinely new angle and link the existing one in Related; where another library source disagrees, record it and say which. List reciprocal-link suggestions in your report instead of editing other files.\n- Use the source's own examples, otherwise start the Example with \"Inference:\". No mechanism the authors do not give. `contested` only for a real contradiction (same question, same population) or a named library source that disagrees; give both pages. Locators \"PDF p. X / printed p. Y\" on every number, definition and quote. \"The authors\" unless a passage clearly belongs to one author (a passage naming Verkhoshansky in the third person is Siff writing; 6.3 \"Periodisation Controversy\" is likely Siff's voice: check).\n- Frontmatter `chapter:` uses the section number (e.g. \"6.2.1\"). `created: 2026-10-03`. No em dashes in titles or body (section headers keep `**What it is** —`). No review history. Never wrap a [[wikilink]] across lines.\n- If 23 pages are too many to do well, stop at a clean section boundary and say exactly where. Quality over coverage.\n- Write a scope file `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6a.txt` listing your new filenames, one per line.\n- Lint: `cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky`; fix anything flagged in YOUR files and paste the lines for your files.\n- No git. No vault edits.\n\n## Final reply\nEntries by kind and confidence; entry count per section with printed and PDF page ranges (for `_book.md`); where you stopped if not at PDF 346; reciprocal-link suggestions; unclear or contradictory spots; anything you could not read; lint output.\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n    The context-mode tools below may be DEFERRED in your harness — their schemas are not loaded yet, so calling them directly fails (e.g. \"tool not found\" / InputValidationError). Load them ONCE before your first ctx_* call:\n    ToolSearch(query: \"select:mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\")\n    After that they are callable. If any ctx_* call fails as not-found, ToolSearch it and retry — do NOT fall back to Bash/Read just because the schema was not loaded yet.\n  </deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n</context_window_protection>","outputFile":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/a4b39cbfd8b94a37b.output","canReadOutputFile":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f0d27859-49ea-40d1-a131-c08b891b8dab","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
