{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:26:37.230Z","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>aad6d0b6c78043607</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01CjMqoWogA7v69Dyi43txdk</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/aad6d0b6c78043607.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Atomize Supertraining 6b\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I wrote 16 new entries for batch 6b, covering sections 6.4 through 6.8.2 (PDF 347-362, printed 336-351). Lint flags nothing in my files, and I edited no existing entry, `_book.md` or the vault.\n\n**Where I stopped:** 6.8.3 starts on PDF 363, the same page as 6.9, so all of PDF 363 is left to batch 6c. At the other end, 6.4 starts mid-page on PDF 347, below two lines that finish 6.3. The scope file is `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6b.txt`.\n\n**By kind:** 4 framework, 5 heuristic, 3 principle, 4 claim.\n**By confidence:** 7 untested, 7 supported, 2 contested, 0 proven. The two contested entries are the distributed-vs-concentrated framework, where Issurin 2016 disagrees, and the block-durations heuristic, where the book contradicts itself (see the list further down).\n\n**Entries per section, for `_book.md`:**\n\n| Section | Printed | PDF | Entries |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 6.4 Training as a Problem in Management | 336-337 | 347-348 | 1 |\n| 6.5 Prerequisites for Organising Training | 338-339 | 349-350 | 1 |\n| 6.6 Basic Management Theory (6.6.1: 339-342 / 350-353; 6.6.2: 342-345 / 353-356) | 339-345 | 350-356 | 3 (6.6.1: 2, 6.6.2: 1) |\n| 6.7 Classification of Sports | 345-346 | 356-357 | 1 |\n| 6.8 lead-in and 6.8.1 Adaptation to Intense Muscular Work | 346-350 | 357-361 | 8 |\n| 6.8.2 Structural-Functional Specialisation | 350-351 | 361-362 | 2 |\n\n**Skipped on purpose:**\n- **Management theory not applied to training on the page:** Maslow, Alderfer, McClelland, the Blake-Mouton grid (the book also credits it to Ohio State), Tannenbaum-Schmidt and Likert, the high/medium/low problem triage and six problem-solving steps, Maier and Roskin, and the 7-S framework.\n- **Management theory I did write up:** Yerkes-Dodson, Vroom and Hersey-Blanchard, because the authors apply each one to training or to coaches. Each entry names whose model it is. The 6.6.1 section cites Siff (1990), but the entries still say \"the authors\".\n- **Duplicates:** the 6.5 passage on VO2 max falling in the competition period is already in two Chapter 4 entries, and the 6.8.2 heterochronicity sentence is already in a Chapter 2 entry. I linked those instead.\n- **Folded in:** the Sargent (1926) \"fourth power of running speed\" sentence went into the specialisation principle, with its single citation and no later check.\n\n**Pages read:**\n- **From the page image:** PDF 347-358 (plus a close-up crop of Fig 6.22) and 360-362 (plus close-up crops of both Fig 6.23 panels).\n- **Text layer only:** PDF 359 and 363, plus quotes I took from PDF 305 and 381.\n\n**Not readable:**\n- In Fig 6.23 panel B, I can't tell which curve dips deepest.\n- In panel A, I matched the speed, maximum-strength and absolute-strength labels to curves only by where each line ends.\n\n**Unclear or contradictory spots in the source:**\n- **Concentrated block length:** 3-4 weeks (PDF 360) against 4-12 and 4-8 weeks (PDF 305) and \"up to 2 months\" (PDF 381). Recorded as contested with all three pages.\n- **18-22 weeks:** three 5-6 week blocks plus pauses come to about 17-21 weeks; three 3-4 week blocks come to about 11-15. The text doesn't say which it means, and the figure it cites (Fig 6.23) stops at 18 weeks. Its dip also lasts until about weeks 7-9, longer than the 3-4 weeks the same page gives.\n- **Fig 6.22:** the text calls graph A a parabola that reaches a peak, but on the page it is a nearly straight line.\n- **Vankov sentence:** distance runners \"of all qualification levels\" are aerobic-focused, yet the top level is anaerobic-focused. Quoted as printed and marked unclear.\n- **Wrong cross-reference:** 6.8.2 says \"the classification scheme of 6.5\"; the scheme is in 6.7.\n- **Situational leadership:** maturity is rated \"low, moderate or high\" but there are four codes, M1-M4.\n- **Spelling:** the book prints \"Prokor\" (left as is).\n- **Volume:** \"the more extensive and longer the exhaustion, the higher the rebound\" sits next to Bondarchuk's finding that 50% more volume changed nothing, and the authors don't reconcile them. Noted as Inference only.\n- **Against Eric's baseline:** the book says team sports run mostly on glycolytic energy; Eric's baseline (lines 116-117) treats football glycolytic work as a worst case to absorb incidentally, though staff do program it in the off-season. Both voices are labelled.\n\n**Reciprocal links to add on existing files (I didn't edit them):**\n- Ch1 `claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation` → the current-adaptation-reserve principle and the compensatory-adaptation claim.\n- 5.3.3 `framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading` and `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next` → the block-durations heuristic, the restorative-stage heuristic and the distributed-vs-concentrated framework.\n- 5.4.3 `framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks` → the specialisation principle.\n- 5.1 `framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type` → the sport-groups framework.\n- Bompa `principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching` and the Issurin 2016 brief → the distributed-vs-concentrated framework.\n- Old Bull `monitoring-versus-testing`, `knowledge-behaviour-matrix`, `replace-translation-with-early-education` and `assign-meeting-ownership-to-develop-staff`, plus Evans's activation-levels entry → the files that cite them.\n\n**Overlaps with the other batches (not linked, since their files are unreviewed and may be renamed):**\n- 6.9.5 `claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes`.\n- 6.12.3 `framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound` and `heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models`.\n- 6.12.2 `claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving`.\n- 6.11 `claim--annual-speed-strength-dynamics-depend-on-qualification`.\n- 6.9.4 `claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality`: its \"3-4 months\" for explosive strength sits oddly next to 6.8.1's 3-4 week block. Worth a reviewer's look.\n- Batch 6a's 6.3 periodisation-controversy material may overlap my mesocycle principle.\n\n**Lint output:**\n```\nmine: (no lines)\nTOTAL ISSUES: 9\n```\nAll 9 issues are in other batches' files, mostly links to entries they haven't written yet. My 16 files also each have the six section headers exactly once and no wikilink split across lines.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>332490</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>83</tool_uses><duration_ms>1209758</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
