documented claims.\n- The 2 `contested` entries: the distributed-vs-concentrated framework (against Issurin 2016: open the vault brief in `research-papers/` and confirm the critique is aimed at Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, accurately stated) and the block-durations heuristic (3-4 weeks PDF 360 vs 4-12 / 4-8 weeks PDF 305 vs \"up to 2 months\" PDF 381): challenge whether these are the same quantity or different ones (block length vs stage length vs delayed-effect duration).\n- Fig 6.22 and Fig 6.23 (PDF ~360-362): read them yourself and check every value and curve label the entries use, including the writer's admitted uncertainty in panel A and B.\n- Any \"Eric's baseline:\" line on football energy systems (lines 116-117).\n\nWrite `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6b_opus.md`. Final reply: overall verdict, counts of GO / FIX / NO-GO, and the one-line list of required fixes."}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffmtUJNxHppb3MCa53h7","type":"assistant","uuid":"259fba81-f958-4f0d-a58b-08a86d7bce45","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:26:49.160Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"259fba81-f958-4f0d-a58b-08a86d7bce45","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PreToolUse:Agent","toolUseID":"toolu_0163cyBWBmCWr8Gzt3ptpxPT","hookEvent":"PreToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"PreToolUse\",\"updatedInput\":{\"description\":\"Review Supertraining 6b round 1\",\"prompt\":\"You are an independent reviewer in Flint's ingest review gate. Another model wrote these entries; your job is to FALSIFY them, not confirm. Read-only on entries and sources. Write only your report file.\\n\\nRead first: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_book_prompt.md` (your instructions; follow its 6 checks and output format exactly) and `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md` (the standards). Both use Windows paths. Mac translations: vault library `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`; method baseline `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/method-baseline.md` (check every \\\"Eric's baseline:\\\" quote, line number and tag).\\n\\nScope: the 16 files listed in `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6b.txt`, in `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`. Other new Chapter 6 files in that folder (batches 6a, 6c, 6d, still being written) are out of scope; use them only in your duplicate check.\\n\\nSource: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, sections 6.4-6.8.2, PDF idx 347-362 (printed 336-351); some entries also cite PDF 305 and 381. PDF idx (0-based) = printed + 11. Text: `~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \\\"import pymupdf; d=pymupdf.open('<pdf>'); print(d[N].get_text())\\\"`. Render pages at dpi=200 to `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_renders/r6b_pN.png` and open with Read for every figure, table and number you check. The writer read PDF 359 from the text layer only: open it yourself.\\n\\nSpecial attention:\\n- Borrowed management theory (Yerkes-Dodson, Vroom, Hersey-Blanchard): each entry must name whose model it is, and must not present the general model as the authors' finding. Check the \\\"untested\\\" ratings are right for undocumented claims.\\n- The 2 `contested` entries: the distributed-vs-concentrated framework (against Issurin 2016: open the vault brief in `research-papers/` and confirm the critique is aimed at Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, accurately stated) and the block-durations heuristic (3-4 weeks PDF 360 vs 4-12 / 4-8 weeks PDF 305 vs \\\"up to 2 months\\\" PDF 381): challenge whether these are the same quantity or different ones (block length vs stage length vs delayed-effect duration).\\n- Fig 6.22 and Fig 6.23 (PDF ~360-362): read them yourself and check every value and curve label the entries use, including the writer's admitted uncertainty in panel A and B.\\n- Any \\\"Eric's baseline:\\\" line on football energy systems (lines 116-117).\\n\\nWrite `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6b_opus.md`. Final reply: overall verdict, counts of GO / FIX / NO-GO, and the one-line list of required fixes.\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <deferred_tool_bootstrap>\\n    The context-mode tools below may be DEFERRED in your harness — their schemas are not loaded yet, so calling them directly fails (e.g. \\\"tool not found\\\" / InputValidationError). Load them ONCE before your first ctx_* call:\\n    ToolSearch(query: \\\"select:mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\\\")\\n    After that they are callable. If any ctx_* call fails as not-found, ToolSearch it and retry — do NOT fall back to Bash/Read just because the schema was not loaded yet.\\n  </deferred_tool_bootstrap>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_conte