{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:29:54.905Z","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a4b39cbfd8b94a37b</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01RHUKnUWFjvT2HYcWopvBdb</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/a4b39cbfd8b94a37b.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Atomize Supertraining 6a\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I wrote 28 new entries for 6.1 through 6.3 and finished at the assigned boundary (6.4 starts partway down PDF 347). The lint script reports no issues in my files; its remaining issues are in other batches' files. I edited no existing entry, did not touch `_book.md`, the vault or git, and the scope file lists all 28 filenames.\n\n**Entries by kind:** 11 frameworks, 8 heuristics, 7 claims, 1 principle, 1 case study.\n\n**By confidence:** 24 `supported`, 2 `contested`, 2 `untested`, none `proven`.\n- **Contested:** `claim--chernyak-volume-intensity-week-combinations` (the book contradicts itself on the same page) and `claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov` (two named authorities give opposite answers to the same question).\n- **Untested:** `case-study--vorobyev-ermakov-weightlifting-load-distributions` and `framework--supplementary-readiness-markers-hr-bp-ceff-palpation` (no data on the pages).\n\n**Section counts for `_book.md`:**\n\n| Section | Printed pp | PDF idx | Entries |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 6.1 | 313-315 | 324-326 | 6 |\n| 6.2 lead-in | 316-319 | 327-330 | 8 |\n| 6.2.1 | 319-323 | 330-334 | 4 |\n| 6.2.2 | 323-332 | 334-343 | 8 |\n| 6.2.3 | 332-333 | 343-344 | 1 |\n| 6.3 | 333-336 | 344-347 | 1 |\n\n6.2.1, 6.2.2, 6.2.3 and 6.4 each start partway down a page (PDF 330, 334, 343, 347). The chronobiology closing part of 6.3 (PDF 346-347) is not atomized: it only says \"the possible link ... then becomes apparent\" and gives no evidence for it.\n\n**Voice:** 6.3 is written as Siff (it names Verkhoshansky in the third person and refers to \"this textbook\"), so Verkhoshansky's critique is attributed \"as reported by Siff.\" Cybernetic periodisation and the order/causal analysis section are cited to Siff (1990), and the cybernetic entry labels the Siff attribution as an inference. Item 5 of the Matveyev critique and scheme 5 are also Siff, by the same third-person test. Every baseline quote carries its line number and tag, and the writer's own reasoning is prefixed \"Inference:\".\n\n**Reciprocal-link suggestions:**\n- Ch1 `claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus` → `claim--hettinger-four-features-that-stimulate-strength-gains`\n- Ch5 `framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means` → `framework--six-broad-periodisation-schemes` and `claim--conversion-phase-is-a-misnomer`\n- Ch5 `framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise` → `framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique`\n- Ch5 `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next` → `heuristic--shock-microcycle-limits`\n- Issurin `framework--microcycle-types-and-load-wave-design` → `framework--matveyev-microcycle-and-mesocycle-types-and-sequences`\n- Bompa `framework--step-loading-paradigm` → the alternation claim and `heuristic--rule-of-sixty-percent-light-day-volume`\n- Evans `claim--periodization-evidence-classic-vs-nonclassic-literature-debate` → the controversy entry and `framework--eight-weaknesses-of-the-matveyev-model`\n- Vault Harries 2015 and Afonso 2019 → the alternation and controversy entries\n\n**Unclear or contradictory spots, all recorded in the entries:**\n- **\"System 4\" (PDF 334):** the book says the chapter's main emphasis is \"system 4\", which is the asymmetric pyramid. Its own pointers lead to concentrated loading, which is system 5, so this is probably a misnumbering.\n- **Two figures numbered 6.2:** the Jim Ryun running plan (PDF 332) and the weightlifter daily load (PDF 333).\n- **Zone I (PDF 335):** Table 6.1 heads it \"60%-\", but the text on the same page defines it as 50-59%.\n- **Warm-up exclusion (PDF 334):** Chernyak counts only loads above 40% of 1RM, then the text excludes warm-ups below about 70%.\n- **Printing slip (PDF 334):** \"3 sets with 75 kg\" is said to give the same 75% relative intensity, but 75 kg is 62.5% of the 120 kg 1RM.\n- **Chernyak (PDF 343):** medium-volume/medium-intensity weeks are both \"inadvisable always\" and the most frequent; high/high weeks are among the most common but only 2-3 a year. The week ranges add up to only about 36-45 weeks.\n- **Table 6.5 (PDF 341):** the text says eccentric work adds 70% in each case, but the table's ratios run from 0% to +100%.\n- **Table 6.2 (PDF 336):** called both an \"optimal\" mean training weight and an \"upper limit\" not to exceed.\n- **Fig 6.3B and Fig 6.5 (PDF 333):** after a high-volume month, 6.3B still peaks in week 4; the Fig 6.5 caption compares it with Fig 6.3 rather than Fig 6.4.\n- **Ryun figure (PDF 332):** the opening block has an unexplained second, much shorter bar.\n- **Technique bullet (PDF 329):** the \"premature introduction of skills\" item is ambiguous about whether it is something to stress or avoid.\n- **Undulating models:** Verkhoshansky's dismissal of \"undulating models\" as word play sits against the book's own heavy/medium/light alternation and Eric's undulating preference (baseline line 106). I recorded it as a tension, not a contradiction.\n\n**Pages read:** I opened every page image from PDF 324 to 346. Values read off graphs (Fig 6.7, the Ryun figure) are marked \"about\"; the bar labels in Figs 6.3-6.6 are printed values. Nothing was unreadable.\n\n**Lint:** the run ended with `TOTAL ISSUES: 3` and none of those lines name my files, so there are no lines for my files to paste. I also checked that each entry has exactly one of each section header, no em dash in any title and no wrapped wikilinks; all pass.\n\nFiles are in `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`:\n- `_scope_supertraining_ch6a.txt` (the filename list, in `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/`)\n- page renders in `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_renders/`, named `6a_p324.png` to `6a_p346.png`</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>339329</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>96</tool_uses><duration_ms>1421864</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
