ontested` entries: PDF 375 vs PDF 536 (intensive vs extensive during LDTE emergence: confirm the Addendum really says the reverse, from the image); PDF 371 \"fairly unimportant\" vs \"preserve the above order\" vs PDF 301 and 288; and the concentrated-vs-distributed claim against the vault Issurin 2016 brief (confirm the critique targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, accurately stated). Challenge whether each is a real contradiction.\n- Figs 6.24, 6.25, 6.28, 6.29, 6.30: every value read off a graph says \"about\" and matches the image; the Fig 6.24 units problem is described correctly.\n- The possible same-study link between Fig 6.24 and the Ch5 novice experiment (PDF 299): it must be labelled Inference and not asserted.\n\nWrite `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6c_opus.md`. Final reply: overall verdict, counts of GO / FIX / NO-GO, and the one-line list of required fixes.\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n    The context-mode tools below may be DEFERRED in your harness — their schemas are not loaded yet, so calling them directly fails (e.g. \"tool not found\" / InputValidationError). Load them ONCE before your first ctx_* call:\n    ToolSearch(query: \"select:mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\")\n    After that they are callable. If any ctx_* call fails as not-found, To