{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:35:54.763Z","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a4ace87316fef52e2</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01YZGLkLzNNdjchJpkqfHbHh</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/a4ace87316fef52e2.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review Supertraining 6a round 1\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Overall verdict: GO AFTER FIXES.** Of the 28 entries, 22 are GO, 6 need a fix and none is NO-GO. Every figure and table you flagged matches the page images.\n\nRequired fixes:\n1. **`framework--early-history-of-periodisation-kotov-to-dyson`**: the entry says the \"may create the impression\" warning is \"on the next page\". It is on the same page as Dyson (PDF 325).\n2. **`heuristic--progress-skills-only-at-70-80-percent-technical-reliability`**: \"the premature introduction of skills\" is the second bullet on PDF 329, not the third.\n3. **`framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation`**: the entry says the worked example follows the 70% warm-up rule. It doesn't. The example keeps the 80 kg set, which is 66.7% of the 120 kg max, so it follows neither the 70% rule nor Chernyak's 40% rule (PDF 334). The writer's other flag, that 3 x 3 at 75 kg is 62.5% of 120 kg, is correct.\n4. **`framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique`**: soften the Siff attribution. PDF 339-340 name neither author. \"This textbook introduced\" (PDF 345) credits the book, not Siff.\n5. **`heuristic--heart-rate-work-capacity-tests-for-lifters`**: reword the Table 6.5 point. The 0% pulls fit the book's \"where there is significant eccentric action\" qualifier. The real mismatches with the \"70% more work\" rule are bench press (+100%), squat clean (about +88%) and the lifts that fall between.\n6. **`claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov`**: the British-runner decline is Verkhoshansky's own claim, not Horwill's or Tschiene's. Horwill is cited for \"slavish worship\"; Tschiene for \"periodisation unchanged since Matveyev's 1965 formalisation\" (PDF 344). Split the citations and add Tschiene's point.\n\nChecks you asked for:\n- **Tables 6.1, 6.2 and 6.5, Figs 6.1-6.7 and the Ryun figure** all match the images. Both pages printing a \"Figure 6.2\" (PDF 332 and 333) are real.\n- **Writer's flagged inconsistencies** are all really on the page and correctly described: the Zone I mismatch (\"60%-\" in the table against \"50-59%\" in the text), 75 kg = 62.5%, and the Chernyak week counts summing to 36-45.\n- **Voice in 6.3**: the third-person test holds on PDF 344-345 (\"this textbook\", \"this book\"). No Siff opinion is presented as Verkhoshansky's or the reverse.\n- **Contested entries**: both are justified, and both sides are on the cited pages. Issurin 2016, Harries, Afonso and Evans are already named; no Bompa entry in the vault takes a side, so none is missing.\n- **Baseline**: every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote is verbatim with the correct line and tag, including line 106.\n- **Wikilinks**: none are broken.\n- **Duplicates**: none against the vault, Ch1, Ch5 or Ch6b-d. The report suggests optional Related links instead: the 6.15 and 6.14.x entries, and Evans's two annual-plan entries (`bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types`, `ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan`).\n\nThe report also lists ideas no entry captures, mainly the chronobiology section (PDF 346-347) and Verkhoshansky's five-year-plan argument with Zheljazkov's partial agreement.\n\nThe report is at `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6a_opus.md`.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>219531</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>31</tool_uses><duration_ms>347214</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
