re the right quantities, and do not rule it out for medium-level athletes unless the page does.\n- Remove any process note mentioning other batches (\"batch 6a\" etc.) from entry bodies.\n- Missing ideas: the management-theory items (decision-making, management versus leadership, Blake-Mouton grid, needs theories) were skipped on purpose because the authors do not apply them to training on the page. Add one only if you find the authors DO apply it to training on the page; otherwise record \"not added: not applied to training\" in the log. The endurance monitoring point on PDF 359: fold it into an existing 6b entry if it fits.\n- Related links: add the review's suggested links only within 6b files and only to targets that exist today.\n- No renames unless required; if you rename, use `mv` with fully literal absolute paths and report any 6a/6c/6d file that links the old slug (6c links several 6b files; do not edit them).\n- Do not touch `_book.md` or the vault.\n- Brief rules hold: label every voice, locators on every number and quote, no em dashes in titles or body (section headers keep `**What it is** —`), no review history in entries, never wrap a [[wikilink]] across lines, baseline quotes carry line number and tag.\n- Write the log to `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6b_applied.md` (Fixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03).\n- Prove the fixes landed: grep each fixed file for the new wording, and grep 6b files for \"batch\" (expect none). Run `cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky` and paste the lines for 6b files and the total. Paste both in your final reply and at the end of the log.\n- No git. No vault edits.\n\nFinal reply: per-fix outcome (applied / disagreed), the Fig 6.22 reading, any renames, grep proof, lint output."}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffni87HQnJZBjm6nsocU","type":"assistant","uuid":"a9c79c