{"parentUuid":"463a653e-b7a0-48f3-bfed-8ae53e040827","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e6067869-283d-436e-bd93-ddd9ceadbde1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019LvdzydrTtGQq8kea666Qd","type":"tool_result","content":"ok","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"9bed4ee4-1f78-4311-bf87-32567901c7b8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:45:01.436Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"ok","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6a_applied.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":93,"oldLines":3,"newStart":93,"newLines":7,"lines":[" ```"," "," No 6a file has a lint line. The one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes), outside scope.","+","+## Coordinator fix after round 2","+","+Round 2 (`review_supertraining_ch6a_opus_round2.md`): GO AFTER FIXES, 27 GO / 1 FIX. Applied in `claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md`: the unlabelled \"This is the section's only physiological argument for cycled training.\" is replaced by a labelled Inference that also names Zheljazkov's physiological point. Wording changed from the review's \"heterochronous restoration\" to the book's own \"heterochronical character\" (checked on PDF 346 text)."]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":21,"oldLines":7,"newStart":21,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Zheljazkov (1998), as reported by Siff.** Periodisation \"still managed to be utilised very effectively for many years in training numerous elite soviet and Eastern European athletes\"; it is \"erroneous\" to treat Matveyev's concept as \"the complete theory of sports training\"; Verkhoshansky's charges of \"primitive methodology\" and \"scholastic demagogy\" are \"a unprofessional personal attack\"; national rises and falls in running are natural, not caused by periodisation. On the communism point he partly agrees: \"models of society such as communism indeed have a profound impact on the nature of sports training, but ... this happens in all countries and should not necessarily be taken to mean that this interaction between sport and society is always negative\" (PDF p. 345 / printed p. 334). Classical periodisation \"has not lost its fundamental importance\" but has \"serious qualitative limitations\" to be modified by research on \"exhaustion, adaptation and restoration,\" since regeneration is \"nonlinear and heterochronical\"; applied sport science \"does not at all replace the entire concept of periodisation, but enriches it\" (PDF p. 345-346 / printed p. 334-335)."," ","-**Chronobiology, the section's closing argument.** The subsection \"Chronobiology and Periodisation\" (PDF p. 346-347 / printed p. 335-336, verified against the page image of PDF 346) offers biological clocks as possible support: \"Future corroboration of the principles of periodisation may rely on some of the advances being made in the field of chronobiology.\" The text describes a master circadian pacemaker, the suprachiasmatic nucleus, \"entrained to the cycle of day and night by nervous input from the retina,\" which \"can also be shifted or entrained by other stimuli, such as an appropriately timed bouts of exercise\"; two further inputs, \"one from the thalamic intergeniculate structure, and another from the midbrain raphe nuclei,\" \"appear to play an important role in entraining or shifting rhythms by means of exercise and emotional arousal.\" It concludes only that \"The possible link to periodisation of physical training then becomes apparent\" (PDF p. 346 / printed p. 335). This is the section's only physiological argument for cycled training. Confidence for this part: `untested`. The text gives no study linking circadian mechanisms to multi-week or annual training cycles; Inference: a 24-hour clock says nothing direct about mesocycle or macrocycle length.","+**Chronobiology, the section's closing argument.** The subsection \"Chronobiology and Periodisation\" (PDF p. 346-347 / printed p. 335-336, verified against the page image of PDF 346) offers biological clocks as possible support: \"Future corroboration of the principles of periodisation may rely on some of the advances being made in the field of chronobiology.\" The text describes a master circadian pacemaker, the suprachiasmatic nucleus, \"entrained to the cycle of day and night by nervous input from the retina,\" which \"can also be shifted or entrained by other stimuli, such as an appropriately timed bouts of exercise\"; two further inputs, \"one from the thalamic intergeniculate structure, and another from the midbrain raphe nuclei,\" \"appear to play an important role in entraining or shifting rhythms by means of exercise and emotional arousal.\" It concludes only that \"The possible link to periodisation of physical training then becomes apparent\" (PDF p. 346 / printed p. 335). Inference: this is the only passage in 6.3 that offers a biological mechanism in support of cycled training; Zheljazkov's physiological point (the \"heterochronical character\" of restoration and supercompensation, PDF p. 345-346) argues for modifying periodisation, not for cycling as such. Confidence for this part: `untested`. The text gives no study linking circadian mechanisms to multi-week or annual training cycles; Inference: a 24-hour clock says nothing direct about mesocycle or macrocycle length."," "," **Why it works** — Each side argues from different evidence: Verkhoshansky from the 1950s origins of the model, its three-sport base and national running records; Zheljazkov from decades of Soviet and Eastern European elite success. Neither gives controlled data on these pages, and both national-results arguments are correlational (Inference)."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6a_applied.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"463a653e-b7a0-48f3-bfed-8ae53e040827","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
