{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:46:02.155Z","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ac6f5ed6959c8e2da</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01BPpg8QgBNSiYYYpTHmuUvp</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/ac6f5ed6959c8e2da.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review Supertraining 6c round 1\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: GO AFTER FIXES. 10 GO, 9 FIX, 0 NO-GO.** No entry is wrong at its core. The nine fixes are mostly misread graph values, wrong page references, missing labels and missing links.\n\n**Your special-attention points:**\n- **LDTE entries:** They agree with the existing library entries (1-3 week lag at PDF 305, 4-12 weeks as how long the effect lasts at PDF 375) and add new angles: the volume optimum, and the difference between maintaining an effect and the LDTE. No number is restated differently.\n- **The three `contested` tags all hold:**\n  - **Intensive vs extensive work (PDF 375 vs 536):** the Addendum image really does say the reverse. Its own example agrees with Chapter 6, though, so a translation slip is plausible.\n  - **Within-session order:** the conflict is real but sits elsewhere than the entry says. The PDF 301 bullets are rules for sequencing stages, not sessions. The real same-session rule is on PDF 300, which the entry missed. PDF 371 on its own is a hedge, not a contradiction.\n  - **Concentrated vs distributed:** the Issurin 2016 brief is stated accurately and does target Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model. It is a disagreement about how far the evidence reaches, not a flat opposite.\n- **Figures:** Figs 6.24 and 6.29 read correctly, and the Fig 6.24 units problem is described correctly. Fig 6.30's loading-block start and end are misread, and three Fig 6.25 values are slightly off (fixes 1 and 8). Fig 6.28 has no numbers and no in-scope entry cites it.\n- **Same-study link (Fig 6.24 and the Chapter 5 novice experiment):** properly labelled Inference and not asserted.\n\n**Also checked:** every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote, line number and tag matches `method-baseline.md`. Every link resolves today, including the five into 6a/6b.\n\n**Required fixes:**\n1. **LDTE principle:** the Fig 6.30 loading block runs from about month 1.6 to 4.5, not 1.3 to 4.3. Record all three \"4-12 weeks\" uses: PDF 305, PDF 375, and PDF 536, where the Addendum uses it for stage length. The book's own rule that the effect lasts about as long as the loading stage reconciles 375 and 536.\n2. **Technique heuristic:** the Addendum is Verkhoshansky's own paper translated by Yessis (PDF 515), so it should not say \"the authors\". Add a link to the 6d `heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block`.\n3. **Altitude claim:** the Susman 1978 sentence (\"not as a documented LDTE\") is the writer's reading. Reword it and label it Inference.\n4. **Concentrated claim:** add links to the 6b `framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation` and the 6d `claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes`. Only part of the quoted conclusion is italic in the original, so say \"partly italicised\".\n5. **Within-session heuristic:** rewrite the contested reason:\n   - PDF 301 is a stage-level rule (see the heading on PDF 300).\n   - Add the PDF 300 finding that strength followed by speed-strength in the same session is least effective, from novices and average-level athletes.\n   - Call PDF 371 a hedge.\n6. **Sequential (4-6 week) heuristic:** label two unlabelled statements as Inference (\"so the 4-6 weeks is a minimum\" and \"Bondarchuk agrees\"). Add the PDF 300-301 stage-level rule that strength work comes after speed-strength work.\n7. **Time-limits claim:** the \"Unclear wording\" paragraph counts PDF 372 as evidence against the literal PDF 370 sentence. PDF 372 actually agrees with it; only the PDF 373 sequential model conflicts.\n8. **Nedobyvailov (Fig 6.25):** power is about 20 (GP3) and about 14 (GP2). Absolute strength GP2 is about 18 and speed GP1 is about 18.\n9. **Restoration heuristic:** label two of the writer's judgments as Inference (\"removes part of the signal\" and the 15% \"confounds\" claim). Add the missing blank line before Example.\n\n**Missing ideas (3):**\n- The warning against using highly stimulating means too early (PDF 368-369, the section with Fig 6.28).\n- Special-preparation exercises must exceed competition conditions to keep their training effect for highly qualified athletes (PDF 367). This may already be partly covered by the 4.3.3 entry.\n- 6.8.3: general fitness qualities trained separately do not add up to sport-specific fitness (PDF 363). This may already be covered by the Chapter 1.3 specificity entries.\n\nThe report also suggests reciprocal links for the 6b and 6d fixers.\n\nReport: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6c_opus.md`</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>297763</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>68</tool_uses><duration_ms>880705</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
