{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:48:45.424Z","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ab1767f74271380f1</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01MucJ9Y72y5p9QT6SJpMPQ8</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/ab1767f74271380f1.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review Supertraining 6d round 1\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: GO AFTER FIXES.** Of the 25 entries, 14 are GO, 11 need fixes and none is NO-GO. Every quote, table value and baseline line I checked is correct; the fixes are about how figures were read and how the contested entries frame Issurin.\n\nThe report is at `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6d_opus.md`.\n\n**Required fixes:**\n- **Explosive-strength annual model (Fig 6.39):** the phase boundaries and block positions are right, but three curve readings are wrong. The strength curves start rising again inside block 1. The first peak is about month 5, inside phase II. Technique rises through most of block 2 rather than falling. The Issurin challenge should be limited to transferring the model to football, because Issurin's p. 11 favours this kind of model in jumping-type sports.\n- **Tri-cyclic model:** boxing and swimming are cited at PDF 387 as sports that could move away from tri-cyclic, not as examples of it. It is rated `supported` even though Issurin names combat sports, which are its own domain. That is inconsistent with the explosive-strength model being `contested`.\n- **Two-mesocycles claim:** it is not the same question. Aim 1 (PDF 389) is about how many large mesocycles, and it explicitly allows one main competition period. The monocyclic advice (PDF 387, 396) is about how many competition peaks. Only the wording at PDF 387 merges the two. `contested` should rest on the Issurin disagreement over stage length and count.\n- **Complex training:** Issurin agrees that complex training falls short for elite athletes; what he disputes is the remedy (one target versus a minimal number). The brief also has Issurin finding no evidence that amateurs can't handle concentrated loads, which cuts against the book's \"complex for beginners\" limit. The entry should add the brief's caveat that the 28 studies are Issurin's own non-systematic selection, and cite Matveyev 1970 separately.\n- **Speed sports (Fig 6.32b):** two curves (strength-endurance and anaerobic-glycolytic) rise during the block. The entry says they dip.\n- **Swimming microcycles:** drop the flagged conflict about aerobic potential rising for 12 h and then falling, because the figure shows exactly that. Restoration is 72 h in the text but about 30-36 h in the figure, and the entry should give both.\n- **Tabachnik sprint tables:** every value matches. The writer's three flagged split sums are correct, but one inconsistency was missed: the men's 5 m from a low start (1.18 s) equals the 15 m time (1.16-1.18 s) for the same 10.0 s goal.\n- **Loading reduction after a block:** record the Chapter 5 figure of a 1-3 week wait before gains (PDF 305) next to \"far longer than 1-2 weeks\", and link the 6c entry with the same no-added-volume rule.\n- **Antonova/Levchenko:** one sentence calls both groups qualified and then says the long jumpers' level isn't stated. Link the 6c delayed-effect entry that cites the same studies, and note the book dates Levchenko as 1980, 1981 and 1982.\n- **Long competition period:** change \"a football season is shorter than 4-5 months\" to \"comparable\".\n- **Concentrated block then moderate explosive work:** the book's own text (strength block after the GPP groundwork) supports the figure labels, so the Fig 6.45 caption is the likelier error.\n\n**What held up:**\n- **Issurin critique:** the vault brief confirms it is aimed at Verkhoshansky's concentrated single-emphasis model. None of the four `contested` entries stretches it to all block periodisation.\n- **Fig 6.45/6.46 caption vs labels:** the mismatch is described correctly.\n- **Duplicates:** both 6d heuristics that overlap the 6c delayed-effect entries add a genuinely new angle.\n- **Five ideas no entry captures** are listed in the report. They include the Table 6.11 javelin strength norms and a unit error in Table 6.12 (values labelled \"cm\" are clearly metres).\n\n**Method:** I checked every quote against the text layer. I opened 14 page images myself, including Tables 6.8-6.12, and read Fig 6.39 at 300 dpi plus enlarged crops. The remaining pages carry no figure values the entries depend on, and I checked them by text and an italics scan only; the report says so.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>281668</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>58</tool_uses><duration_ms>890793</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
