import pymupdf; d=pymupdf.open('<pdf>'); print(d[N].get_text())\\\"`. Render to `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_renders/f6d_pN.png` and open with Read for anything you touch. For Fig 6.39 (PDF ~394-395) render at dpi=400 and read the strength, peak and technique curves yourself before changing them; for Fig 6.32b and Fig 6.36(b) do the same. Values off a graph say \\\"about\\\"; anything the image cannot settle is marked unclear.\\n\\nRules:\\n- Verify each review claim on the page (or in the Issurin brief) before applying. If the review is wrong, do not apply it; record why under \\\"Disagreements with the review\\\".\\n- Explosive-strength model: this is the entry that matters most for Eric's football work. After your fixes, the Issurin challenge must say exactly what the brief says, scoped to transferring the model to football (a team sport), and must not claim Issurin rejects it for jumping-type sports.\\n- Confidence must be consistent across the 4 Issurin-related entries (explosive model, tri-cyclic, two-mesocycles, complex training): each rating needs a stated reason that fits the brief.\\n- Any entry citing the Addendum (PDF 514-590) must attribute it to Verkhoshansky, not \\\"the authors\\\" (the Addendum is his own paper, PDF 515).\\n- Links to 6b/6c files: confirm each target exists at that moment before adding (those batches are being fixed and could rename); otherwise record it in the log.\\n- Missing ideas (5, including Table 6.11 javelin norms and the Table 6.12 cm/m unit error): fold into existing 6d entries where they fit; new entry only if needed, appended to the 6d scope file.\\n- No renames unless required; if you rename, use `mv` with fully literal absolute paths and report any other file linking the old slug.\\n- Do not touch `_book.md` or the vault.\\n- Brief rules hold: label every voice, locators on every number and quote, no em dashes in titles or body (section headers keep `**What it is** —`), no review history, never wrap a [[wiki