narash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch7b.txt` listing your new filenames, one per line.\n- Lint: `cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky`; fix anything flagged in YOUR files and paste the lines for your files.\n- No git. No vault edits.\n\n## Final reply\nEntries by kind and confidence; entry count per section with printed and PDF page ranges (for `_book.md`); where you stopped; reciprocal-link suggestions; unclear or contradictory spots; anything you could not read; lint output.\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n    The context-mode tools below may be DEFERRED in your harness — their schemas are not loaded yet, so calling them directly fails (e.g. \"tool not found\" / InputValidationError). Load them ONCE before your first ctx_* call:\n    ToolSearch(query: \"select:mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\")\n    After that they are callable. If any ctx_* call fails as not-found, ToolSearch it and retry — do NOT fall back to Bash/Read just because the schema was not loaded yet.\n  </deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n</context_window_protection>","outputFile":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/ae553e10f4940b5ab.output","canReadOutputFile":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2ac207d5-0bd2-49c0-8b58-82eefd2707a8","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:22.215Z","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a49b624c4b08292c9</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_014tVM7XExC8TCHj45AaRbvY</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/a49b624c4b08292c9.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Fix Supertraining 6c entries\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I applied all 9 fixes after checking each against the page. Three of the review's suggested wordings were looser than the source, so I changed them (listed under Disagreements below). I folded the 3 missing ideas into existing 6c entries, so there are no new entries and no renames. Every link resolves, and lint shows no issues in any 6c file.\n\nThe log is at `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6c_applied.md`. Edited files are in `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`: the 9 FIX entries plus 2 GO entries (for the missing ideas). The scope file still lists 19 entries. I did not touch `_book.md`, any 6b or 6d file, the vault, or git.\n\n**Fix outcomes (all applied):**\n1. **LDTE principle:** Fig 6.30 block is now about month 1.6 to 4.5. All three \"4-12 weeks\" uses are recorded with their pages (PDF 305, 375, and the Addendum on 536), with T2 ≈ T1 reconciling 375 and 536.\n2. **Technique heuristic:** PDF 515 confirms the Addendum is \"Dr. Yuri Verkhoshansky, Translated from the Russian by Dr. Michael Yessis\". The entry now says \"Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper\" instead of \"the authors\". The translation-slip Inference is added, and the link to the 6d technique-work entry is in. It stays `contested`.\n3. **Altitude claim:** the Susman sentence now uses the review's wording, with the reading labelled Inference.\n4. **Concentrated claim:** now says \"partly italicised\", links the 6b framework and the 6d complex-training claim, and adds the optional scope Inference.\n5. **Within-session heuristic:** the \"contested\" paragraph is rewritten. PDF 371 is called a hedge. The real conflict across chapters is laid out with PDF 288, 299 and 300. The PDF 301 bullets are moved out as stage-level rules.\n6. **Sequential heuristic:** two Inference labels added, plus the PDF 300-301 stage-level rule.\n7. **Time-limits claim:** the \"Unclear wording\" paragraph is re-sorted. The PDF 372 lists agree with the literal p.370 sentence; only p.373 conflicts with it.\n8. **Nedobyvailov:** Fig 6.25 values corrected (below), along with the Example and the When NOT pair.\n9. **Restoration heuristic:** two Inference labels and the missing blank line. Beyond the review, the Example also said the 15% volume rise was in the restoration group; it now says the text does not say which group.\n\n**Disagreements with the review:**\n- In fix 5, the review stated as fact that PDF 300's \"strength followed by speed-strength\" opposes p.371's \"barbell then jumping\". Neither page equates barbell with strength or jumping with speed-strength, so I labelled that link Inference (Fig 5.13 supports it).\n- In fix 7, the review says p.370's citations are \"the same ones\" p.373 gives. p.373 gives four, and p.370's two are among them; the entry now says that.\n- In fix 2, PDF 536 says \"in triple jumpers\" and then switches to \"the athlete … his chance\". I recorded both instead of picking singular or plural.\n- I skipped the optional PDF 438 note because I didn't open that page.\n\n**Figure readings** (300 dpi crops, measured against the gridlines):\n- **Fig 6.30:** the month-1 tick is at about 697 px and month 2 at about 800. The shaded block runs from about 755 px (≈ month 1.6) to about 1025 px (≈ month 4.5). Vertical jump peaks at about 7 near month 3.7 and ends at about 4.5. Stride length peaks at about 11.5 and ends at about 11. Ten jumps peaks at about 9.7 and ends at about 9.5.\n- **Fig 6.25** (≈10.5 px per point):\n\n| | GP1 | GP2 | GP3 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Absolute strength | about 7.0 | about 18.0 | about 16.7 |\n| Speed | about 18.0 | about 11.2 | about 13.6 |\n| Power | about 6.2 | about 14.2 | about 20.0 (on the gridline) |\n\nBoth agree with the review.\n\n**Where the missing ideas went:**\n1. The warning against using highly stimulating means too early (PDF 368-369) went into `principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue`. Its 5.4.4 link is now annotated, and I linked the conjugate-sequence framework.\n2. \"SPP means must exceed competition conditions\" (PDF 367) went into `claim--middle-distance-runners-need-strength-and-jump-training-benchmarks`, with links to the 4.3.3 effort principle and the dynamic-correspondence heuristic.\n3. The 6.8.3 point that general qualities don't add up to special preparedness (PDF 363) went into the same claim, with a link to the specificity entry. The book's \"discussed in 1.3\" points to specificity, which is section 1.4 in this edition, so I noted that as Inference.\n\n**Addendum follow-up:** no staged or vault Supertraining entry attaches \"the authors\" to an Addendum (PDF 514-590) statement. The one near miss is `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md`, with identical copies in staging and the vault. It cites PDF 536 as \"the Addendum says …\" (text layer only), so the voice is unlabelled rather than wrong. I recommend changing it to \"Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper\" in both copies.\n\n**Links:** all three 6b/6d targets existed when I linked them (13:51). The 6b framework had been modified at 13:40, so it was mid-edit; if the 6b fixer renames it, the link in the concentrated claim must follow. The reciprocal-link suggestions are in the log.\n\n**Grep proof** (excerpt; the full list is in the log):\n```\nprinciple--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum.md:25:from about month 1.6 to about month 4.5\nprinciple--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum.md:33:The book uses 4-12 weeks for three things\nheuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window.md:27:Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1,\nheuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window.md:29:Verkhoshansky's own, from the Addendum\nheuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window.md:31:[[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]]\nclaim--ldte-in-endurance-training-documented-only-after-altitude-training.md:19:The authors do not call this an LDTE\nclaim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md:16:partly italicised in the original\nclaim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md:26:[[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]]\nclaim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md:26:[[claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes]]\nheuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast.md:22:a hedge, not a contradiction\nheuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast.md:22:Inference: reading barbell work as\nheuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive.md:22:Inference: so the 4-6 weeks is a minimum\nheuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive.md:22:Chapter 5 states this as a rule\nclaim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality.md:28:appear among the four p. 373 gives\ncase-study--nedobyvailov-light-heavy-and-combined-loads-in-jumpers.md:18:power GP1 about 6, GP2 about 14, GP3 about 20\nheuristic--routine-artificial-restoration-can-blunt-adaptation.md:24:but not whether in both groups or only the restoration group\nprinciple--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue.md:18:inappropriate to use highly stimulating means prematurely\nclaim--middle-distance-runners-need-strength-and-jump-training-benchmarks.md:18:not only conform to, but exceed, competition\n```\n\nAll 13 old phrases now appear in 0 files. Across the 19 files there are no em dashes outside section headers or in titles, no review history, no wrapped wikilinks and no CR line endings. Every file has 6 section headers, and all 83 link targets resolve.\n\n**Lint** (after the final edits):\n```\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 132, 133]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\nNo 6c file appears. The one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive, which was there before my edits.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>235502</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>63</tool_uses><duration_ms>704911</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>","reason":"absorbed_mid_turn","commandUuid":"988b68e5-e64d-47da-81b3-9e6d7ae040c2","deliveryId":"7f546ab6-4f0d-4ad1-a0a8-01cee4ad6344"}
