stem.md\",\n \"351); the page's cross-references are to 2.2.3 and 5.4.3 only.\",\n \"351); the page's cross-references are to 2.2.3, 5.4.3 and \\\"the classification scheme of 6.5\\\" (the scheme itself is in 6.7).\"),\n]\nfor f,o,n in E:\n    p=Path(f);s=p.read_text();assert s.count(o)==1,f;p.write_text(s.replace(o,n));print(\"ok\",f[:40])\nEOF\ncd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \"\nimport pymupdf;d=pymupdf.open('staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf')\nprint('6.5' in d[362].get_text(), 'determined' in d[360].get_text(), 'structural unit' in d[327].get_text())\"","description":"Apply 3 sentence fixes; verify page phrases"}},"wireIngestContext":{"toolu_013D73MVksQYVoHP2tBZwZz4":{"cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffpc59WJYK8CLRpxdLWp","type":"assistant","uuid":"4f320056-2e9c-4830-a14d-9cce61792fbf","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:02:23.780Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"4f320056-2e9c-4830-a14d-9cce61792fbf","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a2b72ded-2f77-4788-be97-76a7696d93d6","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013D73MVksQYVoHP2tBZwZz4","type":"tool_result","content":"ok principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-t\nok heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-we\nok principle--functional-specialisation-is-\nTrue True True","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d318de5f-a9cf-4092-bff6-de5ab56c928d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:02:24.691Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"ok principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-t\nok heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-we\nok principle--functional-specialisation-is-\nTrue True True","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":23,"oldLines":7,"newStart":23,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Inference: sketching an off-season or preparatory phase for a trained athlete: up to three loading blocks, each followed by a 7-10 day lighter pause, then a longer recovery before the next phase. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 183, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the 18-22 week ceiling is a reason to place a longer recovery period on that roadmap rather than run loading blocks back to back."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (concentrated block length).** Here the concentrated block lasts \"3-4 weeks, after which a recuperation period is necessary\", given as the duration that was \"determined\" for maximal enhancement of the CAR (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). In 5.3.3 the book gives, \"for instance, ... 4-8 week concentrated blocks of strength, speed-strength or speed training\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image). In 6.12.2 the method concentrates \"a high volume of unidirectional loading for a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, checked against the page image). The 4-8 weeks and \"up to 2 months\" agree with each other; the 3-4 weeks overlaps them only at 4 weeks, so the conflict is between the 3-4 week figure and the longer blocks. **Different quantities, not part of the conflict:** 4-12 weeks is the length of a unidirectional training *stage*: \"unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294) and \"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370). 4-12 weeks is also how long the delayed training effect lasts: \"the LDTE was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation\", and in principle its duration \"is approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image; see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]). 1-3 weeks is the latency before the gains of a concentrated block appear (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). 18-22 weeks is the stage needed to exhaust the CAR (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). The upper limits for one primary emphasis by motor quality are in [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]]. Inference: one reading links block length to stage length (three 3-4 week blocks give about 9-12 weeks of unidirectional emphasis, inside the 4-12 week stage), but the book does not say so. Inference: 5.3.3 and 6.12.2 both define loading as concentrated by one month's volume (\"23-25% of the general volume for the year\", PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294; \"23-25% of the general annual volume\", PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370), which makes the month the working unit; \"up to 2 months\" reads as a ceiling. **Unclear arithmetic:** three 5-6 week continuous blocks plus two 7-10 day pauses come to about 17-21 weeks; three 3-4 week concentrated blocks plus two pauses come to about 11-15 weeks. The text does not say which form the 18-22 weeks refers to, and Fig 6.23, which it cites, spans only 18 weeks. Inference: the 18-22 week figure fits the continuous form better. The authors do not say how the pause length or the \"longer recuperation period\" should be set beyond these figures.","+**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (concentrated block length).** Here the concentrated block lasts \"3-4 weeks, after which a recuperation period is necessary\", given as the duration that was \"determined\" for maximal enhancement of the CAR (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). In 5.3.3 the book gives, \"for instance, ... 4-8 week concentrated blocks of strength, speed-strength or speed training\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image). In 6.12.2 the method concentrates \"a high volume of unidirectional loading for a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, checked against the page image). The 4-8 weeks and \"up to 2 months\" agree with each other, and 3-4 weeks sits inside the \"up to 2 months\" ceiling; the conflict is between the 3-4 weeks the book says was \"determined\" (PDF p. 360) and the 4-8 weeks of PDF p. 305. **Different quantities, not part of the conflict:** 4-12 weeks is the length of a unidirectional training *stage*: \"unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294) and \"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370). 4-12 weeks is also how long the delayed training effect lasts: \"the LDTE was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation\", and in principle its duration \"is approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image; see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]). 1-3 weeks is the latency before the gains of a concentrated block appear (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). 18-22 weeks is the stage needed to exhaust the CAR (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). The upper limits for one primary emphasis by motor quality are in [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]]. Inference: one reading links block length to stage length (three 3-4 week blocks give about 9-12 weeks of unidirectional emphasis, inside the 4-12 week stage), but the book does not say so. Inference: 5.3.3 and 6.12.2 both define loading as concentrated by one month's volume (\"23-25% of the general volume for the year\", PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294; \"23-25% of the general annual volume\", PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370), which makes the month the working unit; \"up to 2 months\" reads as a ceiling. **Unclear arithmetic:** three 5-6 week continuous blocks plus two 7-10 day pauses come to about 17-21 weeks; three 3-4 week concentrated blocks plus two pauses come to about 11-15 weeks. The text does not say which form the 18-22 weeks refers to, and Fig 6.23, which it cites, spans only 18 weeks. Inference: the 18-22 week figure fits the continuous form better. The authors do not say how the pause length or the \"longer recuperation period\" should be set beyond these figures."," "," **Example** — The authors give the rule, not a worked plan. Inference: a January-to-May preparatory phase of three 4-week concentrated blocks, each followed by a 7-10 day lighter week, then a longer recovery period before summer training (the authors give no length for it)."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," The new point is the qualifier: \"specialisation in the first two cases does not imply that development is either strictly aerobic or anaerobic, but rather that there is a particular primary emphasis of functional specialisation. One can successfully develop a high level of explosive strength in acyclic locomotion only when one possesses sufficiently high aerobic efficiency. At the same time, improving the efficiency of the energy producing systems in cyclic locomotion should be based on highly developed muscular strength and local muscular endurance\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). For cyclic sports: \"In cyclic sports, strength preparedness, mainly in the form of strength-endurance, plays an important role. Research shows that, with a relatively moderate increase in muscular strength, together with a rise in the athlete's qualification, the qualities of dynamic strength-endurance and power accelerate the increase in functional strength (Zhurbinoi, 1978)\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351)."," ","-**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism for why explosive strength needs aerobic efficiency on this page. For the cyclic side the authors cite Zhurbinoi (1978) on strength-endurance (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351); the page's cross-references are to 2.2.3 and 5.4.3 only. Inference: Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities (see Related) supports the same point. On cost they add: \"energy expenditure being almost proportional to the fourth power of running speed (Sargent, 1926),\" with the rising cost met by \"a greater reliance being placed on anaerobic processes\" (Henry, 1951; Margaria, 1963) (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). The fourth-power figure carries only that 1926 citation, and no later check is given.","+**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism for why explosive strength needs aerobic efficiency on this page. For the cyclic side the authors cite Zhurbinoi (1978) on strength-endurance (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351); the page's cross-references are to 2.2.3, 5.4.3 and \"the classification scheme of 6.5\" (the scheme itself is in 6.7). Inference: Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities (see Related) supports the same point. On cost they add: \"energy expenditure being almost proportional to the fourth power of running speed (Sargent, 1926),\" with the rising cost met by \"a greater reliance being placed on anaerobic processes\" (Henry, 1951; Margaria, 1963) (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). The fourth-power figure carries only that 1926 citation, and no later check is given."," "," **When to apply** — Inference: when a speed-power programme drops all aerobic work, or an endurance programme drops strength work, this principle argues for keeping a supporting dose of the other. Eric's baseline: \"For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery (regenerating ATP between bouts)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 123, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: that is one plausible route for the authors' \"sufficiently high aerobic efficiency,\" though the authors do not give it. Eric's baseline: football's priority stack names \"alactic and aerobic capacity/power\" as Goals 1 and 2 (line 116, Explicit, claimed again in interview 2026-09-28, Q1). Inference: the baseline already pairs the explosive and aerobic systems, as the authors' qualifier does."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":26,"oldLines":7,"newStart":26,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Inference: when reviewing a plan, judge progress block by block, not week by week, especially during a deliberately heavy block. Eric's baseline: \"Weekly tracking is preferred over infrequent (e.g., once-a-phase) testing, specifically to catch plateaus or declines early enough to adjust programming\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 59, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the two fit if weekly data are read against the mesocycle's expected shape: a decline during a planned concentrated block is expected, while the same decline in a normal block is a signal. Eric's baseline: undulating periodization, \"waving volume week to week\" (line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: a wave inside a mesocycle is already a planned non-steady rise."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — The authors keep the old rules \"to a certain extent ... still valid,\" and the second one (\"generally undesirable ... permissible in certain instances\") is not withdrawn. Inference: a decline outside a planned loading block, or one that does not recover when load drops, is still the warning the third rule describes. The book calls the training session \"the fundamental unit\" of periodisation in 6.2 (PDF p. 327 / printed p. 316) and the mesocycle the fundamental unit of training in 6.8.1 (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit. Inference: the book does not reconcile the wording (see [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]]).","+**When NOT to apply** — The authors keep the old rules \"to a certain extent ... still valid,\" and the second one (\"generally undesirable ... permissible in certain instances\") is not withdrawn. Inference: a decline outside a planned loading block, or one that does not recover when load drops, is still the warning the third rule describes. The book calls the training session \"the fundamental unit\" of periodisation in 6.2 (PDF p. 327 / printed p. 316) and the mesocycle the fundamental unit of training in 6.8.1 (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347). Inference: the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit (the book calls the session a structural unit, PDF p. 327, but never calls the mesocycle a planning unit). Inference: the book does not reconcile the wording (see [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]])."," "," **Example** — The authors' own contrast: the third rule says any decrease means the training is \"incorrectly organised\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347), while concentrated loading produces a \"decrease of the functional indicators\" followed by a rise that \"exceeds the level achieved with the first form of adaptation\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349)."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4f320056-2e9c-4830-a14d-9cce61792fbf","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
