{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:19.420Z","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a5b11ae5d2f940e54</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_014SRQ63Mf9nesCTFNCxjEfW</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/a5b11ae5d2f940e54.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Fix Supertraining 6d entries\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I applied all 11 fixes to the 6d entries and none are left open; I disagreed with the review's wording or numbers in 7 places, recorded in the log. The 6d files have no lint lines, the grep proof shows every fix landed, and none of the old wording remains.\n\nThe log is at `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6d_applied.md`. The scope file is unchanged at 25 lines, nothing was renamed, and I made no edits to 6b, 6c, `_book.md` or the vault. None of the 25 files cites the Addendum (0 matches across 25 files), so no attribution change was needed.\n\n## Per-fix outcome\n| # | Entry | Outcome |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | explosive-strength bi-cyclic model | Applied. Fig 6.39 rewritten from my own reading. The Issurin 2016 challenge now quotes the brief exactly and is scoped to the football transfer. Stays `contested`, on a different basis than the review gave (see below). |\n| 2 | tri-cyclic | Applied. The boxing/swimming citation is corrected; moved from `supported` to `contested`. |\n| 3 | two mesocycles | Applied. The \"internal conflict\" is now a terminology tension (PDF 387 against 389 and 396); `contested` rests on Issurin. |\n| 4 | complex training | Applied. Issurin agrees with the critique and contests the remedy and the beginners limit. Added the brief's caveat that the 28 studies are Issurin's own selection. Matveyev 1970 is now its own clause. |\n| 5 | speed sport, Fig 6.32(b) | Applied, with one change: An-Gly peaks late in the block, not mid-block. |\n| 6 | microcycles | Applied. The false 12 h conflict is removed and I used my own crossing times. Missing idea 4 is added here. |\n| 7 | Tabachnik | Applied. Added the 5 m (1.18 s) against 15 m (1.16-1.18 s) inconsistency and the review's optional point on the 15 m step. |\n| 8 | loading reduction | Applied. PDF 305's 1-3 week latency now sits beside \"far longer than 1-2 weeks\"; added the link to rule 2 of the 6c time-technique entry. |\n| 9 | Antonova/Levchenko | Applied, plus one extra: PDF 381 also dates Antonova 1980 (1982 elsewhere). Added the 6c LDTE link. |\n| 10 | prolonged competition | Applied (\"comparable\", not \"shorter\"). |\n| 11 | follow-a-concentrated-block | Applied. The text supports the figure labels, so the caption is the likelier error (marked Inference). |\n\n**Where I disagreed with the review (all in the log):**\n- **The two review rows apply different rules.** One keeps the explosive model `contested` for football only; the other says a contest should follow the entry's own sport domain. I applied the domain rule to all four Issurin-related entries.\n- **\"Jumping-type sports\" is not in the brief.** Your prompt and the review both paraphrase p. 11 that way. The brief says only \"better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality\". The entry quotes that and says the brief names no jumping or throwing sports; the link to explosive events is marked Inference.\n- **Fig 6.39 Technique** ends block 2 about level with its first peak, not above it.\n- **Fig 6.36(b) crossings** are about 35 h and 38-40 h, not 30-36 h.\n- **The brief's \"repeats the book's critique\"** refers to Issurin's own book, not Supertraining, so the entry says so.\n- **Antonova's dates** also slip (1980 at PDF 381), which the review did not list.\n\n## Figure readings (all values \"about\")\n- **Fig 6.39** (400 dpi crops):\n  - Block 1 runs from the start to late month 3. Block 2 runs from the month 5/6 tick to just past the month 7/8 tick, about two thirds of block 1's height.\n  - Strength curves: the top two bump in month 1. All three are lowest at month 2 and already rising in month 3, while block 1 is still running. They peak in early month 5, just inside phase II, dip in months 6-7 and are highest at month 11.\n  - The two upper curves cross at the month 8/9 tick. Right-edge order Starting, Explosive, Maximum is certain; which curve is which before the crossing is Inference.\n  - Technique peaks at month 4, is lowest at month 6, is back to its first-peak level by the end of block 2, peaks at month 10 and declines.\n  - GPP and Speed can only be told apart in phase IV: Speed peaks at months 8-9 and falls, GPP is lowest there and rises, and they cross at month 10. Before month 8 they are unclear.\n- **Fig 6.32(b)** (400 and 600 dpi):\n  - Two curves dip below the axis during the block and end at Vmax and STATE; which is which is unclear.\n  - S-E rises steadily.\n  - An-Gly peaks about 70% of the way through the block, falls, then rises to a second hump.\n  - Speed starts only at the block's end.\n- **Fig 6.36(b)** (400 dpi):\n  - Aerobic week: VO2 max goes from 87% to 100% at 35 h and 107% at 72 h. Training effect goes from 93% to 100% at 15-18 h.\n  - Anaerobic week: VO2 max peaks at 113% around 6-10 h and falls to 106%. Training effect goes from 94% to 100% at 38-40 h and 102.5-103% at 72 h.\n\n## Confidence for the four Issurin-related entries\nThe test for all four: does a library source disagree with the entry inside the sport domain the entry claims?\n- **Explosive model: `contested`.** Issurin's 2008 book disputes the two-mesocycle structure in this domain: stages of about two months, four to seven a season, and world-class jumpers peaking 7-11 times a season. Issurin 2016 does not count against the model in its own domain. It counts against transferring it to football, a team sport (brief p. 6: studies \"did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance\").\n- **Tri-cyclic: `contested` (was `supported`).** Brief p. 6 names combat sports and swimming, which are inside this model's stated domain, and the model runs on concentrated blocks.\n- **Two mesocycles: `contested`.** Rests on Issurin's 2008 book. The entry says the 2016 brief does not address mesocycle count.\n- **Complex training: `contested`.** Issurin contests the remedy (a minimal number of targets per block, not one) and the beginners limit (brief p. 11, amateurs). The 28 studies are his own non-systematic selection.\n\n## Missing ideas\n- **1-3** (Table 6.11 javelin norms, Tables 6.7/6.10 middle distance, and the Table 6.12 \"Standing quintuple jump (cm)\" values of 9.25-13.70, marked as Inference that they must be metres): added to `framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models`.\n- **4** (PDF 387 \"currently scant\" findings): added to the microcycle case study, with the reason it stays `supported`.\n- **5** (PDF 396 \"deferred\"): added to the endurance entry.\n\nNo new entry was needed.\n\n## Grep proof\n```\nexplosive: 9:confidence: contested\nexplosive: 23:ends just past the month 7/8 tick, early in month 8\nexplosive: 23:already rising in month 3, while block 1 is still running\nexplosive: 23:They peak at about month 5, just inside phase II\nexplosive: 23:rises through the rest of block 2 to about its first-peak level\nexplosive: 23:GPP ends above Speed at the right edge\nexplosive: 29:does not name jumping or throwing as narrow-target sports, and it does not reject the model for them\nexplosive: 29:it counts against transferring it to American football\nexplosive: 29:for team sports the reviewed studies \"did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance\" (brief, p. 6)\ntricyclic: 9:confidence: contested\ntricyclic: 24:list boxing and swimming as sports that traditionally use tri-cyclic forms but may use bi-cyclic ones\ntricyclic: 26:Contested by Issurin 2016 inside the model's stated domain\ntwo-meso: 22:A terminology tension inside the book, not two opposed positions\ntwo-meso: 28:the basis for the `contested` rating\ntwo-meso: 28:does not address how many mesocycles a year should hold\ntwo-meso: 28:peaking 7 to 11 times\ncomplex: 19:Separately, \"Parallel loading of different emphasis\ncomplex: 29:not on the critique\ncomplex: 29:no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\ncomplex: 29:non-systematic, author-curated selection\nspeed: 22:one rises steadily and keeps rising (ending at S-E)\nspeed: 22:latter part of the block\nspeed: 22:which of the two below-axis curves ends at Vmax\nmicro: 20:crosses 100% at about 38-40 h\nmicro: 27:about 35 h (VO2 max, aerobic week)\nmicro: 29:crosses 100% at about 38-40 h\nmicro: 29:is consistent with the figure\nmicro: 29:currently scant experimental findings\ntabachnik: 32:15 m cannot take the same time as 5 m\ntabachnik: 32:much larger than the other steps\nunload: 18:latency period of 1-3 weeks\nunload: 20:rule 2 of [[heuristic--time-technique\nunload: 26:[[heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window]], [[periodization\nantonova: 26:Levchenko's subjects were \"highly qualified sprinters\"\nantonova: 26:Source note on dates\nantonova: 30:[[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]] (6.10\nprolonged: 20:is comparable in length to the model's 4-5 month competition period\nfollow: 27:Inference: the caption is the likelier error\nendurance: 26:the optimal date for increasing the current adaptation reserves is deferred\nendurance: 35:reads the two passages as a terminology tension\ndescriptive: 32:2 series of 4 x 400m\ndescriptive: 33:Bmass + 15kg\ndescriptive: 34:Standing quintuple jump (cm)\n```\nI also checked 18 old phrases (such as \"about 30-36 h\", \"Contested inside the book.\" and \"reported no effect\"): each is in 0 of the 6d files. Across the 25 files there are no em dashes outside section headers, every file has its 6 headers, no wikilink is wrapped or unresolved, and the confidence tally is 5 contested, 19 supported, 1 untested.\n\n## Lint\n```\n$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 132, 133]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--functional-training-means-sport-specific-and-no-exercise-is-non-functional.md: unresolved [[claim--core-stability-balance-training-has-no-shown-transfer-to-sport]]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md: unresolved [[heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies]]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md: unresolved [[framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set]]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md: unresolved [[heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies]]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 5\n```\nNone of the five lines is a 6d file. The `_book.md` line is the known false positive. The other four are Chapter 7 files that another batch is writing into the same folder; an earlier run this session, before they appeared, gave TOTAL ISSUES: 1.\n\n## For you\n- **Endurance entry (GO, `supported`):** brief p. 6 names swimming, so under the same domain test that entry might need `contested`. I left it alone because it wasn't flagged.\n- **Links I did not add (outside my flagged files):** `heuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions` from two GO planning entries, and return links from the two 6c entries (both listed in the log).\n- **6c link targets** existed when I added the links.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>232758</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>64</tool_uses><duration_ms>1098099</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
