{"parentUuid":"95cc123f-08fe-432c-ac47-b6faeb55c7e5","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PreToolUse:Agent","toolUseID":"toolu_01Jffcu4LoY5io8sZv3qQ1Ni","hookEvent":"PreToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"PreToolUse\",\"updatedInput\":{\"description\":\"Review Supertraining 6d round 2\",\"prompt\":\"You are the round-2 independent reviewer in Flint's ingest review gate. You did not write or fix these entries. Your job is to FALSIFY: check that the round-1 fixes actually landed and are correct, and that no fix introduced a new error. Read-only on entries and sources. Write only your report file.\\n\\nRead first:\\n- Instructions and output format: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_book_prompt.md` (Windows paths; Mac vault is `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`, baseline `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/method-baseline.md`).\\n- Standards: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md`.\\n- Round-1 review: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6d_opus.md`.\\n- Fix log (fixer's claims can overstate; verify in the files): `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6d_applied.md`, including the 7 disagreements and the \\\"domain rule\\\" used for confidence.\\n- The vault Issurin 2016 brief in `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/research-papers/` (grep \\\"issurin-2016\\\"), and Issurin's book entries under `strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin/` where the fix log cites them (stage length about two months, 4-7 per season, jumpers peaking 7-11 times).\\n\\nEntries: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`, scope list `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6d.txt` (25 files). Chapter 7 files are being written into the same folder; ignore them.\\n\\nSource: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx (0-based) = printed + 11, covered PDF 379-403 (plus 305). Text: `~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \\\"import pymupdf; d=pymupdf.open('<pdf>'); print(d[N].get_text())\\\"`. Render to `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_renders/r2_6d_pN.png` (dpi=400 for Figs 6.39, 6.32b, 6.36b) and open with Read.\\n\\nScope of this round:\\n1. The explosive-strength model entry, in full and with the most care (it matters most for Eric's football work): read Fig 6.39 yourself and check every curve, block and phase statement; every Issurin quote verbatim in the brief or Issurin entries; the football-transfer scoping correct; nothing claims more than the brief says (the fixer reports the brief does NOT say \\\"jumping-type sports\\\"; confirm).\\n2. The other 10 files round 1 marked FIX, in full: each problem gone, each fixer disagreement justified, nothing new wrong. Read Figs 6.32b and 6.36b yourself.\\n3. Confidence consistency: the fixer applied a \\\"domain rule\\\" (contested when a library source disagrees inside the sport domain the entry claims) to the four Issurin-related entries. Judge whether it is applied consistently, and whether the long-duration endurance entry (`supported`, a round-1 GO) should be `contested` under the same rule because the brief names swimming. Say which, with the brief's page.\\n4. Spot check at least 4 of the round-1 GO entries against the pages.\\n5. All wikilinks in the 25 files resolve (staging or vault). Every file: six section headers, no em dashes outside headers, no wrapped links, no review-history or process wording.\\n\\nWrite `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6d_opus_round2.md`. Final reply: overall verdict (PROMOTE / GO AFTER FIXES with list / NO-GO), GO/FIX/NO-GO counts, which GO entries you spot-checked, and your call on the endurance entry.\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <deferred_tool_bootstrap>\\n    The context-mode tools below may be DEFERRED in your harness — their schemas are not loaded yet, so calling them directly fails (e.g. \\\"tool not found\\\" / InputValidationError). Load them ONCE before your first ctx_* call:\\n    ToolSearch(query: \\\"select:mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\\\")\\n    After that they are callable. If any ctx_* call fails as not-found, ToolSearch it and retry — do NOT fall back to Bash/Read just because the schema was not loaded yet.\\n  </deferred_tool_bootstrap>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\\n  </when_not_to_use>\\n\\n  <file_writing_policy>\\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\\n  </file_writing_policy>\\n\\n  <output_constraints>\\n    <artifact_policy>\\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\\n    </artifact_policy>\\n  </output_constraints>\\n  <session_continuity>\\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\\n  </session_continuity>\\n\\n</context_window_protection>\",\"subagent_type\":\"general-purpose\",\"model\":\"opus\"}}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/pretooluse.mjs\"","durationMs":93},"type":"attachment","uuid":"b5f9171e-dc1e-4da2-96eb-185b40f88b6f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:33.209Z","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
