{"parentUuid":"4eab9b33-935d-4298-b305-cfcaf37c65c0","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ce914f6b-f81b-42fd-a40b-4c5550786125","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LzKJivhEXy3YjGDjsEuPQd","type":"tool_result","content":"ok principle--long-term-delayed-training-ef\nok heuristic--within-session-order-of-explo\nok heuristic--within-session-order-of-explo\n0","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e073fe07-9527-482f-97d1-9d6e629d18e7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:08:26.054Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"ok principle--long-term-delayed-training-ef\nok heuristic--within-session-order-of-explo\nok heuristic--within-session-order-of-explo\n0","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"returnCodeInterpretation":"No matches found","noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6c_applied.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":140,"oldLines":3,"newStart":140,"newLines":9,"lines":[" ```"," "," No 6c file appears in the lint output. The one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes), outside scope.","+","+## Coordinator fixes after round 2","+","+Round 2 (`review_supertraining_ch6c_opus_round2.md`): GO AFTER FIXES, 17 GO / 2 FIX. Both applied, each checked on the page text:","+1. LDTE principle: \"which reconciles those two readings\" is now a labelled Inference.","+2. Within-session heuristic: PDF 288 now quoted in full (\"even more effectively than plyometrics alone, provided ...\"), replacing \"works best\". PDF 299 now carries the page's own explanation (novices' \"lower level of special fitness\") and the untested well-trained hypothesis, with the novice vs trained split labelled Inference. `contested` kept."]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Building a contrast or complex session for explosive strength from one of the listed pairs: settle the volume and the contrast between the two means first, then keep the listed order as a secondary choice. Inference: when equipment or space forces the order to flip on a given day, the authors' text says little is lost within the session. Eric's baseline: \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 104, Inferred (transcript + slides))."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book.** On PDF p. 371 order is ranked below volume and contrast but still to be kept (\"fairly unimportant,\" then \"Nevertheless, one should still preserve the above order\"): a hedge, not a contradiction. The book contradicts itself across chapters on within-session order. PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277: resistance plus plyometrics works best \"provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\" (checked against the page image). PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288, novices: \"There was no statistically significant difference\" between strength-first and speed-strength-first within the workout (checked against the page image; [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]). PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289, \"Research with novices and athletes of average qualification\": \"Least effective is the sequence of strength followed by speed-strength means during the same session\" (checked against the page image). Inference: reading barbell work as \"strength\" and jumping as \"speed-strength\" (Fig 5.13 on the same page labels the jumping means \"Jumps\"), this opposes p. 371's \"preserve the above order\" for \"barbell exercises and jumping exercises.\" The PDF p. 301 bullets (\"Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises in training\"; \"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before\") are stage-level: they sit under \"The following principles for the sequential use of training means over a given period emerge\" (PDF p. 300), so they belong with [[heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive]], not here. Keep this within-session rule separate from the long-term sequence across stages, where the authors say order does matter: extensive submaximal resistance first, then explosive work, and \"The reverse sequence of this loading scheme leads to a negative cumulative effect\" (PDF p. 372). Inference: Issurin's session rules put the dominant or technical task first and add other modalities after it ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout]]), a stricter view of order than this passage. The authors say endurance interactions are a different case, where order clearly matters ([[framework--positive-and-negative-sequences-of-endurance-loading]]).","+**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book.** On PDF p. 371 order is ranked below volume and contrast but still to be kept (\"fairly unimportant,\" then \"Nevertheless, one should still preserve the above order\"): a hedge, not a contradiction. The book contradicts itself across chapters on within-session order. PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277: periodised programmes combining resistance with plyometrics \"can develop explosive strength even more effectively than plyometrics alone, provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\" (checked against the page image). PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288, novices: \"There was no statistically significant difference\" between strength-first and speed-strength-first within the workout (checked against the page image; [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]); the same page attributes this to the novices' \"lower level of special fitness\" and says \"A well-trained athlete can expect the preceding strength work to have a positive effect,\" a hypothesis it says can be established \"only by\" further experiment. Inference: so the conflict is sharpest for novices and average athletes, and untested for trained athletes. PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289, \"Research with novices and athletes of average qualification\": \"Least effective is the sequence of strength followed by speed-strength means during the same session\" (checked against the page image). Inference: reading barbell work as \"strength\" and jumping as \"speed-strength\" (Fig 5.13 on the same page labels the jumping means \"Jumps\"), this opposes p. 371's \"preserve the above order\" for \"barbell exercises and jumping exercises.\" The PDF p. 301 bullets (\"Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises in training\"; \"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before\") are stage-level: they sit under \"The following principles for the sequential use of training means over a given period emerge\" (PDF p. 300), so they belong with [[heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive]], not here. Keep this within-session rule separate from the long-term sequence across stages, where the authors say order does matter: extensive submaximal resistance first, then explosive work, and \"The reverse sequence of this loading scheme leads to a negative cumulative effect\" (PDF p. 372). Inference: Issurin's session rules put the dominant or technical task first and add other modalities after it ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout]]), a stricter view of order than this passage. The authors say endurance interactions are a different case, where order clearly matters ([[framework--positive-and-negative-sequences-of-endurance-loading]])."," "," **Example** — The authors list barbell exercises at 30% and 90% of maximum as one positive pairing (PDF p. 371). Inference: a session pairing heavy back squats with light jump squats, done as alternating series with active rest and with exercise variants rotated on repeat days, would follow the rule; the authors give no sets, reps or rest times here."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":30,"oldLines":7,"newStart":30,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Planning a concentrated strength block for an advanced athlete when the aim is a later rise in speed-strength, accepting a planned dip during the block. Inference: a dip in speed-strength during such a block is the expected path, not by itself a signal to stop. Eric's baseline: \"Recovery is managed through training-load design alone; nothing objective is tracked, sleep included\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 153, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q23)). Inference: the LDTE rests on one load-design move, the timed volume cut, so it fits that practice. Eric's baseline: \"The mRSI decision read happens on Wednesdays, against individualized thresholds\" (line 75, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q2, Q2a)). Inference: during a deliberate block, an mRSI drop below an athlete's usual band would be expected; the authors' own rule separates a dip \"within an optimal range\" from the F3 case, but gives no threshold for telling them apart."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — The book gives no numbers for \"optimum,\" \"within an optimal range\" or \"excessive,\" and says the effect is individual (PDF p. 375). Figs 6.29-6.31 carry no sample sizes or statistics; Fig 6.31 is a \"general scheme,\" not data. The 5-12 weeks on PDF p. 374 is the length of the loading stages; the 4-12 weeks on PDF p. 375 is how long the LDTE lasts. They are different quantities. The book uses 4-12 weeks for three things: how long unidirectional training stays effective (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image), how long the LDTE lasts (PDF p. 375), and, in Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, translated by Yessis; PDF p. 515), the length of the concentrated stages themselves: \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks\" (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525, checked against the page image). Both PDF p. 375 and the Addendum (PDF p. 536) say the LDTE lasts about as long as the loading stage (T2 about equal to T1), which reconciles those two readings; the PDF p. 305 figure is a separate quantity. Keep the locator with the number. Inference: in-season, when performance is needed every week, a planned multi-week dip conflicts with Eric's baseline that \"in football you want to peak every Saturday\" (line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)); this is an off-season tool. The book's claim that the dip comes without \"marked signs of overtraining\" sits beside Bompa's framing of concentrated loading as planned overreaching ([[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]]); Inference: that is a difference of labels, not of the observed curve.","+**When NOT to apply** — The book gives no numbers for \"optimum,\" \"within an optimal range\" or \"excessive,\" and says the effect is individual (PDF p. 375). Figs 6.29-6.31 carry no sample sizes or statistics; Fig 6.31 is a \"general scheme,\" not data. The 5-12 weeks on PDF p. 374 is the length of the loading stages; the 4-12 weeks on PDF p. 375 is how long the LDTE lasts. They are different quantities. The book uses 4-12 weeks for three things: how long unidirectional training stays effective (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image), how long the LDTE lasts (PDF p. 375), and, in Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, translated by Yessis; PDF p. 515), the length of the concentrated stages themselves: \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks\" (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525, checked against the page image). Both PDF p. 375 and the Addendum (PDF p. 536) say the LDTE lasts about as long as the loading stage (T2 about equal to T1). Inference: this reconciles those two readings, and the PDF p. 305 figure is a separate quantity. Keep the locator with the number. Inference: in-season, when performance is needed every week, a planned multi-week dip conflicts with Eric's baseline that \"in football you want to peak every Saturday\" (line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)); this is an off-season tool. The book's claim that the dip comes without \"marked signs of overtraining\" sits beside Bompa's framing of concentrated loading as planned overreaching ([[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]]); Inference: that is a difference of labels, not of the observed curve."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: in sprinters (Fig 6.29), about 2.5 months of concentrated strength loading drove starting, explosive and absolute strength to about 85-96% of initial; by month 4 they stood at about 112-125% (values read from the image, PDF p. 374)."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6c_applied.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4eab9b33-935d-4298-b305-cfcaf37c65c0","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
