{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:58.175Z","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ae553e10f4940b5ab</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01T2v2tiA2PGxxniikrasjKT</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/ae553e10f4940b5ab.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Atomize Supertraining 7b\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I finished batch 7b with 14 new entries, which completes Chapter 7 at PDF 431. Lint is clean on all 14 files. I added files only: no existing entry or `_book.md` was edited, I made no vault edits and ran no git.\n\n**Entries by kind and confidence**\n- Framework, 5: three `supported` (PNF five P-factors, PNF activation and relaxation techniques, hypertrophy observations) and two `untested` (accelerated powermetrics, which are written up only for the bench press and squat).\n- Claim, 5: two `supported` (trunk rotation from the obliques, time under tension) and three `untested` (novices progress from mobility to stability, \"functional training\" means sport-specific, core stability training).\n- Heuristic, 2: one `supported` (heel raises with inversion) and one `untested` (pulleys and dumbbells for PNF patterns).\n- Principle, 1, `untested`: train for a safety factor above competition forces.\n- Case study, 2: the Fradd 1995 elbow-flexion study is `untested`. The box squat with bands is `contested`, because Frost 2010 (review p. 21) says transfer to sport was not demonstrated and most acute studies found no difference from load-matched free weights. Rhea 2009 points the other way, and the entry records both.\n\n**Entry count per section (for `_book.md`)**\n\n| Section | Printed pp | PDF idx | Entries |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 7.2 PNF (7.2.1-7.2.5) | 403-411 | 414-422 | 6 (PDF 419-420 are figure-only) |\n| 7.3 Combinations of Resistance Methods | 411-416 | 422-427 | 3 |\n| 7.4 Muscle Training (7.4.1-7.4.2) | 416-418 | 427-429 | 3 |\n| 7.5 Use of Strength Training Compendium | 418-419 | 429-430 | 0 on its own; its pec-deck vs cable-crossover example and stretching point are folded into the PNF framework, pulley heuristic and techniques entries |\n| 7.6 Training for Hypertrophy? | 419-420 | 430-431 | 2 |\n\nThe 7.4.1 joint-movement list and the 7.5 compendium list were not atomized, as instructed.\n\n**Where I stopped:** at the end of Chapter 7, PDF 431. Chapter 8 starts at PDF 432 / printed 421. The top of PDF 414 (items 13-28 and \"Concluding Remarks\") belongs to 7.1, so I left it to the other atomizer. Section 7.2 starts mid-page on PDF 414.\n\n**Voice**\n- **7.2 is Siff's.** PDF 430 / printed 419 says his 1989 NSCA workshop \"acted as the basis for Chapter 7.2,\" and PDF 429 says \"the author was invited ... (Siff, 1989)\".\n- **7.3 is mixed.** PDF 422 has \"The author has used\" next to the Siff and Simmons photo. PDF 423 refers to \"one of Siff's ... students\" in the third person, so I wrote that passage as the authors'. The powermetrics passage is first person (\"Let me\", PDF 426); I marked it as probably Siff but not named on the page.\n- **7.6 quotes unnamed commentators.** I labelled their words as quotes, not as the authors' statements.\n\n**Unclear or contradictory spots** (all recorded in the entries with their pages)\n- **Power-load range:** \"about 60-70 percent\" (PDF 422 / printed 411) against \"50-70 percent of one's 1RM\" (PDF 427 / printed 416).\n- **Table 7.1 against the text:** peak power is 306 W against 129 and 131 W, about 2.4 times. The text says \"some three times greater\" (PDF 423-424).\n- **Fig 7.10 against the text:** free-weight force peaks at about 20-40% of the movement, but the text says \"near the beginning\".\n- **Fig 7.12:** the double force peak described in the text is not visible in the printed curve.\n- **7.3 opening against the data:** the opening says greatest force comes \"after the joint has passed through the midpoint\". The study's own free-weight data put force highest early, and Zatsiorsky describes three strength-curve shapes.\n- **Proximal and distal sequences:** PDF 416 gives both directions, principle 18 says distal to proximal for mature subjects, and PDF 418 says proximal to distal for novices.\n- **Other source oddities:** \"Starling's Law\" is used for the stretch reflex (PDF 417); \"brachialis and brachialis\" appears on PDF 427.\n- **Ankle lists:** 7.4.1's inversion list leaves out tibialis posterior, but 7.4.2 names it; flexor hallucis longus is missing from the plantarflexion list (PDF 429).\n- **Uncited claims:** that multifidus is \"the only\" back rotator (PDF 429), and the extension of Wolff's law to \"functional training\" (PDF 422).\n- **No library source to check against:** nothing in the vault or staging covers core stability, so that claim stays `untested`.\n\n**What I read from images versus text**\n- I opened page images for PDF 419-431. Table 7.1, Figs 7.3-7.8 and 7.10-7.13, and every number cited come from those images.\n- I rendered PDF 414-418 but did not open them; quotes from those pages are text-layer only, and the entries say so.\n- The Fig 7.9 photo is not cited.\n- Figs 7.10-7.12 plot all curves on one unlabelled axis, so velocity and acceleration heights cannot be read as real values.\n\n**Reciprocal-link suggestions** (vault or existing staging files; I made none of these)\n- Irradiation claim, stretch-reflex framework, stretching taxonomy, voluntary-relaxation framework and the four-methods ROM framework → the PNF framework and techniques entries.\n- `heuristic--nfr-machine-limitations-safety-and-appropriate-use`, `framework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines` and the equip-circuits heuristic → the pulley heuristic.\n- Muscle-isolation claim, multiarticular claim and movement-matrix framework → the core-stability and trunk-rotation entries.\n- Inertial and elastic devices framework, CAT framework and Zatsiorsky's joint strength curves → the box-squat and Fradd case studies.\n- Frost 2010 and Rhea 2009 briefs → the box squat.\n- Jandacka 2008, Handford 2024 and the 3.3.2 power-load claim → powermetrics.\n- Bompa's progressive-overload threshold, the differential-tissue claim and the sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic framework → the two hypertrophy entries.\n- Moyer's plantar-flexor claim → the heel-raise heuristic.\n- Functional-vs-structural framework → the functional-training claim.\n\n**Lint output**\n```\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 132, 133]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\nThat one issue is in `_book.md`, which I did not touch; none of my files was flagged. Each of my files has exactly 6 em dashes, all in the section headers, and no wikilink wraps across lines.\n\nThe scope file is `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch7b.txt`, and the entries are in `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`. Page renders are in `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_renders/7b_p*.png`.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>248119</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>67</tool_uses><duration_ms>816663</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
