{"parentUuid":"141237d8-5563-4206-ab31-053dc2b31b67","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffr78QsdtYuXuBiCZ65q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014FRMXhEgXQjf3owkgQpzZd","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nE=[\n(\"framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports.md\",\"confidence: supported\",\"confidence: contested\"),\n(\"framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports.md\",\n \"The models are for qualified athletes and carry the same Issurin 2016 challenge to concentrated loading as the other 6.14 models ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]).\",\n \"The models are for qualified athletes. **Contested by Issurin 2016 inside this model's own domain (the basis for the `contested` rating):** the book counts swimming among the cyclic sports where the delayed effect of concentrated strength blocks was observed (PDF p. 374) and scopes the medium-duration model to \\\"cyclic sports of medium duration\\\" (PDF p. 399), while the library's brief of Issurin 2016 judges the evidence for concentrated loading \\\"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\\\" (brief p. 6). Compare [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], where Issurin 2016 counts only against transfer to football.\"),\n(\"claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md\",\n \"**Contested by Issurin on the remedy and on the beginners limit, not on the critique (the basis for the `contested` rating).**\",\n \"**Contested by Issurin on the remedy and on the beginners limit, not on the critique (the basis for the `contested` rating).** On the beginners limit the contest rests on the 2016 review only: Issurin's own 2008 book agrees with this book that the traditional complex model \\\"has visible benefits for the preparation of low- and mid-level athletes\\\" (Block Periodization PDF p. 44 / printed p. 33; see [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]]).\"),\n(\"claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation.md\",\n \"A terminology tension inside the book, not two opposed positions.\",\n \"Inference: a terminology tension inside the book, not two opposed positions.\"),\n]\nfor f,o,n in E:\n    p=Path(f);s=p.read_text();assert s.count(o)==1,(f,o[:30]);p.write_text(s.replace(o,n));print(\"ok\",f[:45])\nEOF\ngrep -c \"swimming preparation\" ~/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/research-papers/issurin-2016*.md; cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \"\nimport pymupdf;d=pymupdf.open('staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf')\nprint('swim' in d[374].get_text().lower(), 'cyclic sports of medium duration' in d[399].get_text())\"","description":"Apply 3 6d fixes; verify quotes on pages and brief"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1148,"cache_read_input_tokens":436505,"output_tokens":1354,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":156},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1148,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1354,"cache_read_input_tokens":436505,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1148,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1148},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_014FRMXhEgXQjf3owkgQpzZd":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky && python3 - <<'EOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nE=[\n(\"framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports.md\",\"confidence: supported\",\"confidence: contested\"),\n(\"framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports.md\",\n \"The models are for qualified athletes and carry the same Issurin 2016 challenge to concentrated loading as the other 6.14 models ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]).\",\n \"The models are for qualified athletes. **Contested by Issurin 2016 inside this model's own domain (the basis for the `contested` rating):** the book counts swimming among the cyclic sports where the delayed effect of concentrated strength blocks was observed (PDF p. 374) and scopes the medium-duration model to \\\"cyclic sports of medium duration\\\" (PDF p. 399), while the library's brief of Issurin 2016 judges the evidence for concentrated loading \\\"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\\\" (brief p. 6). Compare [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], where Issurin 2016 counts only against transfer to football.\"),\n(\"claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md\",\n \"**Contested by Issurin on the remedy and on the beginners limit, not on the critique (the basis for the `contested` rating).**\",\n \"**Contested by Issurin on the remedy and on the beginners limit, not on the critique (the basis for the `contested` rating).** On the beginners limit the contest rests on the 2016 review only: Issurin's own 2008 book agrees with this book that the traditional complex model \\\"has visible benefits for the preparation of low- and mid-level athletes\\\" (Block Periodization PDF p. 44 / printed p. 33; see [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]]).\"),\n(\"claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation.md\",\n \"A terminology tension inside the book, not two opposed positions.\",\n \"Inference: a terminology tension inside the book, not two opposed positions.\"),\n]\nfor f,o,n in E:\n    p=Path(f);s=p.read_text();assert s.count(o)==1,(f,o[:30]);p.write_text(s.replace(o,n));print(\"ok\",f[:45])\nEOF\ngrep -c \"swimming preparation\" ~/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/research-papers/issurin-2016*.md; cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \"\nimport pymupdf;d=pymupdf.open('staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf')\nprint('swim' in d[374].get_text().lower(), 'cyclic sports of medium duration' in d[399].get_text())\"","description":"Apply 3 6d fixes; verify quotes on pages and brief"}},"wireIngestContext":{"toolu_014FRMXhEgXQjf3owkgQpzZd":{"cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffr77eFRPnd24rxzrLVV","type":"assistant","uuid":"86c745e7-ccf0-4395-a86c-aad7d44ff9d8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:22:07.783Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"86c745e7-ccf0-4395-a86c-aad7d44ff9d8","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"189515d2-55e8-482a-8b52-f518760b72bc","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014FRMXhEgXQjf3owkgQpzZd","type":"tool_result","content":"ok framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-\nok framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-\nok claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highl\nok claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-\n2\nTrue True","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"7c62efc8-7564-4faa-b017-597e8a11eb2c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:22:08.781Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"ok framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-\nok framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-\nok claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highl\nok claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-\n2\nTrue True","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":26,"oldLines":7,"newStart":26,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Deciding whether a well-trained athlete's week should mix every quality in each session or give whole sessions and whole weeks to one emphasis. Eric's baseline: \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 182, Inferred (transcript + slides)). Inference: the baseline and the authors agree that not everything is trained hard at once; the baseline still trains other qualities \"at a retention dose,\" closer to Issurin's multi-targeted blocks than to a single emphasis."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin on the remedy and on the beginners limit, not on the critique (the basis for the `contested` rating).** Issurin agrees that traditional complex training falls short for elite athletes: the library's brief of Issurin 2016 says the review \"repeats the book's critique of traditional planning,\" where \"the book\" is Issurin's own Block Periodization ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]], pp. 6-7, 11). Inference: traditional planning there means developing many abilities at once, the same complex organisation this entry's authors criticise. He contests the remedy: his multi-targeted blocks train a minimal number of targets, not one ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]]). He also contests who the concentrated approach is for: the brief records \"no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\" with highly concentrated block workloads (brief, p. 11), against the book's view that complex loading is \"applicable only to beginners or athletes of average qualification\" (PDF p. 380). The brief reports 28 studies supporting the multi-targeted model, but they are Issurin's own non-systematic, author-curated selection, with varied designs, populations, outcomes and comparators (brief, p. 11), not a pooled estimate. Inside the book there is a caveat a few pages later: a swimming week built from single-emphasis workouts of different kinds still \"typifies the complex (multi-faceted) form of training organisation\" and \"fails to produce a definite increase in speed, aerobic or anaerobic productivity\" (PDF pp. 387-388 / printed pp. 376-377; see [[case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength]]). So single-emphasis workouts alone are not enough; the authors want the emphasis held across a microcycle or stage. Unidirectional loading \"is effective only if it forms part of a complex of several means of a single primary emphasis involving a variety of methods\" (PDF p. 381). The argument is for highly qualified athletes; for beginners the authors keep complex training.","+**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin on the remedy and on the beginners limit, not on the critique (the basis for the `contested` rating).** On the beginners limit the contest rests on the 2016 review only: Issurin's own 2008 book agrees with this book that the traditional complex model \"has visible benefits for the preparation of low- and mid-level athletes\" (Block Periodization PDF p. 44 / printed p. 33; see [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]]). Issurin agrees that traditional complex training falls short for elite athletes: the library's brief of Issurin 2016 says the review \"repeats the book's critique of traditional planning,\" where \"the book\" is Issurin's own Block Periodization ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]], pp. 6-7, 11). Inference: traditional planning there means developing many abilities at once, the same complex organisation this entry's authors criticise. He contests the remedy: his multi-targeted blocks train a minimal number of targets, not one ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]]). He also contests who the concentrated approach is for: the brief records \"no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\" with highly concentrated block workloads (brief, p. 11), against the book's view that complex loading is \"applicable only to beginners or athletes of average qualification\" (PDF p. 380). The brief reports 28 studies supporting the multi-targeted model, but they are Issurin's own non-systematic, author-curated selection, with varied designs, populations, outcomes and comparators (brief, p. 11), not a pooled estimate. Inside the book there is a caveat a few pages later: a swimming week built from single-emphasis workouts of different kinds still \"typifies the complex (multi-faceted) form of training organisation\" and \"fails to produce a definite increase in speed, aerobic or anaerobic productivity\" (PDF pp. 387-388 / printed pp. 376-377; see [[case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength]]). So single-emphasis workouts alone are not enough; the authors want the emphasis held across a microcycle or stage. Unidirectional loading \"is effective only if it forms part of a complex of several means of a single primary emphasis involving a variety of methods\" (PDF p. 381). The argument is for highly qualified athletes; for beginners the authors keep complex training."," "," **Example** — The authors' own example of a complex unit: a workout of \"resistance training, plyometrics and sprinting,\" or a week using those means on different days (PDF p. 379). Their single-emphasis examples are the swimmer and jumper microcycles of Kudelin (1980) and Mironenko (1981) (PDF p. 381)."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," Fig 6.34 (PDF p. 387, read from the image, no numeric scale): two shaded strength-loading waves, V1 and V2. The fitness-state curve dips during V1, rises to a peak between the waves, dips again during V2, and then climbs to a level above the first peak at the right edge."," ","-**A terminology tension inside the book, not two opposed positions.** The book uses \"two large mesocycles\" and \"bi-cyclic\" interchangeably at PDF p. 387 (\"this form of bi-cyclic organisation\"), but keeps them apart at PDF p. 389, which asks for two large mesocycles \"even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period and use monocyclic periodisation,\" and at PDF p. 396 / printed p. 385, where the monocyclic long-duration model, with \"one competitive stage,\" still provides \"a two-stage organisation of strength and speed training.\" Read with PDF pp. 389 and 396, the passages answer different questions: how many large mesocycles a year, and how many competition peaks. The apparent conflict with \"in long-duration endurance sports, monocyclic periodisation is usually recommended\" (PDF p. 387) comes from the PDF p. 387 wording. See [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]].","+**Inference: a terminology tension inside the book, not two opposed positions.** The book uses \"two large mesocycles\" and \"bi-cyclic\" interchangeably at PDF p. 387 (\"this form of bi-cyclic organisation\"), but keeps them apart at PDF p. 389, which asks for two large mesocycles \"even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period and use monocyclic periodisation,\" and at PDF p. 396 / printed p. 385, where the monocyclic long-duration model, with \"one competitive stage,\" still provides \"a two-stage organisation of strength and speed training.\" Read with PDF pp. 389 and 396, the passages answer different questions: how many large mesocycles a year, and how many competition peaks. The apparent conflict with \"in long-duration endurance sports, monocyclic periodisation is usually recommended\" (PDF p. 387) comes from the PDF p. 387 wording. See [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]]."," "," **Why it works** — The authors' reason is supercompensation of the \"current adaptation reserves\" and the claim that the second mesocycle builds on the first. They cite no data for the second peak being higher beyond \"research shows\" and long empirical use."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":6,"oldLines":7,"newStart":6,"newLines":7,"lines":[" chapter: \"6.14.2\""," topic: endurance annual models"," category: [endurance, periodization, annual-plan, concentrated-loading, bi-cyclic, monocyclic]","-confidence: supported","+confidence: contested"," ingest-tier: full-text"," created: 2026-10-03"," ---"]},{"oldStart":32,"oldLines":7,"newStart":32,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Annual planning for middle-distance (medium) or long-distance (long) endurance athletes who use strength blocks. Not a football model; the medium-duration sequence \"General endurance > Speed > Speed-endurance\" is the transferable idea. Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once\" and \"priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 115, Explicit; the two phrases are joined by a dash in the baseline). Inference: both models pick one energy-system emphasis per stage in that spirit."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Long-duration sports are given a monocycle, while the 6.13 aims call for \"at least two large mesocycles... even in sports which traditionally... use monocyclic periodisation\" (PDF p. 389); the monocycle here still provides \"a two-stage organisation of strength and speed training\" (PDF p. 396), and [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]] reads the two passages as a terminology tension (number of mesocycles versus number of competition peaks), not opposed positions. The models are for qualified athletes and carry the same Issurin 2016 challenge to concentrated loading as the other 6.14 models ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]). Fig 6.46's caption says \"A is the block of concentrated strength loading,\" but the figure prints \"GPP\" under A and \"Strength Load\" under B; which wave is the strength block is unclear.","+**When NOT to apply** — Long-duration sports are given a monocycle, while the 6.13 aims call for \"at least two large mesocycles... even in sports which traditionally... use monocyclic periodisation\" (PDF p. 389); the monocycle here still provides \"a two-stage organisation of strength and speed training\" (PDF p. 396), and [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]] reads the two passages as a terminology tension (number of mesocycles versus number of competition peaks), not opposed positions. The models are for qualified athletes. **Contested by Issurin 2016 inside this model's own domain (the basis for the `contested` rating):** the book counts swimming among the cyclic sports where the delayed effect of concentrated strength blocks was observed (PDF p. 374) and scopes the medium-duration model to \"cyclic sports of medium duration\" (PDF p. 399), while the library's brief of Issurin 2016 judges the evidence for concentrated loading \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" (brief p. 6). Compare [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], where Issurin 2016 counts only against transfer to football. Fig 6.46's caption says \"A is the block of concentrated strength loading,\" but the figure prints \"GPP\" under A and \"Strength Load\" under B; which wave is the strength block is unclear."," "," **Example** — The authors give the schematics only. Inference: an 800 m runner competes at 400 m and 1500 m in the first competition stage as control tests, then runs the main 800 m season in months 10-11 after a smaller second block focused on local muscular endurance."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"86c745e7-ccf0-4395-a86c-aad7d44ff9d8","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
