{"parentUuid":"ddfbb6ee-c70b-464f-bf26-75b10e167cc9","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ef194c20-d5f8-4d01-bce0-254fc908bea5","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a738c543bcd777bcb</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01BgG1uGh31vYAAXiTJiEkQL</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/a738c543bcd777bcb.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Fix Supertraining 7a entries\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I applied all 7 fixes, with small refinements to fixes 4, 5 and 7, and placed all 5 missing ideas without writing a new entry. Lint shows no problems in any 7a file. I edited only 7a files: 7 FIX entries plus the GO chart entry. I made no renames, the scope file is unchanged (10 lines), and I did not touch `_book.md`, 7b, 6d, the vault or git. The log is at `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch7a_applied.md`.\n\n## Per-fix outcome\nI checked each fix against the page image before applying it.\n1. **Maximal methods: applied.** The `contested` reason now covers only the rest between near-maximal singles. It adds the \"optimal recovery intervals between sets\" point (PDF 406) and the note that the two passages may describe different methods. Forced reps no longer appears as an injury-warned method: PDF 407 warns only about eccentrics and cheating. I also made two small consistency edits the review didn't ask for:\n   - \"two incompatible values\" in this entry now says the values \"may describe different methods\".\n   - The interval entry's restatement of the same conflict now says the same.\n2. **Three regimes: applied.** Rating is now `supported`, and the split/zone sentence is labelled Inference.\n3. **Set structures: applied.** Rating is now `supported`. The step-set purpose and \"not for a peaking block\" are labelled Inference.\n4. **Interval and cluster: applied, with one refinement.** The `contested` reason now leads with the 5RM minimum against the 4-6RM and the 10-20% strip, then the 75-90%. The review said both fall below the minimum. Only the 6RM end of 4-6RM does, so the entry says \"(a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM)\".\n5. **Hybrid: applied.** The book's 6.6.2 notation shows the superscript is the number of sets:\n   - PDF 335 lists \"60 x 10, 70 x 8, 80 x 6, 80 x 6\".\n   - PDF 336 writes the same sets as \"10(60)¹, 8(70)¹, 6(80)²\".\n\n   So 80³ over 3+5+2 means three sets, and the book's own \"for a set of 3 repetitions\" contradicts its notation. I cite PDF 335-336, not 336 alone. The time/space clause is now labelled Inference.\n6. **Segmental: applied.** The random variant is now used from the start of a block, which is the authors' preventive purpose (PDF 410).\n7. **Contrast: applied, with one refinement.** `contested` now covers only the heavy-light set. Load release and pneumatics are explicitly outside the conflict. Both Russian findings from PDF 175 are quoted word for word with their full citation lists. I did not use the review's \"Western studies that found none\": the same page reports Murray 1959 and Van Huss 1962 as positive. The entry now says the Western studies are split and the Russian studies found a boost.\n\n## Confidence changes\n- **Three regimes: `untested` to `supported`.** The book gives sets, reps, load and rest, calls it \"one of the best known elementary bodybuilding methods\", and says the add-ons are \"popularly used\" (PDF 408).\n- **Set structures: `untested` to `supported`.** They have parameters, plateau and stripping are \"commonly used\" (PDF 408), and the pyramid values come from Fig 1.9 equations (PDF 409).\n- **The other eight keep their ratings:**\n  - Hybrid stays `untested`: it gives reps but no load or rest, and the examples are left to \"the inventive coach\".\n  - Segmental stays `untested`: Twenty-Ones gives reps only, the random variant is a novelty the authors name themselves, and Rigert is an anecdote.\n  - Contrast stays `contested`: the conflict on PDF 175 outweighs its parameters.\n  - Chart, miscellaneous and sticking-points stay `untested` for the review's reasons.\n  - Maximal and interval stay `contested`.\n- **Tally:** `untested` 7 to 5, `supported` 0 to 2, `contested` 3 unchanged.\n\n## Missing ideas\nAll five went into the chart entry as quoted text:\n- **Superslow (PDF 405):** no entry anywhere mentions it. It links to `framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology`.\n- **Connective tissue first / low-intensity cyclical work (PDF 405):** connective tissue is already covered, so it links to `claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates` (Ch1 basis) and `principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue` (Ch6 rule). No entry covers the cyclical work. I quoted it rather than writing an entry: the book gives it in two sentences with no parameters, so a separate entry would need an invented \"When NOT\" and Example. You can overrule this.\n- **Active stretching (PDF 405):** links to the stretching taxonomy and eccentric quasi-isometrics entries.\n- **Sources and scope (PDF 404 and 414):** a new paragraph, plus a \"When NOT\" line saying the catalogue has not been tested.\n\nFive entries outside 7a could link back to the chart entry; they're listed in the log for you.\n\n## Grep proof (new wording)\n```\nframework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md:9:confidence: supported\nframework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md:31:Inference: step sets can find\nframework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md:33:Inference: not for a peaking block\nframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:29:most frequently used in the training of high-level\nframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:32:superslow method\nframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:33:very effective means of active stretching\nframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:34:Connective tissue first\nframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:35:capillarisation in all the relevant\nframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:41:Do not read the catalogue as tested\nframework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md:36:The rating applies only to the rest between near-maximal singles\nframework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md:36:optimal recovery intervals between sets\nframework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md:42:injury warnings for eccentrics and cheating\nframework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md:42:two values that may describe different methods\nframework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md:9:confidence: supported\nframework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md:37:Inference: split routines and zone training\nframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:25:The rating applies to the heavy-light set\nframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:25:Portnov, 1955; Ratov, 1957\nframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:25:Ermishkin &amp; Vozniak, 1965; Chuprun, 1966\nframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:25:the conflict does not reach them directly\nframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:29:the Western studies the book cites are split\nframework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md:21:By the 6.6.2 convention\nframework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md:21:three sets of the 10-repetition hybrid\nframework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md:27:Inference: also when session time or space\nframework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md:32:with one's 4-6RM\" (a 6RM is lighter\nframework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md:34:may describe different methods (sets of singles\nframework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md:42:the 10-20% strip and 75-90%\nheuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md:22:from the start of a block, to limit early accommodation\n```\n**Old phrases:** none of the old wording remains in any 7a file. One \"has stalled\" hit is the sticking-point entry's own, different sentence.\n\n**Other checks:** none of the 7a files has an em dash outside section headers, review-history wording, a wrapped wikilink or a CR line ending. All 10 have exactly 6 headers, and all 5 new link targets exist.\n\n## Lint\n```\n$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 152, 153]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\nThe one issue is a known false positive in `_book.md`, which is outside scope.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>197084</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>36</tool_uses><duration_ms>497465</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"},"uuid":"0652cfa8-b474-4349-8340-d1907dbd91e9","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:28:28.038Z","permissionMode":"bypassPermissions","origin":{"kind":"task-notification","producer":"session-task"},"promptSource":"system","turnOrigin":"task_notification","turnPosition":{"promptIndex":15,"turnIndex":55},"queueSkipAttachments":true,"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"0652cfa8-b474-4349-8340-d1907dbd91e9","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"UserPromptSubmit","toolUseID":"bcb2747e-230e-4e96-af28-d0df1173b365","hookEvent":"UserPromptSubmit","content":"<skill_relevance>\nRelevant to the current request: anthropic-skills:xlsx. Ignore this if it does not fit what the user actually asked for.\n</skill_relevance>","stdout":"<skill_relevance>\nRelevant to the current request: anthropic-skills:xlsx. Ignore this if it does not fit what the user actually asked for.\n</skill_relevance>\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"python3 \"$HOME/.claude/skill-router/router.py\"","durationMs":732},"type":"attachment","uuid":"982e572b-da27-4d66-80e0-478d4ae65698","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:28:28.775Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nUserPromptSubmit hook success: <skill_relevance>\nRelevant to the current request: anthropic-skills:xlsx. Ignore this if it does not fit what the user actually asked for.\n</skill_relevance>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:28:38.853Z","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>afcd6d73916c3b3e0</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01PgbYCjuHQJPRCN53F9ZPNF</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tasks/afcd6d73916c3b3e0.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Fix Supertraining 7b entries\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>All seven review fixes are applied, and the lint shows no issues in any 7b file. I checked each fix on the page images before making it. Fix 3 and Fix 4 were only partly right in the review, so I applied corrected versions. I edited only the 10 flagged 7b entries. The 4 GO entries, the 7a and 6d files, `_book.md` and the vault are untouched. No renames, no git.\n\nLog: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch7b_applied.md`\n\n**Per-fix outcome**\n1. **Siff attribution (applied, 6 files):** all six 7.2 entries now cite PDF 429 / p.418 for \"the author\" giving the 1989 NSCA talk (Siff, 1989), and PDF 430 / p.419 for \"acted as the basis for Chapter 7.2 above\". The PNF framework entry no longer says \"he states ... his\".\n2. **Principle 4 (applied):** \"exercising of related muscle groups (principle 4)\" is now in the PNF framework entry's movement-form group.\n3. **Activation entry (applied, wording corrected):** \"the latter [the main exercises]\" is fixed, and the Knott and Voss link is now to classical PNF (PDF 415). The review gave \"a few\" for contract-relax and \"several\" for slow-reversal-hold-relax. The page (PDF 417) gives contract-relax both: \"repeated several times\" and \"a few repetitions\". The entry now quotes both.\n4. **Fradd (applied, partly disagreed):** Fig 7.10 is on PDF 423, as the review says. But the \"near the beginning\" quote really is on PDF 424, so item (b) now carries both locators. The ratio reads \"about 2.3-2.4 times\" (306/129 = 2.37, 306/131 = 2.34), the plotted peaks read \"about 345 against about 115-125\", and the Fig 7.12 wording now says \"at most a faint shoulder at about 30%\".\n5. **Box squat (applied):** the Frost point now targets the effectiveness claim, not item 1. The item 4 mechanism is labelled \"Inference:\".\n6. **Powermetrics (applied):** \"physical-education students (55 men, 48 women)\", checked against the Jandacka vault brief, line 34.\n7. **Trunk (applied):** both pages are recorded (7.4.1 flexion and rotation lines on PDF 428; 7.4.2 on PDF 429). I did not set `contested`. Both sections answer the same question, but 7.4.1 is a list that leaves muscles out without saying \"only\", and 7.4.2 adds to it rather than reversing it. The heel-raise entry already handles the same pattern as `supported`. The reasoning is in the entry, labelled Inference.\n\n**Frost and Rhea against the book's claims (Fix 5)**\n- **Frost 2010** (vault brief, p.21) bears only on \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF 425): transfer to sport was not shown, and acute studies against load-matched free weights mostly found no difference. It does not test item 1, because 200 kg plus bands against 200 kg is not load-matched. Its point that bands may keep late-range force up is consistent with item 4.\n- **Rhea 2009** (vault brief, pp.4-5) bears on the same effectiveness claim, as a 12-week training result. Jump power rose 17.8% with bands, 11.0% fast-only and 4.8% slow; bands beat slow (p = .02) but not fast-only (p = .09), and strength did not differ. Statistical power was .67. It says nothing about the acute force items 1-4, and it cannot separate the bands from fast bar speed.\n- `contested` stays, limited to the effectiveness and transfer claim.\n\n**Confidence changes:** Fradd only, `untested` to `supported`. The entry limits that rating to the measured Table 7.1 comparison; every number read from the figures still says \"about\", and the entry makes no training-effect claim. Nothing else changed.\n\n**Missing ideas (no new entry; scope file still 14 lines)**\n\n| # | Idea | Where it went |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Errors in the 7.4.1 joint-action list | Trunk entry, When NOT, labelled as the writer's reading (Inference). Shoulder flexion \"posterior deltoid\" (PDF 428). Elbow note \"at the wrist\" (PDF 429): the book does mention the radius and ulna twisting, so only the place name is wrong. |\n| 2 | \"Bicep curls\" are not biceps-only (\"brachialis and brachialis\", PDF 427; elbow-flexion list, PDF 429) | PNF framework entry, beside the dumbbell-curl example |\n| 3 | Eyes leading the limbs, and deliberately resisting or provoking compensatory movements (PDF 418) | Novices entry, When to apply |\n| 4 | 7.5 list of PNF methods to add to gym training; physios using gym methods (PDF 429-430) | Pulleys entry, When to apply |\n| 5 | \"Even more forceful than\" the Ch 4.2.3 drop-and-catch methods (PDF 426) | Powermetrics entry, linked to the 4.2.3 kinetic-energy entry |\n\nI skipped the review's optional items, following the ch5b precedent. The log lists them.\n\n**Grep proof** (each count = matches in that file; the log also has extra rows for two late edits and the old-phrase checks)\n```\npnf-five-p-factors: [\"the author\" gave the 1989 NSCA presentation (Siff, 1989) (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418)] 1\npnf-five-p-factors: [exercising of related muscle groups (principle 4)] 1 | [brachialis and brachialis to differing degrees] 1\npnf-activation: [if the latter [the main exercises] were also] 1 | [with repetition counts only] 1 | [classical PNF (the Knott and Voss approach, PDF p. 415] 1\n[PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418, and that \"An overview] 1 in each of: activation, novices, pulleys, safety-factor, functional\nnovices: [eyes following the extremities] 1 | pulleys: [cheating to compel stronger muscles] 1\nfradd: [confidence: supported] 1 | [covers only the descriptive Table 7.1 comparison] 1 | [Fig 7.10's force curve (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412)] 1\nfradd: [about 2.3-2.4 times] 1 | [about 345 against about 115-125] 1 | [at most a faint shoulder at about 30%] 1\nfradd: [Table 7.1, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412; text, PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413] 1\nbox-squat: [Item 1 compares a heavier total load with free weight alone] 1 | [Inference: item 4 follows from band tension rising] 1 | [bears on the same effectiveness claim as a training outcome] 1\npowermetrics: [physical-education students (55 men, 48 women)] 1 | [even more forceful than the load dropping and catching methods] 2\ntrunk: [Bilateral action of rectus abdominis and psoas] 1 | [Source note on the 7.4.1 summary (Inference] 1 | [rates the claim `supported`] 1\nOld phrases: all 0 across the 14 files (\"confidence: untested\" is 0 in the Fradd file)\nMechanical checks (14 files): no em dashes in titles or body, no review-history words, no wrapped wikilinks, all 14 have the 6 section headers, no CR line endings\n```\n\n**Lint**\n```\n$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 152, 153]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\nThe only flag is the known false positive in `_book.md`, which I did not touch. Its line numbers differ from ch5b's because `_book.md` has changed since that run, not because of anything in this batch.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>199232</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>68</tool_uses><duration_ms>587823</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
