{"parentUuid":"609ce91d-35f6-41cc-93b3-6b06776979ae","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a90fffde-8683-4164-b9c5-6f51905bb539","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012bKiKfViwcFdd1qbPGR2Go","type":"tool_result","content":"33:| case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md | FIX | Scope of `supported` leaks. When NOT now limits `supported` to \"the descriptive Table 7.1 comparison ... not any training-effect claim\", but When to apply opens with an unlabelled prescription the authors do not make: \"When choosing a resistance mode for a goal: free weight for peak power, combined resistance for high force across the range.\" PDF 423-424 report measurements and say nothing about choosing a mode for a goal. \"High force across the range\" is the writer's reading of Fig 7.12. Minor: When NOT says \"(a) to (c) read from the page images\", but (a) is mainly Table 7.1 against the PDF 424 text. Figure readings, ratios, locators and table values all check (see above). | Change the opening sentence of When to apply to: \"Inference: when choosing a resistance mode for a goal, this one comparison points to free weight for peak power and combined resistance for high force across the range; the authors draw no such training rule from it.\" Optional: change \"(a) to (c) read from the page images\" to \"(a) table against text, (b) and (c) read from the page images\". |\n34:| case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md | FIX | (1) Rhea is misdescribed in a labelled Inference: \"Rhea gives partial support for bands in power training but does not separate the bands from the fast bar speed\". The design does separate them: Rhea has a fast-only arm, and fast-plus-bands against fast-only gave p = .09 (vault brief line 50, p. 5). What the study shows is that the band effect beyond fast squatting was not significant, with power .67. (2) Two sentences of the writer's own analysis are unlabelled: \"Item 1 compares a heavier total load with free weight alone, so it is not what Frost tests\" and \"Frost's acute summary (bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, vault brief, p. 21) does not contradict items 1-4.\" Neither is the authors' or Frost's statement. Everything else checks: Frost quote matches brief line 49; Rhea numbers match lines 46-53; book items 1-4, 200 / 150 / 250 kg, calibration and Fig 7.13 match PDF 425 image. | (1) Replace \"but does not separate the bands from the fast bar speed\" with \"but its fast-only arm shows no significant added effect of the bands (p = .09, statistical power .67), so it does not show that bands add to fast bar speed\". (2) Prefix \"Inference: \" to \"Item 1 compares a heavier total load ...\" and change \"Frost's acute summary (...) does not contradict items 1-4.\" to \"Inference: Frost's acute summary (...) does not contradict items 1-4.\" |\n35:| framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics.md | FIX | The new When NOT Inference widens the book's comparison. PDF 426: \"Let me now elaborate on how one can use **actively** accelerated powermetrics ... in a way which can be even more forceful than the load dropping and catching methods discussed in Ch 4.2.3. This type of training may be done in two voluntary forms\". The comparison covers the two active forms (3 and 4), and the book says \"can be\". Entry: \"the 'even more forceful ...' comparison ... puts these forms at or above the Ch 4.2.3 drop-and-catch methods in intensity, so they belong after those methods in a progression\". \"These forms\" reads as all four, including the band and partner forms. \"What it is\" places the quote correctly. Checked otherwise: Jandacka sample and 47-63% (brief lines 34, 48-49); four forms verbatim, 40-50 kg, PDF 426; Newton's Second Law, \"supramaximal\", 50-70%, PDF 427; 60-70%, PDF 422; 4.2.3 link target is chapter 4.2.3. | Replace \"puts these forms at or above the Ch 4.2.3 drop-and-catch methods in intensity, so they belong after those methods in a progression\" with \"says the two active forms (3 and 4) 'can be' more forceful than the Ch 4.2.3 drop-and-catch methods, so they belong at or after those methods in a progression\". |\n36:| claim--trunk-rotation-is-driven-by-the-obliques-not-the-erector-spinae.md | FIX | The new elbow-note correction is itself imprecise. Entry: \"the twisting it describes is at the radioulnar joints, so 'at the wrist' is the wrong place name.\" Pronation and supination happen at the proximal and distal radioulnar joints. The distal one lies at the wrist, so \"at the wrist\" is half right. The proximal one lies inside the elbow joint capsule, which is what makes the book's \"no rotation of the elbow joint as a whole\" misleading. Everything else checks: claims (1)-(4) verbatim on PDF 429; 7.4.1 flexion, lateral flexion and rotation lines on PDF 428; shoulder flexion \"posterior deltoid, upper trapezius (for scapular rotation)\" on PDF 428; \"Limitations of Anatomical Movement Analysis\" on PDF 427; `supported` decision (see section above). | Replace \"the twisting it describes is at the radioulnar joints, so \"at the wrist\" is the wrong place name\" with \"the twist happens at the proximal and distal radioulnar joints; the distal joint is at the wrist, but the proximal joint sits inside the elbow joint capsule, so \"no rotation of the elbow joint as a whole\" is misleading\". |","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"64981c06-0f3d-4d62-96ad-9f0282e04d1d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:32:54.231Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"33:| case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md | FIX | Scope of `supported` leaks. When NOT now limits `supported` to \"the descriptive Table 7.1 comparison ... not any training-effect claim\", but When to apply opens with an unlabelled prescription the authors do not make: \"When choosing a resistance mode for a goal: free weight for peak power, combined resistance for high force across the range.\" PDF 423-424 report measurements and say nothing about choosing a mode for a goal. \"High force across the range\" is the writer's reading of Fig 7.12. Minor: When NOT says \"(a) to (c) read from the page images\", but (a) is mainly Table 7.1 against the PDF 424 text. Figure readings, ratios, locators and table values all check (see above). | Change the opening sentence of When to apply to: \"Inference: when choosing a resistance mode for a goal, this one comparison points to free weight for peak power and combined resistance for high force across the range; the authors draw no such training rule from it.\" Optional: change \"(a) to (c) read from the page images\" to \"(a) table against text, (b) and (c) read from the page images\". |\n34:| case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md | FIX | (1) Rhea is misdescribed in a labelled Inference: \"Rhea gives partial support for bands in power training but does not separate the bands from the fast bar speed\". The design does separate them: Rhea has a fast-only arm, and fast-plus-bands against fast-only gave p = .09 (vault brief line 50, p. 5). What the study shows is that the band effect beyond fast squatting was not significant, with power .67. (2) Two sentences of the writer's own analysis are unlabelled: \"Item 1 compares a heavier total load with free weight alone, so it is not what Frost tests\" and \"Frost's acute summary (bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, vault brief, p. 21) does not contradict items 1-4.\" Neither is the authors' or Frost's statement. Everything else checks: Frost quote matches brief line 49; Rhea numbers match lines 46-53; book items 1-4, 200 / 150 / 250 kg, calibration and Fig 7.13 match PDF 425 image. | (1) Replace \"but does not separate the bands from the fast bar speed\" with \"but its fast-only arm shows no significant added effect of the bands (p = .09, statistical power .67), so it does not show that bands add to fast bar speed\". (2) Prefix \"Inference: \" to \"Item 1 compares a heavier total load ...\" and change \"Frost's acute summary (...) does not contradict items 1-4.\" to \"Inference: Frost's acute summary (...) does not contradict items 1-4.\" |\n35:| framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics.md | FIX | The new When NOT Inference widens the book's comparison. PDF 426: \"Let me now elaborate on how one can use **actively** accelerated powermetrics ... in a way which can be even more forceful than the load dropping and catching methods discussed in Ch 4.2.3. This type of training may be done in two voluntary forms\". The comparison covers the two active forms (3 and 4), and the book says \"can be\". Entry: \"the 'even more forceful ...' comparison ... puts these forms at or above the Ch 4.2.3 drop-and-catch methods in intensity, so they belong after those methods in a progression\". \"These forms\" reads as all four, including the band and partner forms. \"What it is\" places the quote correctly. Checked otherwise: Jandacka sample and 47-63% (brief lines 34, 48-49); four forms verbatim, 40-50 kg, PDF 426; Newton's Second Law, \"supramaximal\", 50-70%, PDF 427; 60-70%, PDF 422; 4.2.3 link target is chapter 4.2.3. | Replace \"puts these forms at or above the Ch 4.2.3 drop-and-catch methods in intensity, so they belong after those methods in a progression\" with \"says the two active forms (3 and 4) 'can be' more forceful than the Ch 4.2.3 drop-and-catch methods, so they belong at or after those methods in a progression\". |\n36:| claim--trunk-rotation-is-driven-by-the-obliques-not-the-erector-spinae.md | FIX | The new elbow-note correction is itself imprecise. Entry: \"the twisting it describes is at the radioulnar joints, so 'at the wrist' is the wrong place name.\" Pronation and supination happen at the proximal and distal radioulnar joints. The distal one lies at the wrist, so \"at the wrist\" is half right. The proximal one lies inside the elbow joint capsule, which is what makes the book's \"no rotation of the elbow joint as a whole\" misleading. Everything else checks: claims (1)-(4) verbatim on PDF 429; 7.4.1 flexion, lateral flexion and rotation lines on PDF 428; shoulder flexion \"posterior deltoid, upper trapezius (for scapular rotation)\" on PDF 428; \"Limitations of Anatomical Movement Analysis\" on PDF 427; `supported` decision (see section above). | Replace \"the twisting it describes is at the radioulnar joints, so \"at the wrist\" is the wrong place name\" with \"the twist happens at the proximal and distal radioulnar joints; the distal joint is at the wrist, but the proximal joint sits inside the elbow joint capsule, so \"no rotation of the elbow joint as a whole\" is misleading\". |","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"609ce91d-35f6-41cc-93b3-6b06776979ae","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
