{"parentUuid":"a5a7f62f-25d1-4002-86b2-a0d10d054fed","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a90fffde-8683-4164-b9c5-6f51905bb539","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Guf7E3Q6xJ2DjPZhvrv25n","type":"tool_result","content":"ok case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combi\nok case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elast\nok case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elast\nok case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elast\nok framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics.\nok claim--trunk-rotation-is-driven-by-the-obliques-no","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"a877cd55-75bc-45e7-b790-b1f946155c24","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:33:03.084Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"ok case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combi\nok case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elast\nok case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elast\nok case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elast\nok framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics.\nok claim--trunk-rotation-is-driven-by-the-obliques-no","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — To raise force late in the range of a squat, deadlift, bench or clean where the free-weight load underloads the stronger positions. The authors extend the method to \"the deadlift, the clean, the bench press, good mornings and one arm deadlifts,\" to bands between fixed and moving parts of pulley machines, and to chains, \"pioneered in the USA by Louie Simmons primarily for powerlifting purposes,\" where \"as the weight is lifted further off the floor, the more links of chain are raised and the more the resistance increases\" (PDF pp. 425-426 / printed pp. 414-415, checked against the page images). Calibration: stand on a bathroom scale holding the bar with stretched cable at the start and end positions, or hang loads from a band to draw \"a load vs extension graph\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). For power, the authors cap loads at \"about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411). Eric's baseline: \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)). Inference: with bands, the bar's %1RM no longer describes the load at the top or bottom, so a Prilepin prescription needs the band tension stated beside it."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Contested. Frost et al. 2010 (a narrative biomechanical review) state that bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, but \"improved transfer to sport had not been demonstrated and much acute evidence showed no difference from load-matched free weights\" (vault brief, review p. 21). Item 1 compares a heavier total load with free weight alone, so it is not what Frost tests. Frost's point is against the authors' claim that bands \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414): improved transfer was not shown, and acute studies against load-matched free weights mostly found no difference. Frost's acute summary (bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, vault brief, p. 21) does not contradict items 1-4. Rhea et al. 2009 (48 NCAA Division I men, 12 weeks) bears on the same effectiveness claim as a training outcome, not on items 1-4, which are acute force measures: jump power rose 17.8% with fast squats plus bands, 11.0% with fast squats and 4.8% with slow squats; bands beat slow training (p = .02) but not fast-only training (p = .09), and squat strength did not differ (vault brief, pp. 4-5); the brief also gives statistical power of only .67 (p. 4). Inference: Rhea gives partial support for bands in power training but does not separate the bands from the fast bar speed, so `contested` here is scoped to the effectiveness and transfer claim, not to the measured force differences. The authors themselves warn of \"large individual variations\" and that the curve \"depends on the magnitude of the weights load relative to the top and bottom resistance provided by the elastic bands\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). The study is described without sample size or statistics. A faster, band-driven descent raises eccentric force, so the authors' caution on accelerated eccentrics applies ([[framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics]]). Inference: this is not in conflict with the 4.2.2 warning that pure elastic resistance \"begins from zero\" and should not be used for starting or explosive strength ([[framework--inertial-flywheel-and-elastic-band-resistance-devices]]), because here the bands sit on top of a 200 kg bar.","+**When NOT to apply** — Contested. Frost et al. 2010 (a narrative biomechanical review) state that bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, but \"improved transfer to sport had not been demonstrated and much acute evidence showed no difference from load-matched free weights\" (vault brief, review p. 21). Inference: Item 1 compares a heavier total load with free weight alone, so it is not what Frost tests. Frost's point is against the authors' claim that bands \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414): improved transfer was not shown, and acute studies against load-matched free weights mostly found no difference. Inference: Frost's acute summary (bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, vault brief, p. 21) does not contradict items 1-4. Rhea et al. 2009 (48 NCAA Division I men, 12 weeks) bears on the same effectiveness claim as a training outcome, not on items 1-4, which are acute force measures: jump power rose 17.8% with fast squats plus bands, 11.0% with fast squats and 4.8% with slow squats; bands beat slow training (p = .02) but not fast-only training (p = .09), and squat strength did not differ (vault brief, pp. 4-5); the brief also gives statistical power of only .67 (p. 4). Inference: Rhea gives partial support for bands in power training but its fast-only arm shows no significant added effect of the bands (p = .09, statistical power .67), so it does not show that bands add to fast bar speed, so `contested` here is scoped to the effectiveness and transfer claim, not to the measured force differences. The authors themselves warn of \"large individual variations\" and that the curve \"depends on the magnitude of the weights load relative to the top and bottom resistance provided by the elastic bands\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). The study is described without sample size or statistics. A faster, band-driven descent raises eccentric force, so the authors' caution on accelerated eccentrics applies ([[framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics]]). Inference: this is not in conflict with the 4.2.2 warning that pure elastic resistance \"begins from zero\" and should not be used for starting or explosive strength ([[framework--inertial-flywheel-and-elastic-band-resistance-devices]]), because here the bands sit on top of a 200 kg bar."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: 200 kg on the bar, bands adding 150 kg on the box and 250 kg at lockout, so the lifter meets about 350 kg at the bottom and about 450 kg at the top (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414; the totals are this entry's arithmetic from the stated values, and match Fig 7.13's 175% and 225%)."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":25,"oldLines":7,"newStart":25,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism for the differences beyond the resistance types themselves. They stress variability: curve shape \"varies with every individual, according to their motor characteristics, and, in the combined scenario, with the magnitude of the inertial load and the velocity of isokinetic resistance\"; combined resistance \"may be made more auxotonic (free weight) if the added weight is large and more isokinetic if the added load is relatively small\" (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412). Inference: free weight lets the load accelerate and then forces deceleration, which yields high peak power mid-range; an isokinetic device caps velocity, so force can stay high while power stays low."," ","-**When to apply** — When choosing a resistance mode for a goal: free weight for peak power, combined resistance for high force across the range. The authors' rule for all variable resistance: \"it is important to fully understand the emphasis of the resistance training being used, because frequent use of any inappropriate training method or resistance pattern can be detrimental to the performance of the actual sporting movements,\" and for maximal power, \"train regularly with loads that do not exceed about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411, checked against the page image). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides)).","+**When to apply** — Inference: when choosing a resistance mode for a goal, this one comparison points to free weight for peak power and combined resistance for high force across the range; the authors draw no such training rule from it. The authors' rule for all variable resistance: \"it is important to fully understand the emphasis of the resistance training being used, because frequent use of any inappropriate training method or resistance pattern can be detrimental to the performance of the actual sporting movements,\" and for maximal power, \"train regularly with loads that do not exceed about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411, checked against the page image). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides))."," "," **When NOT to apply** — One single-joint movement, an unreported sample, a student project: `supported` covers only the descriptive Table 7.1 comparison for this sample (free weight gives the highest velocity and power; isokinetic and combined give the highest force, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412), not any training-effect claim. Everything read from Figs 7.10-7.12 below is approximate. Four places where the section does not line up with itself, (a) to (c) read from the page images and (d) text against data: (a) Table 7.1 gives 306 W against 129 and 131 W, about 2.3-2.4 times, while the text says \"some three times greater\" (Table 7.1, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412; text, PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413). Inference: Figs 7.10-7.12 show peak power of about 345 against about 115-125 on the plotted scale, nearer three times, so the text may describe the figures rather than the table. (b) Fig 7.10's force curve (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412) starts at about 165-170 and peaks at about 225-230 near 20-40% of displacement, then declines slowly; the text says peak force is \"near the beginning\" (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413). (c) Fig 7.12's combined force curve (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413) rises to about 350 by about 30%, dips slightly through the middle, and is highest at about 370-375 near 85-90% before a sharp drop at the end: the curve shows at most a faint shoulder at about 30% and its main peak at about 85-90%; nothing peaks near the beginning, so finding 4's \"near the beginning and the end of range\" is not what this printed curve shows. (d) The 7.3 opening says greatest force is \"usually ... possible after the joint has passed through the midpoint of the movement\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411), yet the free-weight data here put force highest early. Inference: the opening speaks of maximal joint strength across the range; Fig 7.10 shows force applied to a given accelerating load. Zatsiorsky describes ascending, descending and concave strength curves, with elbow flexion strongest at about 90 degrees ([[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]]), so \"after the midpoint\" is not a general rule. Velocity and acceleration in Figs 7.10-7.12 share one unlabelled axis with force and power, so their plotted heights cannot be read as m/s."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--trunk-rotation-is-driven-by-the-obliques-not-the-erector-spinae.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — The authors' own scope is \"all sporting activities that require rotation of the trunk\" (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418). Inference: for throwers, batters and linemen who drive and turn, load rotation through the obliques rather than relying on back extensions, and when trunk flexion work is crunches only, add oblique-dominant patterns. Inference: the PNF trunk rotation pattern of Fig 7.8, done supine or standing with cable resistance, is the book's own tool for this (PDF p. 420 / printed p. 409, checked against the page image; see [[heuristic--use-pulleys-and-dumbbells-for-spiral-diagonal-pnf-patterns]])."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — The claim that multifidus is \"the only muscle of the back\" involved in spinal rotation is stated without citation. Inference: check this sentence against an anatomy reference before teaching it as exact. Claim (4) is given without data. Before any multi-joint analysis the authors themselves tell the reader to remember the end of Chapter 3: single-joint labels miss that \"muscles which do not span other joints can still produce acceleration about those joints\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416; [[claim--multiarticular-muscle-action-cannot-be-predicted-from-joints-spanned]]). The book's own 7.4.1 summary lists spine flexion as \"Bilateral action of rectus abdominis and psoas\" (PDF p. 428 / printed p. 417), without the obliques that 7.4.2 says also flex the trunk (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418), and its rotation line names only the obliques, not multifidus; the two sections do not fully agree. Inference: 7.4.1 is a list of movers that leaves muscles out rather than saying \"only\" these muscles act, and 7.4.2 adds to it rather than reversing it, so this entry records both and rates the claim `supported`. Source note on the 7.4.1 summary (Inference, the entry writer's reading, not the book's): the summary that this entry and [[heuristic--heel-raises-with-inversion-and-dorsiflexion-both-ways]] rest on has reference-list errors. Shoulder flexion \"Upwards in sagittal plane\" is given as \"posterior deltoid, upper trapezius (for scapular rotation)\" (PDF p. 428 / printed p. 417), which reads as an error for the anterior deltoid. The elbow note says \"Apparent rotation of the elbow joint is actually rotation at the wrist - the radius and ulna twist about one another\" (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418); the twisting it describes is at the radioulnar joints, so \"at the wrist\" is the wrong place name. Check any muscle list from 7.4.1 against an anatomy reference before teaching it. Confidence `supported` covers the broad joint-action claims, not the multifidus sentence.","+**When NOT to apply** — The claim that multifidus is \"the only muscle of the back\" involved in spinal rotation is stated without citation. Inference: check this sentence against an anatomy reference before teaching it as exact. Claim (4) is given without data. Before any multi-joint analysis the authors themselves tell the reader to remember the end of Chapter 3: single-joint labels miss that \"muscles which do not span other joints can still produce acceleration about those joints\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416; [[claim--multiarticular-muscle-action-cannot-be-predicted-from-joints-spanned]]). The book's own 7.4.1 summary lists spine flexion as \"Bilateral action of rectus abdominis and psoas\" (PDF p. 428 / printed p. 417), without the obliques that 7.4.2 says also flex the trunk (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418), and its rotation line names only the obliques, not multifidus; the two sections do not fully agree. Inference: 7.4.1 is a list of movers that leaves muscles out rather than saying \"only\" these muscles act, and 7.4.2 adds to it rather than reversing it, so this entry records both and rates the claim `supported`. Source note on the 7.4.1 summary (Inference, the entry writer's reading, not the book's): the summary that this entry and [[heuristic--heel-raises-with-inversion-and-dorsiflexion-both-ways]] rest on has reference-list errors. Shoulder flexion \"Upwards in sagittal plane\" is given as \"posterior deltoid, upper trapezius (for scapular rotation)\" (PDF p. 428 / printed p. 417), which reads as an error for the anterior deltoid. The elbow note says \"Apparent rotation of the elbow joint is actually rotation at the wrist - the radius and ulna twist about one another\" (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418); the twist happens at the proximal and distal radioulnar joints; the distal joint is at the wrist, but the proximal joint sits inside the elbow joint capsule, so \"no rotation of the elbow joint as a whole\" is misleading. Check any muscle list from 7.4.1 against an anatomy reference before teaching it. Confidence `supported` covers the broad joint-action claims, not the multifidus sentence."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: sit-ups done with a lateral flex shift work from rectus abdominis to the obliques and psoas (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418)."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":24,"oldLines":7,"newStart":24,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — When an athlete needs higher eccentric and amortisation forces than the bar weight alone supplies, without heavier loads or drop equipment. Start \"with relatively light loads in the bench press or squat (say, about 40-50kg, depending on your strength and training experience) and use very few repetitions,\" with \"considerably lower\" loads for smaller muscle groups (PDF pp. 426-427 / printed pp. 415-416). Load ceiling: \"maximal power production, depending on the specific exercise, occurs with loads of between 50-70 percent of one's 1RM, so it is unnecessary ever to exceed this range of loading in these accelerated training methods (unless one uses them over a very small range of motion)\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides)). Inference: these are advanced options for that already-strong group, not for athletes still building relative strength."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — The author warns: \"do not overestimate your capabilities, because all of these forms of accelerated powermetrics can be very demanding on the body,\" and they \"can impose extremely large stresses on your soft tissues\" (PDF pp. 426-427 / printed pp. 415-416). For safety \"do not allow the accelerated load to force you into your extreme end position of joint action\" (PDF p. 426). No training study, injury data or force data are given: confidence `untested`. Inference: the \"even more forceful than the load dropping and catching methods\" comparison (PDF p. 426 / printed p. 415) puts these forms at or above the Ch 4.2.3 drop-and-catch methods in intensity, so they belong after those methods in a progression, but the book gives no force data for the comparison ([[framework--kinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]], 4.2.3). The book gives two power-load ranges six pages apart: \"about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411) and \"between 50-70 percent of one's 1RM\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416). Both differ from the Chapter 3 figures of about 25% of maximal force in isotonic laboratory models and about two-thirds of maximum in the competition squat ([[claim--maximal-power-output-load-differs-lab-vs-competition-lift]]); Jandacka and Vaverka 2008 put the acceleration-phase optimum at 47-63% 1RM by sex and exercise in physical-education students (55 men, 48 women) (vault brief, p. 11). Inference: treat 50-70% as a broad zone, not a fixed number.","+**When NOT to apply** — The author warns: \"do not overestimate your capabilities, because all of these forms of accelerated powermetrics can be very demanding on the body,\" and they \"can impose extremely large stresses on your soft tissues\" (PDF pp. 426-427 / printed pp. 415-416). For safety \"do not allow the accelerated load to force you into your extreme end position of joint action\" (PDF p. 426). No training study, injury data or force data are given: confidence `untested`. Inference: the \"even more forceful than the load dropping and catching methods\" comparison (PDF p. 426 / printed p. 415) says the two active forms (3 and 4) 'can be' more forceful than the Ch 4.2.3 drop-and-catch methods, so they belong at or after those methods in a progression, but the book gives no force data for the comparison ([[framework--kinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]], 4.2.3). The book gives two power-load ranges six pages apart: \"about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411) and \"between 50-70 percent of one's 1RM\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416). Both differ from the Chapter 3 figures of about 25% of maximal force in isotonic laboratory models and about two-thirds of maximum in the competition squat ([[claim--maximal-power-output-load-differs-lab-vs-competition-lift]]); Jandacka and Vaverka 2008 put the acceleration-phase optimum at 47-63% 1RM by sex and exercise in physical-education students (55 men, 48 women) (vault brief, p. 11). Inference: treat 50-70% as a broad zone, not a fixed number."," "," **Example** — The author's own starting point: an accelerated-eccentric bench press or squat with \"about 40-50kg\" and very few repetitions (PDF p. 426 / printed p. 415). Inference: an actively accelerated ballistic bench at that load would be pulled down fast, stopped a few centimetres above the chest, and driven up at once."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--trunk-rotation-is-driven-by-the-obliques-not-the-erector-spinae.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a5a7f62f-25d1-4002-86b2-a0d10d054fed","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
