{"parentUuid":"33fca1e7-9f21-47e8-a933-09a87d309c8d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a90fffde-8683-4164-b9c5-6f51905bb539","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013Yf63q2xXD3XECZEcbqSD1","type":"tool_result","content":"ok framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-m\nok framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluste\nok framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluste\nok heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-o\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"63290b7b-7aa5-4729-a936-2d3378939d33","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:33:33.485Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"ok framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-m\nok framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluste\nok framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluste\nok heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-o\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch7a_applied.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":142,"oldLines":3,"newStart":142,"newLines":10,"lines":[" ```"," "," No 7a file appears in the lint output. The one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes), outside scope.","+","+## Coordinator fixes after round 2","+","+Round 2 (`review_supertraining_ch7a_opus_round2.md`): GO AFTER FIXES, 7 GO / 3 FIX. All applied:","+1. Three regimes, When NOT: the PDF 412 \"minimal significance\" sentence (it opens the Miscellaneous Methods section) is replaced by the PDF 405 strength-deficit rule, quoted from the page text: weightlifting and reactive methods for a large deficit, \"submaximal bodybuilding methods should be relied upon\" for a small one.","+2. Interval and cluster: \"both of which can take the load below that minimum\"; the `contested` reason now opens by scoping the rating to the cluster-set loads.","+3. Sticking points, When to apply: the writer's guidance is now a labelled Inference; the authors' framing is \"a sticking point\" (PDF 413)."]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":29,"oldLines":7,"newStart":29,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Cluster training** is \"another form of interval training\": one or more repetitions \"with a 10-20 second interval between each repetition or cluster of repetitions in an extended set\". \"Characteristically, the minimum load used is one's 5RM and 4-6 sets are performed\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398). *Extensive clustering*: \"4-6 repetitions with one's 4-6RM, with 10 second rest intervals between each cluster\", or stripping-style with the load cut \"by 10-20%\" and a 10 s rest after each set (PDF pp. 409-410). *Intensive clustering*: \"4-6 sets of only one repetition with 75-90% of 1RM, with about a 20 second rest between repetitions for 4-6 repetitions\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, image). That last sentence is unclear as printed: it gives both \"sets of only one repetition\" and \"4-6 repetitions\" without saying how they nest."," ","-**Why `contested`.** The same paragraph sets the minimum at \"one's 5RM\" (PDF p. 409) but prescribes extensive clustering \"with one's 4-6RM\" (a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM) and a stripping form that cuts the load \"by 10-20%\" after each set (PDF pp. 409-410), both of which take the load below that minimum; intensive clustering then gives \"75-90% of 1RM\" (PDF p. 410). All of these state the load for the same method. Inference: the authors' own maximal pyramid puts 6 repetitions at 85% of 1RM, with values \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (PDF p. 409), so by the book's own figures a 5RM sits above 85%, and the lower end of the intensive range (75%) falls below the stated minimum. The book does not reconcile them.","+**Why `contested`.** The rating applies only to the cluster-set loads; the extensive and intensive interval prescriptions are not in dispute. The same paragraph sets the minimum at \"one's 5RM\" (PDF p. 409) but prescribes extensive clustering \"with one's 4-6RM\" (a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM) and a stripping form that cuts the load \"by 10-20%\" after each set (PDF pp. 409-410), both of which can take the load below that minimum; intensive clustering then gives \"75-90% of 1RM\" (PDF p. 410). All of these state the load for the same method. Inference: the authors' own maximal pyramid puts 6 repetitions at 85% of 1RM, with values \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (PDF p. 409), so by the book's own figures a 5RM sits above 85%, and the lower end of the intensive range (75%) falls below the stated minimum. The book does not reconcile them."," "," A second internal conflict touches this section: the weightlifting method rests \"1-5 minutes\" between singles (PDF p. 406), while this section says weightlifting and powerlifting rely on near-maximal singles \"with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\" (PDF p. 409). The two passages may describe different methods (sets of singles against clustered singles), and the book does not say which; see [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":36,"oldLines":7,"newStart":36,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — When a hypertrophy or work-capacity phase is planned and the coach wants named, bounded regimes. Eric's baseline (line 180): \"The preparatory period is sequenced hypertrophy/work-capacity, then basic strength, then strength-to-power, each with defined intensity/volume bands (e.g., hypertrophy phase 50-75% intensity, 3-6 sets of 5-20 reps ...)\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: the classical and extensive regimes sit inside those course bands; the intensive regime's 80-90% sits above the course's hypertrophy band, in the range the course gives for the strength phase (\"strength phase 80-90% intensity\", same line). Inference: split routines and zone training suit athletes who lift on more days than a whole-body session allows."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Not as sport strength methods. The authors say bodybuilding methods \"are of minimal significance for developing most of the specific functional types of strength fitness required in competitive sport\" (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401). Supersets are, in the authors' words, \"not intended to develop strength\" (PDF p. 408). Eric's baseline (line 104): \"single-joint assistance work is kept to a small share of total volume\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: pre-exhaustion and isolation-led splits build a session around single-joint work, which runs against that rule outside a dedicated hypertrophy block. Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**; the regimes' percentages would rest on projected maxes.","+**When NOT to apply** — Not when the strength deficit is large: the authors then concentrate \"on weightlifting and reactive methods\", and say \"submaximal bodybuilding methods should be relied upon\" when the deficit is small (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394). Supersets are, in the authors' words, \"not intended to develop strength\" (PDF p. 408). Eric's baseline (line 104): \"single-joint assistance work is kept to a small share of total volume\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: pre-exhaustion and isolation-led splits build a session around single-joint work, which runs against that rule outside a dedicated hypertrophy block. Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**; the regimes' percentages would rest on projected maxes."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: \"if supine dumbbell flyes are performed before a bench press, the pectoral and anterior deltoid muscles are pre-exhausted and the triceps are forced to become more vigorously involved\" (PDF p. 408 / printed p. 397)."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":24,"oldLines":7,"newStart":24,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors give reasons for a few items only: unfamiliar increments, because \"sticking points often relate to the numerical value of the load which one associates with one's current 1RM\" (4); minimal increments, for a \"largely psychological barrier\" (8); a short break, \"to overcome mental stagnation and facilitate restoration\" (14); rest experiments, because rests \"either too short or too long both may disrupt your ability to perform optimally\" (13); technique, because \"Sometimes your sticking point is due to imperfect technique\" (12) (PDF pp. 413-414). The list cites no study."," ","-**When to apply** — When a lift has stalled for weeks in a lifter with a training history, as a checklist to pick one or two changes at a time. Eric's baseline (line 30): \"Data collection has a cost in time, athlete compliance, and staff attention.\" **Explicit**. Inference: item 13's heart-rate or motor-control tests for rest length are a data cost of that kind, worth paying only for a lifter whose rests are a real question. Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**. Inference: items 1, 2, 4 and 8 are written around a known 1RM; with projected maxes the \"numerical value\" the athlete associates with a max may be a number they have never lifted.","+**When to apply** — When a lift has reached a sticking point, as a repertoire to choose from (PDF p. 413). Inference: change one or two things at a time, in a lifter with a training history, so the cause of any change can be seen. Eric's baseline (line 30): \"Data collection has a cost in time, athlete compliance, and staff attention.\" **Explicit**. Inference: item 13's heart-rate or motor-control tests for rest length are a data cost of that kind, worth paying only for a lifter whose rests are a real question. Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**. Inference: items 1, 2, 4 and 8 are written around a known 1RM; with projected maxes the \"numerical value\" the athlete associates with a max may be a number they have never lifted."," "," **When NOT to apply** — Not before ruling out overtraining. This list does not say so, but the book's Chapter 5 says lack of progress \"may be due to overtraining rather than long-term stagnation\", in which case a shock method is \"potentially harmful\" ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]); several items here (forced repetitions, supramaximal holds, contrast and ballistic methods) are on that shock list. Not technique changes near competition (item 12's own limit). Eric's baseline (line 142): \"Progressions in exercise selection, volume, and intensity are kept smooth and gradual by design; abrupt, large changes are treated as a direct injury-risk driver\" **Inferred (transcript)**. Inference: item 6 (bigger jumps between sets) and item 11 (loads well above 1RM) are the kind of abrupt change that rule warns against, so in a team setting they need a strength base and close supervision."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch7a_applied.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"33fca1e7-9f21-47e8-a933-09a87d309c8d","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
