b import Path\np=Path(\"heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md\");s=p.read_text()\no=\"and the Addendum says \\\"the tendency\"; n=\"and Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, his own paper translated by Yessis, PDF p. 515) says \\\"the tendency\"\nassert s.count(o)==1; p.write_text(s.replace(o,n)); print(\"ok\")\nEOF\ncd ~/SecondBrain && git pull -q --no-rebase --no-edit; git status --porcelain | head -3; cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/promote_mac.py strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky 2>&1 | tail -5","description":"Fix Addendum line in staging; pull vault; dry run"}},"wireIngestContext":{"toolu_01N2DhHj2892u3osZP62CktW":{"cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffuGncPVBb7CZTjWH5Kd","type":"assistant","uuid":"99a4a97b-8290-4b27-9fa4-eb61000f00c1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T20:03:32.271Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"99a4a97b-8290-4b27-9fa4-eb61000f00c1","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"7d4e68b7-8a02-45cd-860f-ed8fad204c0b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N2DhHj2892u3osZP62CktW","type":"tool_result","content":"ok\n== strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky: 112 new, 2 changed, 0 vault-only\n   changed: heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md\n   changed: _book.md\n\nunresolved links: 0; dry run, nothing written","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"a52e6822-8be8-4827-97de-06a4708e12d3","timestamp":"2026-10-03T20:03:33.770Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"ok\n== strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky: 112 new, 2 changed, 0 vault-only\n   changed: heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md\n   changed: _book.md\n\nunresolved links: 0; dry run, nothing written","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — After any 4-8 week block that concentrates on one quality, plan a lower-load period before the next concentrated block. Inference: judge the block's result after the 1-3 week latency, not on the last day of loading. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred). Inference: the 1-3 week latency is a reason to leave slack between blocks in that roadmap, so a delayed gain is not cut short by the next block."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — The 1-3 week figure is \"most commonly,\" with no study named. It is a latency to the onset of gains. Chapter 6 gives a different number for a different quantity: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation,\" its duration \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage,\" and its appearance \"individualised\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image). **Contested across the library:** Bompa & Haff (5th ed.), not Supertraining, write that \"Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase\" (Bompa & Haff 5e, PDF idx 60 / printed p. 49), and the Bompa entry [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]] repeats this as a 4-12 week delay. Bompa & Haff also say that \"the greater the magnitude and duration of the concentrated loading phase, the more time needed for fatigue to dissipate and performance to improve\" (same page, refs 83, 91, 103, 104). Inference: read as a 4-12 week wait before gains, the Bompa & Haff sentence matches neither Supertraining statement: 5.3.3 gives a 1-3 week latency to gains (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294), and Chapter 6 gives 4-12 weeks as how long the delayed effect lasts (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364). Read as the window in which gains show, it is close to the Chapter 6 figure. Fig 6.31 on that page (checked against the page image) shows the timing: the trained qualities (F1-F3) are depressed during the concentrated loading stage T1 and climb during T2, which begins where T1 ends (dashed line). The other 4-12 week figures in the book are also durations: Chapter 6 calls unidirectional training \"for prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" very effective (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, text layer), and the Addendum says \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks,\" with the delayed effect's duration \"equal to the duration of the stage\" (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525, text layer). Use the onset figure for when to expect a result and the duration figure for how long it may hold. Chapter 6 also qualifies the warning: during the emergence of the delayed effect \"it is relatively easy and painless for the body to change to intensive loading, but it will react negatively to extensive work\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364), so the restorative period is about volume, not a full stop.","+**When NOT to apply** — The 1-3 week figure is \"most commonly,\" with no study named. It is a latency to the onset of gains. Chapter 6 gives a different number for a different quantity: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation,\" its duration \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage,\" and its appearance \"individualised\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image). **Contested across the library:** Bompa & Haff (5th ed.), not Supertraining, write that \"Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase\" (Bompa & Haff 5e, PDF idx 60 / printed p. 49), and the Bompa entry [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]] repeats this as a 4-12 week delay. Bompa & Haff also say that \"the greater the magnitude and duration of the concentrated loading phase, the more time needed for fatigue to dissipate and performance to improve\" (same page, refs 83, 91, 103, 104). Inference: read as a 4-12 week wait before gains, the Bompa & Haff sentence matches neither Supertraining statement: 5.3.3 gives a 1-3 week latency to gains (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294), and Chapter 6 gives 4-12 weeks as how long the delayed effect lasts (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364). Read as the window in which gains show, it is close to the Chapter 6 figure. Fig 6.31 on that page (checked against the page image) shows the timing: the trained qualities (F1-F3) are depressed during the concentrated loading stage T1 and climb during T2, which begins where T1 ends (dashed line). The other 4-12 week figures in the book are also durations: Chapter 6 calls unidirectional training \"for prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" very effective (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, text layer), and Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, his own paper translated by Yessis, PDF p. 515) says \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks,\" with the delayed effect's duration \"equal to the duration of the stage\" (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525, text layer). Use the onset figure for when to expect a result and the duration figure for how long it may hold. Chapter 6 also qualifies the warning: during the emergence of the delayed effect \"it is relatively easy and painless for the body to change to intensive loading, but it will react negatively to extensive work\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364), so the restorative period is about volume, not a full stop."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: after a 4-8 week concentrated strength block, the gains \"may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Inference: an off-season strength block ending in late March would be judged in April, and the speed-emphasis block that follows would open at lower volume rather than at full load."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"99a4a97b-8290-4b27-9fa4-eb61000f00c1","session_id":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
