{"parentUuid":"2f54c924-0e86-4153-884a-139bd2855e86","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"42233eee-507d-4049-9948-8f8841a04483","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TJVYeZALBq1G81iSb6HKaK","type":"tool_result","content":"33 added, 0 already linked, 0 unresolved\n## Manual edits (not handled by the script)\nMake these in the vault (`/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/`). None of the six\nfiles is the source of a script row, so order does not matter. Each Old string was confirmed to\noccur exactly once in its file. All six files also have a staging copy under `staging/library/`\nthat is identical to the vault today; edit them too, or refresh them from the vault, before any\nlater promotion of those folders.\n1. **`strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy.md`**, lines 19-20 (What it is) and 59-60 (When NOT). The book's own pointer (PDF p. 216, \"(see 6.7.1)\") names a section that does not exist: 6.7 is \"Classification of Sports\" and has no subsections. The ATE/DTE distinction is taken up in 6.9.1. That entry classifies training effects; it gives no rest-interval rule, so the When NOT point about missing rest-interval numbers stays. Source: placeholder grep (the brief's example).\n   ```\n   Old:\n   follows once the volume of training influence is sufficient (cross-referenced forward to\n   chapter 6.7.1, not yet ingested in this pass). Whether the immediate reaction is\n   New:\n   follows once the volume of training influence is sufficient (cross-referenced forward to\n   \"6.7.1\", a number with no matching section (6.7 has no subsections); the acute and delayed effects are set out in 6.9.1,\n   [[framework--temporal-and-qualitative-forms-of-the-training-effect]]). Whether the immediate reaction is\n   ```\n   ```\n   Old:\n   given ATE is potentiating or depleting; the fuller treatment is deferred to chapter 6.7.1,\n   which is pending in this ingest.\n   New:\n   given ATE is potentiating or depleting. The book defers the fuller treatment to \"6.7.1\", which\n   does not exist; the 6.9.1 classification of training effects\n   ([[framework--temporal-and-qualitative-forms-of-the-training-effect]]) does not supply such a\n   method either.\n   ```\n2. **`strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation.md`**, line 28 (Why it works) and line 32 (When NOT). The book's pointer is \"(see 7.1)\", after its list of shock methods. The 7.1 entries define forced repetitions, supramaximal methods, restricted range repetitions and maximal eccentrics (maximal-supramaximal entry) and the contrast methods; plyometrics and electrostimulation are covered in earlier chapters, not 7.1. Source: placeholder grep.\n   ```\n   Old:\n   No study is cited here; the text refers to 7.1 (not yet ingested).\n   New:\n   No study is cited here; the text refers to 7.1, where most of these methods are defined ([[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]], [[framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set]]).\n   ```\n   ```\n   Old:\n   The list gives no dosing; the methods are described elsewhere (7.1).\n   New:\n   The list gives no dosing; the methods are described elsewhere (7.1: forced repetitions, supramaximal methods, restricted range repetitions and maximal eccentrics in [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]], contrast methods in [[framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set]]).\n   ```\n3. **`strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order.md`**, lines 32-33 (What it is). The book's pointer is \"(Ch 7.1)\" at PDF p. 217. The hybrid entry's guideline that the order of movements be arranged for safe, controlled execution fits the point made here. Source: placeholder grep.\n   ```\n   Old:\n   movements combined into one repetition, cross-referenced to chapter 7.1, not yet ingested in\n   this pass), the order of the individual movements likewise has a major effect on execution\n   New:\n   movements combined into one repetition, cross-referenced to chapter 7.1, see\n   [[framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets]]), the order of the individual movements likewise has a major effect on execution\n   ```\n4. **`strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases.md`**, lines 65-66 (When NOT). The Chapter 5 table left this line alone because Chapters 6-8 were not ingested. Chapter 6 is now in, and 6.12.2 takes up the complex-training question directly; Chapter 8 is still not ingested, so \"later chapters\" stays. Source: placeholder grep (revises the Chapter 5 table's last Dropped item).\n   ```\n   Old:\n   training methods, since the authors themselves defer that judgment to later chapters not yet\n   ingested. This claim is also a summary of an older, mixed literature base spanning several\n   New:\n   training methods, since the authors themselves defer that judgment to later chapters. Chapter 6\n   takes it up: complex training suits beginners and athletes of average qualification, while\n   highly qualified athletes are better served by single-emphasis work (6.12.2, rated contested,\n   [[claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes]]). This claim is also a summary of an older, mixed literature base spanning several\n   ```\n5. **`strength-and-conditioning/elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types.md`**, lines 72-73 (inside **Related**, after the ten-step item and before the \"Reciprocal-link suggestion\" note). Leave the trailing `</content>` line as it is (or remove it in a separate cleanup). Source: review_supertraining_ch6a_opus.md, \"Reciprocal link suggestions\".\n   ```\n   Old:\n   not say which one it uses). Reciprocal-link suggestion: `periodization--bompa/_domain.md` or\n   New:\n   not say which one it uses), [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]]\n   (Supertraining 6.2, the cycle hierarchy from session to multi-year cycle with typical durations,\n   and the swap of the meso and macro terms in older Russian texts). Reciprocal-link suggestion: `periodization--bompa/_domain.md` or\n   ```\n6. **`strength-and-conditioning/elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan.md`**, line 94 (inside **Related**, after the wide-receiver template link). Leave the trailing `</content>` line as it is. Source: review_supertraining_ch6a_opus.md, \"Duplicate check\"; review_supertraining_ch6d_opus_round2.md, eight-step row.\n   ```\n   Old:\n   [[case-study--wide-receiver-preparatory-phase-weekly-loading-template]]. Step Nine (U3 L7\n   New:\n   [[case-study--wide-receiver-preparatory-phase-weekly-loading-template]],\n   [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions]]\n   (Supertraining 6.2.2, estimate 1RM, lay out the year and work backwards from each competition),\n   [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]]\n   (Supertraining 6.15, eight steps that start from the predicted result rather than a sport and\n   position profile). Step Nine (U3 L7\n   ```\n---\n**Counts:** 33 script rows (supertraining 16, issurin 2, bompa 1, research-papers 3, in-batch 11,\nof which 8 reviewer-flagged and 3 fixer-only). Manual edits 6 files, 8 edits. Dropped list: 9\nitems (two already-linked in-batch suggestions, one new-to-old suggestion, one unflagged in-batch\npair, the Evans pairs routed to Manual edits, one alternative target not used, three pointers\nleft as they are). Dry run on the Mac copy: \"33 to add, 0 already linked, 0 unresolved\".","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"164a4328-16d0-4f96-bd42-6062864